f6b4d5f36adc-c5ba-f6ec-4ccbf92bc8c6	2026-10-24 05:02:46	2026-10-24 05:02:46	0	<p><strong>Agent Achilles needs your input</strong></p><p><b>Yes, all three gaps are still there.</b> I checked against dev at e5e7b9e:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Cross-idea and cross-project references.</b> <code>Ideaplanissue</code> and <code>Ideamessage</code> only run <code>auditBehavior</code>, <code>dateCreatedBehavior</code> and <code>softDeleteBehavior</code> (<code>ideaScreensBehavior</code> on messages). Nothing checks that <code>ideaplanId</code> belongs to the same idea. Nothing checks that <code>issueId</code> belongs to the idea's project or to one the user can read either. <code>Acl::assertWriteInScope</code> only follows the <code>ideaId</code> path.</li>\n<li><b>Plan version race.</b> <code>planVersionBehavior::getLatestVersion</code> still runs a separate <code>maximum()</code> query and returns max + 1. Two plans created at the same moment hit the unique <code>(ideaId, version)</code> index, and the second one gets a 500.</li>\n<li><b>Partial PATCH of Comment or Activity.</b> <code>Project::resolveProjectIdForAttributes</code> matches paths on the payload alone. A PATCH that sends <code>relatedId</code> without <code>relatedTo</code> matches no <code>when</code> path, returns <code>false</code>, and skips the scope check on the new parent.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Two decisions are needed before I build it:</p>\n<p><b>A. May a plan issue point at an issue in another project?</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>A1 (recommended): same project only.</b> <code>Ideaplanissue.issueId</code> must be in the idea's project, and <code>ideaplanId</code> must be a plan of the same <code>ideaId</code>. Both checks would go in one validation behavior on Ideaplanissue (Ideamessage gets the plan check only), returning 422 on a mismatch. This matches 4069, which left cross-project ideas out of scope. It is also the simplest rule that closes the <code>rels=issue</code> leak.</li>\n<li>A2: any project the user can read. Plan items carry their own project, so cross-project plans would work. The check would be <code>assertProjectIdInScope</code> on the issue's project. It is looser, and it needs a decision on what other members of the idea's project see when they lack access to that issue.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>B. How should concurrent plan versions be numbered?</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>B1 (recommended): lock the idea row while numbering.</b> Run <code>SELECT … FROM ideas WHERE id = :ideaId FOR UPDATE</code> inside the create transaction, then max + 1. This is deterministic and needs no schema change.</li>\n<li>B2: catch the unique violation and retry the save a few times. This needs no lock, but it means catching a Postgres error code in the model save path, and it can still fail under heavy contention.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Item 3 needs no decision. I will merge the PATCH payload over the stored record before matching paths in <code>assertWriteInScope</code>, and add a failing test first.</p>\n<p>Reply with A1/A2 and B1/B2, or just "go with the recommendations", and I'll implement it on <code>claude-issue-4075</code>. The branch is created, with nothing committed yet.</p>	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	conversationrooms	8b1885a16adc-c5ba-f6ec-f3e74294b2dc	Agent Achilles	Agent Achilles	\N
e21b47c26adc-34ae-534f-31193dd5c3f5	2026-10-24 05:32:18	2026-10-24 05:39:20	0	<p><strong>Cross-idea and cross-project reference.</strong>&nbsp;We cannot restrict the creation of issue cross-project, but we will have to manage how we created them and what we say in them. There are going to be cases where a complex project is being managed using multiple repos or multiple projects all within Projects4Me. Just like in our case where we have separate project for the frontend and a separate project for the backend. In such cases if a user is in the front-end project and the Agent Nestor has access to other projects (linked to each other, which we are assuming are being linked in the workspace/runtime, we will ask project layer or linking in prometheus and gaia later) then it should be able to determine if the implementation of the front end needs a new API so we cannot limit the cross-project awareness or issue creation. I understand that so far I have only mentioned the permissions of the Agent Nestor. What about the permission of the user. I don't think that we should stop the user from seeing if their idea resulted in the creation of an issue in a project that they were not a part of, but I understand that if we should them a link that they do not have access to then it can needlessly take them to a page showing permission denied, this can easily be handled by not show those issue references as links if the other does not have accesso the said project or issue.&nbsp;Also regarding creation of the issue cross-project. If the user was in the project for the front-end the Agent should of course create and issue for the front-end project as per normal, but if they need an API then it while creating an issue for the back-end project it must explicitly mention this is a request by another project and must be evaluated for implementation as a request.</p>\n<p>I imagine that this will most probably have little impact for Gaia and has more to do with Dev-tools project, so I have created issues to handle this in 'A Dev Tools project', you just handle Gaia changes assuming that Agent Nestor and Prometheus are already doing their part.</p>\n<p><strong>Concurrent Plan versions</strong><br>\nGo for transactions, they are better</p>\n<p><br data-tomark-pass=""><br>\n<strong>PATCH</strong><br>\nGo with your recommendation</p>\n	1	1	conversationrooms	8b1885a16adc-c5ba-f6ec-f3e74294b2dc	Hammad Hassan	Hammad Hassan	\N
