331c0e726ae3-8556-8b3e-0da85626617c	2026-10-29 17:41:07	2026-10-29 17:41:07	0	<p><strong>Agent Achilles needs your input</strong></p><p><strong>Agent Odysseus needs your input on the route for the agent spec</strong></p>\n<p>The issue asks for <code>GET /agent/{id}/spec</code>. In Gaia that path is already taken by the generic related route, <code>/agent/{id}/{relation}</code> (<code>relatedAction</code>). <code>RestController::authorize()</code> runs <code>Acl::authorizeRelated('Agent', 'spec', …)</code> before any controller code. Because <code>spec</code> is not a relationship, the request is rejected before it reaches the controller. To serve <code>/agent/{id}/spec</code> I would have to special-case <code>spec</code> in <code>RestController</code>, or in the authorize or related path for Agent. That is a one-off custom action, which the house rules forbid ("standard CRUD only, no custom REST actions or one-off routes"; open/closed: don't edit <code>RestController</code> for one resource).</p>\n<p><b>Options:</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>(Recommended)</b> <b>A read-only <code>agentspec</code> resource: <code>GET /api/v1/agentspec/{id}</code></b>, where <code>{id}</code> is the agent id. This uses the same pattern as <code>milestoneoverview</code>: a route entry with GET only, and an <code>AgentspecController</code> whose <code>aclMap</code> has only <code>get</code>. The controller stays thin. A domain service, <code>core/libs/agents/AgentSpec</code>, builds the document from the agent row, the existing <code>AgentToolGrants</code> and the live <code>agent_skills</code> → <code>skills</code> (with content). The resource has its own ACL entry (<code>agentspec.read</code>), so you can let a runtime such as Talos read specs without full <code>agent.*</code> rights. There is no permission seed: you grant it by hand, and I add api-tester acl cases. The JSON Schema would live in the repo (proposed: <code>docs/schemas/agent-spec.schema.json</code>), with <code>specVersion: 1</code> in the document. A PHPUnit test checks the built document against the schema.</li>\n<li>Keep <code>GET /agent/{id}/spec</code> as written, and accept a narrow exception in the related-route authorization and dispatch for this one path. It works, but it breaks the house rule and puts an Agent-specific branch in the core.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>Two smaller decisions; I'll go with these defaults unless you say otherwise:</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Who can read a spec:</b> only callers with <code>agentspec.read</code>, scoped like <code>agent.read</code>. An agent does <i>not</i> get its own spec automatically.</li>\n<li><b>What the document contains:</b>\n  <ul>\n  <li>The agent's id, name and purpose.</li>\n  <li>The config: <code>rolePrompt</code>, <code>modelConfig</code> (decoded from JSON), <code>maxTurns</code>, <code>maxRunsPerIssue</code> and <code>permissionMode</code>.</li>\n  <li>Tool grants as <code>AgentToolGrants</code> returns them today: server and tool identity plus <code>permissionRule</code>. Server endpoint, command and spec are never included, since they may hold credentials.</li>\n  <li>Skills with name, description, version and content.</li>\n  </ul>\n  Skill attachment files and tool <code>inputSchema</code> are left out for now.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Reply with 1 or 2, plus any change to the defaults, and I'll build it. The Talos consumer (adt-4047) isn't built yet, so the path can still change at no cost.</p>	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	conversationrooms	315139506ae3-8556-8b3e-0a13b6be0099	Agent Achilles	Agent Achilles	\N
