73e663f76ae4-c7eb-7da8-d4fa67a59660	2026-10-30 12:20:03	2026-10-30 12:20:03	0	<p><strong>Agent Achilles needs your input</strong></p><p>I can't start this one yet. The issue lists an <b>open decision: whose identity and ACL workflow actions run with</b>. The answer shapes the <code>workflow_runs</code> migration, which identity the actions write under, and the audit. So I need your choice before I write the engine. The branch <code>claude-issue-4051</code> is clean: nothing is committed.</p>\n\n<h4>1. Identity and ACL for actions</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>A. The workflow's owner (<code>workflows.ownerId</code>)</b>. Actions run with the owner's permissions, and <code>createdUser</code>/<code>modifiedUser</code> on the records they change is the owner. Each change also stores <code>workflowRunId</code>, which drives the "via workflow X" audit and the loop guard. It reuses the ACL we already have, and a workflow can never do more than its owner could do by hand. The downside: if the owner loses a permission, the steps that need it fail. Each such step is recorded in <code>workflow_run_steps</code> with the ACL error.</li>\n<li><b>B. A workflow system user</b>. There is one "Workflow" user, and you give it permissions by hand (no seed, by the house rule). Its behaviour doesn't change when people leave. The downside: anyone who can write a workflow gets that user's rights, which is privilege escalation, unless writing workflows is itself admin-only.</li>\n<li><b>C. The actor of the step or event</b>. Actions run as whoever caused the trigger. Schedule triggers have no actor, so they would still need A or B. Users would also see changes under their name that they never made.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>I recommend A</b>, with one guard: only the owner (or an admin) can set a workflow to Active, so nobody can borrow someone else's rights by setting <code>ownerId</code>. The run stores <code>runAsUserId</code>, so the history shows who it ran as.</p>\n\n<h4>2. Scope: split <code>notify</code> and <code>call_tool</code> out?</h4>\n<p>Two of the actions have nothing to build on yet:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>call_tool</code>: Gaia can only list MCP tools (<code>McpToolLister</code>); it can't call them. Calling one needs an MCP client in Gaia, or a call through Ares/Hermes, which is a design question of its own.</li>\n<li><code>notify</code>: we need to choose a channel: a <code>Systemnotification</code> row, email, or a Hermes push.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>I recommend</b> keeping these in this issue:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the engine;</li>\n<li><code>workflow_runs</code> and <code>workflow_run_steps</code>;</li>\n<li>Filter and Switch;</li>\n<li>expressions;</li>\n<li>the loop guard and depth limit;</li>\n<li>the actions assign, set_field, set_status, add_comment, create_issue, create_wiki and end.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>I would move <code>notify</code> and <code>call_tool</code> into two follow-up issues. I'll propose those issues before creating them. The actor nodes (run_agent, ask_user) stay out of this issue: I assume they belong to the later workflow issues.</p>\n\n<p>Please answer with 1: A/B/C and 2: split / keep all.</p>	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	conversationrooms	71e69c876ae4-c7eb-7da8-20ce1be6cc08	Agent Achilles	Agent Achilles	\N
