35608d836ae9-28cc-64ae-b500a028fb7b	2026-11-02 21:27:17	2026-11-02 21:27:17	0	<p><strong>Agent Achilles needs your input</strong></p><p><b>Agent Odysseus needs your input on the scope of adt-4047</b></p>\n<p>All the dependencies are merged: agaia-4052 (the <code>workflowactorstep</code> claim and report), agaia-4043 (<code>agentspec</code>), ah-4055 (the event), adt-4041 (client credentials) and agaia-4054 (the seeded "Agent coding loop", still Draft). Adding the "Run agent" path to Talos is clear. Removing Talos' own status → action logic in the same change is not, because more depends on that path than the seeded workflow replaces:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Outcomes:</b> Gaia accepts <code>done, needs_input, blocked, failed, limit</code>. Talos runs also end with <code>ready_to_ship</code> (a fresh ship session), <code>awaiting_bot_review</code> (the CodeRabbit loop, <code>lib/bot_review.py</code>) and <code>escalate</code> (a rerun on Opus). The seed's prompts work around this by shipping inside the same run and ending with <code>done</code>. That gives up the small ship session and the CodeRabbit rounds.</li>\n<li><b>Agamemnon:</b> its conflict keeper sets <code>next_action=merge_conflict</code> on the worker (<code>lib/orchestrator/keeper.py:136</code>, <code>lib/agamemnon/tool.py:295</code>). Its merge route and owners come from the dispatcher and <code>handoffTo</code> (<code>lib/orchestrator/setup.py:82</code>, <code>worker.py:40</code>). Removing the action path breaks the auto-merge milestone flow.</li>\n<li><b>Other behaviour in the dispatcher:</b> the "Depends on" hold, the iteration cap per issue, the hand-back when a run is interrupted, and retrying hand-offs that Gaia refused. The workflow (Filter → Switch → Run agent → outcome ports) takes over the hand-off itself. The other three have no equivalent yet.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Options</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>(Recommended) Add the workflow path now and remove the old one in a follow-up.</b> In this issue:\n  <ul>\n  <li>Talos subscribes to the user-scoped <code>workflow.agent.run.requested</code> for each agent, reads and claims the step, and loads <code>/agentspec/{agentId}</code> through gaia-mcp. The spec's role prompt, <code>maxTurns</code> and <code>permissionMode</code> override <code>agent.json</code>; its tool grants and skills map onto the agent's allowed tools and skills.</li>\n  <li>It runs Claude with the step's prompt, session (new or resume) and model, in the per-issue workspace, and reports the outcome.</li>\n  <li>Inside one step, <code>ready_to_ship</code> still opens a fresh ship session, <code>escalate</code> still reruns on the stronger model, and <code>awaiting_bot_review</code> waits for CodeRabbit before reporting <code>done</code>. Only the five Gaia outcomes leave Talos.</li>\n  <li>Each agent opts in with a setting in <code>agent.json</code>, so Odysseus keeps working while you activate and try the seeded workflow.</li>\n  </ul>\n  A follow-up issue (complex) then removes the status → action path, <code>actions/handoffStatuses/pickupStatus/acceptAssignmentsFrom</code> and the workflow fields of <code>agent.json</code>. It also moves Agamemnon's <code>merge_conflict</code> hand-back onto a workflow, once the workflow has run real issues.</li>\n<li><b>Everything in one PR, as the issue is written.</b> Remove the dispatcher's policy, the per-status actions, the hand-off and the bot-review mechanics now, and rework Agamemnon's conflict and merge route in the same PR. This is a much larger change (dispatcher, policy, bot_review, orchestrator, registry, about 2,000 lines of tests). Talos also stops working on issues until the seeded workflow is Active and matches the current flow.</li>\n<li><b>Like option 1, but drop the in-step mechanics.</b> Map <code>ready_to_ship</code>, <code>awaiting_bot_review</code> and <code>escalate</code> straight to <code>done</code> or <code>failed</code>, as the seed assumes. This is simpler, but the ship and CodeRabbit loops