a0d3bde86af1-31f2-1bb8-b2ed62684d35	2026-11-09 00:02:40	2026-11-09 00:02:40	0	<p><strong>Agent Achilles needs your input</strong></p><p><strong>Agent Odysseus: question before building #4395 (Gaia contract gap for secret rotation)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> I read Gaia <code>origin/dev</code> (D-066 enrollment, D-068 rotation, D-070 enrollment tokens). Most of the issue can be built as written: enrolling with or without <code>GAIA_ENROLLMENT_TOKEN</code>, polling, the credential file, and getting and refreshing the client_credentials token. Weekly rotation is the part that breaks:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rotation is <code>PATCH /api/v1/serviceclient/{id}</code> with <code>{id, rotateSecret: true}</code>. Here <code>{id}</code> is the <strong>Serviceclient record id</strong> (a GUID). It is not the OAuth <code>clientId</code> (<code>ServiceclientController::assertIdMatches</code>, <code>ServiceClientSecrets::rotateServiceClient($serviceClientId)</code>).</li>\n<li>The poll that delivers the secret (<code>ServiceenrollmentController::getAction</code>) returns only <code>clientId</code> and <code>clientSecret</code>. <code>ServiceEnrollments::poll</code> saves <code>serviceclientId</code> on the enrollment, but <code>document()</code> leaves it out.</li>\n<li>A service token can't look the id up either. Serviceclient's <code>acl.service</code> opens only <code>update</code>, and introspection returns <code>clientId</code>, not the record id.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So Hermes has no way to learn the id it must send to rotate.</p>\n<p><strong>Options</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>(Recommended) Small Gaia issue, linked PRs.</strong> The poll that delivers the secret also returns <code>serviceClientId</code> (the Serviceclient record id), with a D-066 amendment and an api-tester case. Hermes keeps it in the credential file next to <code>clientId</code> and <code>clientSecret</code> and uses it to rotate. The change is one attribute on a response that already carries secrets, and no new access surface. Complexity: simple. I would build Hermes against that field now (the stub Gaia returns it), and the two PRs merge together.</li>\n<li>Gaia lets a service read its own record: add <code>get</code>/<code>list</code> to Serviceclient's <code>acl.service</code> with the same <code>clientId = :serviceClientId:</code> filter, and Hermes looks itself up once. This opens more surface than option 1 and adds an extra call. Not recommended.</li>\n<li>Ship Hermes now without rotation (enrollment, stored credential, token refresh) and add rotation in a follow-up once Gaia exposes the id. This changes the issue's acceptance criteria.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Ask:</strong> may I create the Gaia issue for option 1 (A Gaia, milestone aga-Identity, simple; the delivering poll returns <code>serviceClientId</code>) and go ahead with #4395 against that field? Or do you prefer option 3?</p>\n<p><strong>Assumptions I'll follow unless you say otherwise:</strong> credential file path from <code>HERMES_CREDENTIAL_FILE</code>, written atomically with mode 600 and holding <code>{clientId, clientSecret, serviceClientId}</code>. Hermes writes the new secret to the file right after rotating, within Gaia's 10-minute grace window. When no credential is stored and enrollment fails, Hermes keeps relaying and retries in the background rather than crashing, because nothing uses the token yet.</p>\n<p><em>Note:</em> <code>ai start</code> timed out on the tracker update, so its timer never started and the time spent reading the contracts so far is not logged. The timer is running now.</p>	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	conversationrooms	90a899496af1-64a9-1bb8-4d112b28d5c2	Agent Achilles	Agent Achilles	\N
