10f23dc36af1-683e-2dd5-252996a183e8	2026-11-09 00:38:41	2026-11-09 00:38:41	0	<p><strong>Agent Achilles needs your input</strong></p><p><strong>Evaluation: accept, with adaptations.</strong> Both dependencies are merged. agaia-4391 lets a runtime token read <code>GET /agent</code>, which returns only delegable agents, so a disabled agent or one with no delegation scopes drops out of the list. It can also read <code>agentspec</code>, <code>agenttool</code>, <code>agentskill</code> and <code>skill</code>. The D-067 grant (<code>urn:projects4me:params:oauth:grant-type:agent-delegation</code>, <code>agent_id</code>) issues a <strong>one-hour</strong> token with no refresh token. Before I build it, three things the issue leaves open would change its shape:</p>\n\n<p><strong>1. Where a discovered agent's local settings come from.</strong> Today an agent also needs settings Gaia does not hold: <code>projects</code> (project → repo, which <code>workflow_steps._subject</code> uses), <code>runner</code>, <code>maxParallel*</code>, <code>testSlots</code>, <code>limitsByModel</code>, <code>shipPrompt</code>/<code>botRoundPrompt</code>, <code>env</code> and <code>docker</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A (recommended):</strong> one template agent folder, <code>talos/agents/gaia-runtime/agent.json</code>. It is <code>dispatch: workflow</code> and has no <code>name</code>, <code>gaiaEnv</code>, <code>model</code> or <code>rolePrompt</code>. Every discovered agent is built from it plus its spec (name, role prompt, models, limits, tool grants). Overrides per Gaia agent can come later if needed.</li>\n<li>B: a <code>gaiaAgents</code> block in <code>talos/config.json</code> holding the same keys.</li>\n<li>C: run only agents that also have a local folder keyed by Gaia agent id. This keeps local naming, which the issue wants to remove.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>2. How child processes authenticate.</strong> Today Claude's gaia MCP server, <code>ai</code> and the container runner get the agent's <code>GAIA_*</code> sign-in values from <code>gaiaEnv</code>. A discovered agent has none, and the runtime's own secret must not reach a child process. Runs often last longer than the one-hour token.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A (recommended):</strong> Talos writes the agent's delegated token to an owner-only file, <code>state/&lt;agent&gt;/gaia-token</code>, and renews it 5 minutes before it expires. The child gets <code>GAIA_TOKEN_FILE</code>. gaia-mcp's <code>Gaia.token()</code> reads that file when the variable is set, and reads it again after a 401. This is a small, additive gaia-mcp change. The container runner mounts the file read-only.</li>\n<li>B: pass the token in <code>GAIA_ACCESS_TOKEN</code>. This is simpler, but long runs fail after an hour.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>3. Scope of this issue.</strong> The plan below covers the issue's acceptance criteria. Point 2 adds a gaia-mcp change and container mounts on top, so it is about 12 files. Splitting the child-process token (2A) into its own issue would keep this PR to discovery, the token cache, the Tracker and listener on the delegated token, and the removal of <code>gaiaAgent</code>. Until that issue lands, discovered agents could not run Claude with Gaia tools. <strong>Recommendation:</strong> keep it in one issue, because a discovered agent that can't use Gaia in its runs isn't useful.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Planned design (with 1A and 2A):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>lib/gaia_agents.py</code> (discovery): on a timer (5 minutes by default) it lists the agents through <code>ServiceIdentity.gaia()</code> and loads each spec with <code>agent_spec.settings_from</code> plus <code>workflow_steps.granted_tools</code> and the skills. It returns which agents were added, removed or changed.</li>\n<li><code>lib/delegated_tokens.py</code>: a token cache per agent through the delegation grant, renewed 5 minutes before expiry. A refusal (401 or <code>invalid_scope</code>) stops new runs for that agent until a later discovery shows it delegable again.</li>\n<li>The Tracker gets a constructor that takes a token source instead of <code>gaiaEnv</code>, so Gaia calls and the Hermes <code>TALOS_TOKEN</code> use the delegated token.</li>\n<li><code>talos run</code> starts a dispatcher, step runner and listener for each discovered agent. When an agent is removed, its listener and step intake stop and a run in progress finishes.</li>\n<li>Remove <code>gaiaAgent</code> from <code>registry.py</code>, <code>agent_spec.follow</code>/<code>SpecSource</code>, <code>talos agents</code> and the README. Add a README section on Gaia-declared agents. Unit tests for discovery (an agent is added, removed or its spec changes), token renewal and refusal handling.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Please confirm 1A, 2A and the single-issue scope (or pick other options).</strong> I'll implement it in the next run.</p>	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	conversationrooms	13d60ca86af1-efa6-2dd5-4a0f631f3588	Agent Achilles	Agent Achilles	\N
