163339b96af7-aa50-1f38-e876027aa8cd	2026-11-13 21:41:42	2026-11-13 21:41:42	0	<p><strong>PR opened:</strong> <a href="https://github.com/projects4me/gaia/pull/269">gaia#269</a> (commit 182eeee9, based on f969732d).</p><p><b>Root cause of the 200:</b> Acl::isResourceAllowed treats a missing permission row as allow under the default permissive mode (RBAC D-011/D-014). The aclMembers role had no doc.update row. RestController already checks doc.update for PATCH, and an explicit deny row gives 403, which the new cases show. The shared ACL code is unchanged. Follow-up for the reviewer: should new modules seed explicit rows on existing roles, or should Gaia move to restrictive mode?</p><p><b>Decisions to review:</b></p><ul><li>Gaia has no project admin role. I mapped project admin to a capability: the ACL allows project.update (RBAC D-052). An alternative would be a field grant such as doc.locked.update.</li><li>I used validation errors in model behaviours, not a 403 in the controller, so the rule holds on every write path. Outside a request (no ACL loaded), only the owner may change the lock.</li><li>A change to ownerId is guarded too, because the design review found that otherwise an editor could make itself the owner and then change the lock.</li><li>Locked docs reject content create and delete as well as update. Doc metadata, such as the title, stays editable.</li><li>Delete returns 409 (RestController's response for a failed delete). The guard is a beforeDelete in doccontentBehavior, because softDeleteBehavior::notify ignores the result of its clone update, so a validation failure there would not cancel the delete (the API returned 200). Possible follow-up: make softDeleteBehavior pass on that failure for every model.</li><li>The project.update check is the role's global capability. It is not scoped to the doc's project.</li></ul><p><strong>Tests</strong></p><pre>api backend: 220 passed, 0 failed, 22 skipped\napi acl: 245 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped\napi client: 390 