d89403a36afc-e4ec-cd83-89e655482a33	2026-11-17 15:50:48	2026-11-17 15:50:48	0	<p><strong>Agent Achilles needs your input</strong></p><p><b>I haven't shipped #4645 yet. The new unique index on <code>mentions</code> fails to migrate.</b></p>\n<p>The full suite fails in the api run before any test executes. The migration stops with: <code>could not create unique index "mentions_relatedTo_relatedId_userId" — Key ("relatedTo","relatedId","userId")=(activities, 61, 16) is duplicated</code>. Everything else passes. All PHPUnit suites are green apart from the 6 known ShortCodeBehaviorTest failures.</p>\n<p>The handoff assumed the <code>mentions</code> table had never been written. That was wrong: <code>pr4m</code> holds legacy rows from 2015–2017, which I counted with a read-only query:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>activities</code>: 262 rows, 3 of them duplicates</li>\n<li><code>comments</code>: 238 rows, 3 of them duplicates</li>\n<li><code>Comments</code>: 1 row (capital C)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Production probably has rows like these too.</p>\n<p><b>Options</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Drop the unique index from this PR. (Recommended.)</b> Keep plain indexes on <code>projectId</code>, <code>userId</code> and <code>(relatedTo, relatedId)</code>, and rely on <code>MentionRecorder</code>'s read-before-write dedup. Two concurrent saves of the same record could then, in rare cases, write a duplicate row. The only effect would be a possible double notification later. A follow-up issue can add the unique constraint together with a cleanup of the legacy duplicates. This keeps the PR free of data migration and needs no contract change.</li>\n<li><b>Keep the unique index and add a data cleanup</b> that deletes the duplicates before the index is created. Gaia's migrations are declarative from metadata, so this needs a pre-migration data step. It also deletes legacy rows in every environment.</li>\n<li><b>Keep the unique index and scope it to new rows</b>, for example a partial index on <code>projectId