f1e32c796adc-e2c6-a0a9-92bdf4a416b8	f057b8796adc-e2c6-a0a9-8a64e255dbf5	Follow-up from the agaia-4069 design review. The Ideas resources check only ideaId against the project scope (through the Idea groupKeys path):\n\nIdeaplanissue.issueId can name an issue in another project, and Ideaplanissue.ideaplanId / Ideamessage.ideaplanId can name a plan of another idea. Records can then link across projects, and rels=issue could show an issue the user can't otherwise see. Fix: add a domain-layer check (behaviour or validation) that the referenced plan belongs to the same idea, and that the referenced issue is in the idea's project or one the user can read. Note that plan items carry their own project, so decide first whether a plan may create issues in other projects (cross-project ideas were out of scope for 4069).\n\nWe cannot restrict the creation of issue cross-project, but we will have to manage how we created them and what we say in them. There are going to be cases where a complex project is being managed using multiple repos or multiple projects all within Projects4Me. Just like in our case where we have separate project for the frontend and a separate project for the backend. In such cases if a user is in the front-end project and the Agent Nestor has access to other projects (linked to each other, which we are assuming are being linked in the workspace/runtime, we will ask project layer or linking in prometheus and gaia later) then it should be able to determine if the implementation of the front end needs a new API so we cannot limit the cross-project awareness or issue creation. I understand that so far I have only mentioned the permissions of the Agent Nestor. What about the permission of the user. I don't think that we should stop the user from seeing if their idea resulted in the creation of an issue in a project that they were not a part of, but I understand that if we should them a link that they do not have access to then it can needlessly take them to a page showing permission denied, this can easily be handled by not show those issue references as links if the other does not have accesso the said project or issue. Also regarding creation of the issue cross-project. If the user was in the project for the front-end the Agent should of course create and issue for the front-end project as per normal, but if they need an API then it while creating an issue for the back-end project it must explicitly mention this is a request by another project and must be evaluated for implementation as a request.\n\nI need you to create the issue for Prometheus project only. I am handling the creation of issues in the other projects.	message	\N	\N	\N	1	Hammad Hassan	2026-10-24 06:06:41	f	\N
