babd9c886adc-f42c-e36e-eeb31a398f7b	5b131d096adc-f42c-e36e-1693e76da846	**The idea:** when you click a notification, open the issue in the view you're already on if it can show it. On the task board, if the issue is in one of the lanes on screen, open the board's quick view instead of going to the issue page. If it isn't, navigate as today.\n\n**What exists**\n- Clicking a notification always runs `router.transitionTo(link)`: in `notification.js` `markAsRead`, and in `group.js` `goToIssue` for the group heading.\n- The board already has the preview: `selectIssue(issue)` in `controllers/app/project/board.js`. The board shows every open milestone plus the backlog as lanes, so "on the related milestone" just means "the issue is in a lane on the board right now", after any search filter.\n- The issue list (`selectIssue`) and the gantt (`openIssuePanel`) have their own previews too.\n- The notification context already has `projectShortcode` and `issueNumber`, so Gaia doesn't need to change.\n\n**Approach:** a small `issue-preview` service. A screen that can preview issues registers an opener when you enter its route and removes it when you leave, the same pattern as `hermes.register`. A notification click asks the service first and only navigates if no screen took the issue. The notification components don't need to know which screens exist.\n\n**Defaults I've assumed (say "yes" or correct me):**\n1. **Screens:** the board, together with the service, comes first. The issue list and gantt follow as a second small issue.\n2. **Comment and time-log notifications** open the same preview. Scrolling to the comment (`s_id`) only works on the full page, so that stays out of scope.\n3. **Fallback:** if the issue is in another project, filtered out or not on screen, navigate to the issue page as today. Ctrl/middle-click still opens the URL in a new tab.\n\n**Left out:** milestone and project notifications, and previews on any other screens.	message	["Reading dev-tools/talos/state/