46e183276ae0-6045-29af-117bb153b15b	afa54b056ada-2ec4-1fa7-d7b41cc59b57	why we are working on the Taskboard click. can you also look into this\n<span class="colour" style="color: rgb(191, 191, 191);">**Cause:** the drag starts as soon as the mouse moves a single pixel while the button is down. The board passes `@forceFallback={{true}}` ([board.hbs:15](vscode-webview://0ovjkq73g4isnfumdle5atdc66385s65g44dgq670jmu9ql8hp29/app/templates/app/project/board.hbs#L15)), so SortableJS (1.15.0 in the dev container) uses its own mouse-event dragging instead of the browser's. The options in [initialize-sortable.js](vscode-webview://0ovjkq73g4isnfumdle5atdc66385s65g44dgq670jmu9ql8hp29/app/modifiers/initialize-sortable.js) (`getSortableOptions()`) never set `fallbackTolerance`, `delay` or `delayOnTouchOnly`. Sortable's default `fallbackTolerance` is `0`. In `_onTouchMove`, the tolerance check is `if (fallbackTolerance && …)`, so with 0 it is skipped and **any** `mousemove` after `mousedown` calls `_onDragStart`.</span>\n\n<span class="colour" style="color: rgb(191, 191, 191);">Once a fallback drag has started, Sortable sets `ignoreNextClick = true`. It then swallows the next `click` in a document-level capture listener (its fix for issue #1184). So the `{{on "click" (fn @selectIssue issue)}}` on the `LinkTo` never runs, and the quick view doesn't open.</span>\n\n<span class="colour" style="color: rgb(191, 191, 191);">That explains the "sometimes": a perfectly still click works, but a 1px wobble from a mouse or trackpad turns it into a drag. The `ignore: 'a, img'` default doesn't help, because it only applies to native HTML5 drag, not fallback mode.</span>\n\n<span class="colour" style="color: rgb(191, 191, 191);">**Suggested fix:** add `fallbackTolerance` to `getSortableOptions()`, around 5px (3–8 is typical). Optionally add `delay: 150, delayOnTouchOnly: true` for touch. You could add the link to `filter` instead, but then the subject, which is most of the card, could no longer start a drag.</span>\n\n<span class="colour" style="color: rgb(191, 191, 191);">While reading I also noticed that the `disableSortable` getter returns `this.args.named` instead of `this.args.named.disableSortable`. It's harmless, because `disableSortable` isn't a Sortable option at all (the real one is `disabled`).</span>	message	\N	\N	\N	1	Hammad Hassan	2026-10-27 16:11:50	f	\N
