8ad77e836ae9-be29-8752-b0117f840e81	fad996876ae9-78e7-2d0a-c16a5271c4a2	Yes, we can add this. How exact the cursors can be depends on whether people are only *editing* at the same time or *co-editing* the same text.\n\n## Two levels of cursors\n\n**1. Paragraph-level flags (fits Stage 1, recommended now)**\n- Each person editing the page gets a coloured name flag on the paragraph or heading where their cursor is.\n- The flag moves as they move through the page.\n- The header shows a matching coloured chip for each person editing.\n- **Why paragraph and not character:** in Stage 1 everyone types in their own copy until they save. A character-exact cursor would point at text the other people can't see yet. A paragraph position stays correct.\n\n**2. Exact cursors and selections like Google Docs (Stage 2 only)**\n- These only make sense once everyone edits one shared document (the Yjs editor and collaboration server from Stage 2).\n- Yjs provides this through its "awareness" feature, so it would come with that work and not before.\n\n## What paragraph-level flags need\n- **Hermes design change (the real cost):** today only Gaia can publish to Hermes. Decision D-001 makes Hermes a relay with no domain knowledge, and D-002 deliberately stops browsers from publishing.\n  - Cursors change many times a second, so they can't go through Gaia's REST API (that would mean a database write and an audit row for every cursor move).\n  - Instead, Hermes would gain a separate, clearly limited channel for client signals. It relays a small `wiki.cursor` message (page, block index, user) only to other members of the same project, applies a rate limit, never stores it, and keeps it apart from domain events.\n  - This needs a new decision entry in Hermes, so it's a `complex` issue.\n- **Prometheus:**\n  - Read the caret's block from the Toast UI editor (we already use its hooks in `modifiers/initialize-toastui.js`), throttled to a few updates a second.\n  - Render the other people's flags over the editor.\n  - Give each person a colour from the existing palette: crimson, blue, green, amber, teal, plum. The design system allows no new colours.\n- **Risk:** Toast UI doesn't expose its ProseMirror decorations, so the flags are an overlay placed on the block's DOM element. It works, but a big paste or reflow can make a flag jump briefly.\n- **Size:** this adds 2 issues to Stage 1 (Hermes channel, then Prometheus flags). The Prometheus issue also depends on the presence issue, because the colours and names come from the edit sessions.\n\n## Screen\n`wiki-edit-cursors` is the edit page with two other people in it. Hammad is in "2. QA sign-off" and Sara has selected text in step 1. The coloured chips at the top match the flags.\n\n\n\nIn the app, each flag will sit just above the left edge of its block and have a thin coloured caret bar. The mock-up shows the flags inline because the mock-ups can't use custom positioning. The highlight on Sara's selection is a Stage 2 feature; in Stage 1 she'd have just a flag.\n\n## Your decisions (my recommendation in brackets)\n1. **Paragraph-level flags now, exact cursors with Stage 2?** *(Yes)*\n2. **Is the new Hermes signal channel acceptable?** It's the only lightweight way to send cursors. Hermes still stores nothing, but it does change its design decisions. *(Yes, as its own `complex` Hermes issue)*\n3. **My earlier three questions:** do you still agree with presence in Gaia and blocking conflicting saves with the dialog? *(Yes to both)*\n\nOnce you confirm, I'll send the full plan: the 7 Stage 1 issues, the 2 cursor issues and 1 Stage 2 Research issue.	message	["Searching the code in hermes/docs/architecture/decisions.md", "Searching the code in prometheus/app/modifiers", "Reading hermes/docs/architecture/decisions.md", "Updating the knowledge map"]	\N	0.235703	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-11-03 11:24:58	f	[{"uploadId": "8a1bdca66ae9-be29-8752-d1cbd7272148", "key": "wiki-edit-cursors", "title": "Wiki edit page with live cursor flags", "version": 1, "width": 1440, "height": 900}]
