b7b7c9b16aec-8a66-ed75-94d94a8c5615	ff0998196aec-c6f1-ed75-2f407967e79a	OPcache is PHP's built-in bytecode cache. Without it, PHP reads and compiles every file a request touches on every request. With it, PHP compiles each file once, keeps the result in shared memory and reuses it. Gaia is a large Phalcon app, so OPcache cuts CPU per request and response times noticeably.\n\nIt's switched on only in the deployed image (`gaia/docker/dev/Dockerfile`), using the settings in `docker/dev/opcache.ini`:\n\n- `opcache.enable=1` turns it on.\n- `opcache.memory_consumption=128` gives it 128 MB of shared memory for compiled code. This is separate from `memory_limit`, which caps each request.\n- `opcache.max_accelerated_files=20000` is enough slots for Gaia's files plus `vendor/`.\n- `opcache.validate_timestamps=0` stops PHP from checking whether files changed. That's safe because the code is baked into the image and a deploy means a new container. It's also why this image isn't suitable for development: edits wouldn't show up until a restart.\n\nThe deployed Dockerfile also removes the Xdebug and xhprof ini files that the `env-dev` base image loads. Those are debugging and profiling tools that slow every request, so production doesn't need them.\n\nLocally (`gaia/Dockerfile` with compose) OPcache stays off and Xdebug stays on, so code you edit through the bind mount takes effect on the next request and you can step through it in a debugger.\n\nThe repo doesn't document why it was set up this way: there's no OPcache entry in `docs/architecture/*/decisions.md`. The reasons above come from the comments in the two config files.\n\nThis doesn't change the plan, which is still the one issue above.	message	["Reading gaia/docker/dev/opcache.ini", "Searching the code in gaia"]	\N	0.046418	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-11-05 07:26:47	f	\N
