f5805e816aee-698e-6251-090bb2227f1c	599b10726aeb-f308-85f1-2dd28eca8a3d	Neither issue was a judgement call by Agamemnon: both are Talos bugs, and Talos's log shows where each one broke. Plan v3 below fixes them, then adds what you asked for: a few CI retries, and Agamemnon filing a bug and resuming the issue after it.\n\n## 1. Why 4303 was never released\n- **06:56:** Achilles held 4303 because it was waiting on 4302, and posted its "waiting on #4302" comment. That comment made Achilles itself the issue's last modifier.\n- **11:53:** the Hermes connection dropped and Talos reconnected. On reconnect, Talos re-checks every assigned issue, and only acts on issues last changed by someone allowed to hand it work (`policy.decide`, `talos/lib/policy.py:80`). Achilles isn't on that list. The log says: `#4303 skipped: last changed by a user who can't hand this agent work (left the held set)`.\n- **12:26:** 4302 was merged and set done. 4303 was no longer on the waiting list, so nothing released it.\n- **Agamemnon** only assigns new issues that have no assignee yet. An issue already assigned to Achilles looked to it like the worker's job, so it reported "nothing ready" even with free slots.\n\nSo any issue that is waiting on another one is lost at the next reconnect or Talos restart. The waiting list also only lives in memory.\n\n## 2. Why CI on 4301 was never re-run\n- Agamemnon did try. When the merge check fails only on CI, it re-runs the failed jobs once per commit (`orchestrator/merging.py` `_ci_failed`).\n- But `gh run rerun` exited 1 on every sweep from 07:18 onwards, until you re-ran the job by hand. The error is raised before the re-run is recorded. So nothing was counted, nothing was commented, and the issue was never handed back: it just looped every 1.5 minutes.\n- **The likely cause is a missing permission, and only you can fix it.** The Agamemnon GitHub token in `agamemnon/README.md` has no Actions permission, and `gh run rerun` needs Actions: Read and write. Please add it to the token now. The log doesn't keep gh's error text, so this isn't confirmed; the fix issue adds that.\n- The first stop on 4301 was different: "postgres is not running". That was classified as `other`. adt-4279 (Docker incidents) doesn't cover containers of the stack that are down, like Postgres. I can plan a small "stack down → `p4m up <service>`" issue if you want it.\n\n## 3. What Agamemnon gets\n- **Re-runs:** up to 3 per commit, with a wait between them, instead of 1. A failing re-run command is counted and reported, and ends in a handback rather than a silent loop.\n- **Stranded issues:** each sweep also looks for issues assigned to the worker, ready to start, with nothing queued, held or running, and gives them back to the worker with a comment. This is a safety net for any lost event in future.\n- **Telling CI failures apart:** in triage, Agamemnon reads the failing job's log and checks whether the same job also fails on the base branch. From that it decides:\n  - **Temporary failure** (timeouts, network, registry errors): re-run it.\n  - **Caused by the PR:** send the issue to Odysseus with the failing test and the log.\n  - **Already broken on the base branch** (like the image preview case): file a Bug, hold the issue until the bug is done, then merge the base branch into the PR branch and let CI run again. It merges, never rebases, per the house rule.\n- **Filing issues is a new power.** It is visible and capped: at most one bug per failure signature, a daily limit, and the bug goes in the blocked issue's project and milestone, assigned to the worker.\n- **Small conflicts** are already in Agamemnon's premise (`resolve-conflict` for additive hunks), so there's nothing new there.\n\nAll issues go in **aga-agentic system**. The hold-until-done issue builds on adt-4278 (condition holds). Every issue that touches `talos/lib/orchestrator` or `talos/lib/agamemnon` fails the merge gate's `self` check, so you'll need to merge those by hand.	message