1bb00aa36af0-3f84-f538-d7302a022588	c31674596af0-6d25-f538-e6760785c02e	A lot of this already works. Every link is authenticated today, but each one uses a different mechanism, and there are some real gaps.\n\n## What exists now\n\n| Link | How it authenticates | Where |\n|---|---|---|\n| **People → Gaia** | OAuth password grant | `gaia/.../TokenController.php` |\n| **Talos → Gaia** | Talos signs in as an agent with OAuth `client_credentials` (agaia-4028, adt-4041, both done). The client ID and secret sit in a local `gaia-mcp.env` | gaia-mcp |\n| **Talos → Hermes** | Socket connection using that same agent token | `talos/lib/hermes.py:43` |\n| **Gaia → Hermes** (publishing events) | One fixed shared secret in the `X-Hermes-Secret` header | `HermesPublisher.php:137`, `hermes/src/http/middleware/require-secret.js` |\n| **Hermes → Gaia** | Hermes has no identity of its own. For each socket it calls `/user/me` and the membership list with the *user's* token, and caches the result for 5 minutes | `hermes/src/auth/gaia-session.js` |\n| **Prometheus → Hermes** | The user's access token in the socket handshake's `auth.token`, plus a CORS allowlist | `prometheus/app/services/hermes.js:202` |\n| **Ares → Gaia** | Token check (agaia-4029) behind a shared-secret header. The agent noted at the time that "Gaia has no service-client concept yet" | |\n\n## Gaps\n\n1. **No identity for services.** Hermes and Ares only have fixed shared secrets. If one leaks, anyone can publish events or check tokens. There's no rotation or expiry, and you can't tell which service made a call.\n2. **The shared-secret check is weak.**\n   - Hermes compares the secret with a plain `!==`, which isn't timing-safe.\n   - Outside production, both sides fall back to `hermes-dev-secret`.\n3. **A Hermes socket is only checked when it connects.** After that:\n   - It stays authorised after the token expires or the user is disabled.\n   - It still gets a project's events after the user is removed from the project, until it reconnects or the 5-minute cache runs out.\n4. **Talos keeps its secrets in local env files.** That covers the Gaia client secret, GitHub tokens and similar.\n\n## Questions\n\n1. **What does "secure information" mean for Talos?**\n   - (a) Authenticated access to normal data under its own permissions. That already exists.\n   - (b) Secrets that Gaia stores and hands out, such as GitHub tokens, model API keys and agent credentials, so they stop living in local env files.\n\n   *My guess is (b).* If so, that's a secrets store in Gaia: encrypted at rest, readable only by named service clients, and every read logged. It's the biggest piece and needs its own design.\n2. **Should services get real identities?** I recommend one Gaia concept, **service clients**: Hermes, Ares and Talos each use `client_credentials` with scopes such as `events.publish`, `token.introspect` and `secrets.read`. This would replace the shared secrets. Hermes could then check a user's token through the same introspection endpoint instead of calling `/user/me` plus the membership list. For Gaia → Hermes, Hermes would check a token Gaia issues to itself (short-lived and signed) instead of the fixed secret.\n3. **For Prometheus ↔ Hermes, is closing gap 3 enough?** Recommended answer: yes:\n   - Hermes disconnects a socket when its token expires.\n   - Gaia publishes an event when a user's sessions are revoked or their memberships change, and Hermes clears that user's cached access.\n   - The secret check becomes timing-safe, and the dev fallback is refused outside local dev.\n\n   This doesn't need a new protocol, just tightening. TLS (`wss`) is a deployment setting, not code.\n\n## Proposed order\n1. Quick hardening (gap 2, Hermes only).\n2. Gaia service clients.\n3. Hermes and Ares move onto service clients.\n4. Live revocation (gap 3).\n5. Secrets store plus Talos reading from it, only if your answer to question 1 is (b).\n\nEach Gaia ↔ Hermes contract change will be a pair of issues linked to each other, with a Hermes ADR.\n\nAnswer the three questions (or just "yes" to my recommendations) and I'll write the plan.	message	["Reading dev-tools/talos/state/nestor/map.md", "Reading dev-tools/talos/state/nestor/map.md", "Running cd /d/Claude/Workspace/Projects/Pr4M; dev-tools/ai/ai kb search \\"service token\\" 2>&1 | head -30; dev-tools/ai/ai kb sear", "Searching issues: \\"service token authentication\\"", "Searching issues: \\"hermes auth jwt\\"", "Searching the code in hermes/src", "Reading hermes/src/auth/gaia-session.js", "Reading hermes/src/http/middleware/require-secret.js", "Reading hermes/src/socket/middleware/authenticate.js", "Reading issue 4041", "Reading issue 4029", "Searching the code in gaia/app/api/v1/controllers/TokenController.php", "Searching the code in dev-tools/talos/lib/hermes.py", "Searching the code in hermes/src/config.js", "Searching the code in prometheus/app/services/hermes.js", "Searching the code in hermes/src/auth/session-cache.js", "Searching the code in hermes/src/auth/tokens.js", "Searching the code in gaia/app/api/v1/controllers", "Searching the code in hermes/src", "Searching the code in hermes/docs/architecture/decisions.md", "Updating the knowledge map"]	\N	0.804136	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-11-08 18:48:59	f	\N
122e36826af0-e0ab-e38a-7d031e09c052	c31674596af0-6d25-f538-e6760785c02e	The biggest gap is like you mentioned there is no identity of the services. If the service is asking for information on an actor, human or agent's behalf, like you said we need to be able to rely on one token to handle that. Is JWT a good options or will that be us complicating things.\nAnswers\n1) Talos is running agents locally, we need to be able to run agents that have been declared in Gaia. For this we need to transfer the Agent's credentials to Talos. As you can imagine this can be a big issue unless it is handled with care.\n2) Service should get real identities, however need to make sure that if we are also using client credentials for agents, we do not confuse one with the other.\n3) If we were to follow your recommendation what would look and what are the other options.\n\nLets explore the details more before we decide on a plan	message	[]	\N	\N	1	Hammad Hassan	2026-11-08 19:05:54	f	[]
159387796af0-e0ab-e38a-f24e67f903f4	c31674596af0-6d25-f538-e6760785c02e	Agent Nestor is working on it.	working	\N	\N	\N	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-11-08 19:05:57	f	\N
620517f16af0-e0ab-e38a-308bdac770d9	c31674596af0-6d25-f538-e6760785c02e	**Short answer on JWT:** not now. Keep Gaia's current opaque tokens and let services check them by asking Gaia (token introspection). Shape that answer with JWT claim names, so moving to JWT later costs little. My reasoning, and the design that follows from your three answers, are below.\n\n## JWT or opaque tokens\n\nGaia is the only place that decides access. Any service that reads or writes data calls Gaia with the token anyway, and Gaia checks it with one database lookup. JWT only helps a service that wants to check a token *without* calling Gaia. Today that's Hermes at socket connect and Ares.\n\n| | Opaque token + introspection (recommended) | JWT |\n|---|---|---|\n| Revocation | Immediate: disabling a user or agent kills the token on the next check | Lives until it expires, so it needs short lifetimes plus a revocation list or events |\n| Membership and permissions | Always current | Frozen in the claims until expiry |\n| New moving parts | One introspection endpoint (agaia-4029 already built an RFC 7662-style one) | Signing keys, key rotation, a public-key (JWKS) endpoint, claim design, and verification in PHP, Node and Python |\n| Cost | One Gaia call per connect or per Ares request, which a cache absorbs | No call |\n\nOur library (bshaffer) can issue JWT access tokens, but that doesn't remove the revocation and staleness problems. Its introspection replies would use `sub`, `act`, `scope`, `aud` and `exp`. If load ever justifies JWT, the claims are already designed.\n\n## "One token" when a service acts for an actor\n\nThere are three ways a service can act for a person or agent:\n\n- **A. Pass-through.** The service forwards the actor's own token. Hermes does this today. Simple, but the service gets everything the actor can do, and Gaia can't see the service at all.\n- **B. Delegated token (recommended).** The service authenticates as itself and asks Gaia for a short-lived token for the actor.\n  - The token says `sub = actor`, `act = service`, has narrow scopes and lasts about an hour, with no refresh token.\n  - Gaia's permissions still apply to the actor. The audit log shows both the actor and the service.\n  - This follows the token-exchange standard (RFC 8693). Our library doesn't have it, but we already add a custom grant the same way for agents (`AgentClientCredentials`).\n- **C. "On behalf of" header.** The service sends its own token plus a user ID. Rejected: any service bug becomes a way to impersonate anyone.\n\n## Q1: Talos running agents declared in Gaia\n\nWith B, **agent secrets never leave Gaia**:\n\n1. Each Talos installation is registered in Gaia as a runtime, a service client with its own secret. That one secret is the only thing on the machine.\n2. The agent's sponsor allows the agent to run on that runtime.\n3. For each run, Talos asks Gaia for a token for agent X. Gaia checks that the runtime is active and that agent X is allowed on it. It returns a one-hour token with `sub = agent X` and `act = runtime`.\n4. Revoking the runtime stops every agent on it at once. A leaked delegated token is dead within the hour.\n\nThe agent credentials from agaia-4028 (client ID + secret) stay for agents that run outside a runtime, such as gaia-mcp on someone's laptop. That keeps adt-4041 valid.\n\n## Q2: Services vs agents\n\nGaia would mark every OAuth client with one of three kinds:\n\n- **app:** Prometheus. It uses the password grant and gets a user's token.\n- **agent:** It uses `client_credentials` and gets the agent's user token, as today. An agent is an actor and Gaia's permissions apply to it.\n- **service:** Hermes, Ares, Gaia's own publisher, Talos runtimes. It uses `client_credentials`, but the token has **no user**, only scopes such as `token.introspect`, `agent.delegate`, `events.publish`.\n\nRules that keep them apart:\n\n- The `client_credentials` handler branches on the client's kind. An agent client never gets a service token, and a service client never gets a user token.\n- Any CRUD resource rejects a token without a user (401). Services can only reach the few endpoints their scopes allow.\n- Introspection returns an actor type, and Hermes refuses service tokens on sockets.\n- Service clients are not users. They don't appear in user lists, assignee pickers or ACL roles.\n\n## Q3: Prometheus ↔ Hermes options\n\n| | How | Fixes | Cost |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **A (recommended)** | Hermes checks the user's token through Gaia introspection, using its own service token, instead of `/user/me` plus membership. It disconnects the socket when the token expires. Prometheus already reconnects with the new token after a refresh. Gaia publishes a `user.access.changed` event (user disabled, sessions revoked, membership added or removed), and Hermes clears that user's cached access and re-applies their subscriptions. | Expiry, revocation and membership lag; Hermes gets an identity | One Gaia endpoint change, one event, Hermes changes, plus a Hermes decision record |\n| **B. One-time socket ticket** | Prometheus asks Gaia for a 60-second, single-use ticket (like our download tokens) and connects with that, so Hermes never sees the access token. | Hermes never holds user tokens | A new Gaia resource. Doesn't solve revocation, so it still needs A's event |\n| **C. JWT** | Hermes checks tokens locally with Gaia's public key. Memberships go in the claims. | No Gaia call | Memberships stale until expiry, the key handling above, and it still needs A's event |\n\nB could be added on top of A later if keeping user tokens out of Hermes ever matters.\n\n**Gaia → Hermes publishing:** Gaia would get a service client too. Hermes accepts a publish only with a valid service token that has the `events.publish` scope, checked by introspection and cached. That replaces the fixed shared secret. The simpler option is to keep a secret but sign each request body with a timestamp and support two keys for rotation. I lean towards service tokens, so there is one model everywhere.\n\n## Still open before a plan\n1. **Runtime ↔ agent binding:** who may allow an agent on a runtime? I recommend the sponsor, since they already own the agent's access. And can one agent be allowed on several runtimes? I recommend yes, as a list.\n2. **Delegated token lifetime:** one hour, no refresh, and Talos asks again for each run? Workflow steps can last up to 4 hours (`FINISH_MINUTES`), so Talos would re-request during a long run.\n3. **Scope of the first round:** service clients, introspection with actor type, Talos runtime delegation, then Hermes option A. Ares moving off its shared secret and the Gaia → Hermes publish change would come right after. Does that order work for you?	message	["Searching the code in gaia", "Searching the code in gaia", "Reading gaia/core/libs/authorization/AgentClientCredentials.php", "Searching the code in gaia/core/libs/authorization/OAuthServer.php", "Updating the knowledge map"]	\N	0.265804	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-11-08 19:07:14	f	\N
8fbf10b46af0-1149-51c5-c5526b853141	c31674596af0-6d25-f538-e6760785c02e	While we are discussing things, lets also discuss ACL, how will ACL impact here. If we only want Talos to be able to pull in limited information without a user's identity, or with a user's identity. How will that work.	message	[]	\N	\N	1	Hammad Hassan	2026-11-08 19:16:31	f	[]
