d3c879696af0-1149-51c5-242c4d67d600	c31674596af0-6d25-f538-e6760785c02e	Gaia's ACL stays the single source of truth for what an **actor** may do. The token adds a second, separate limit on what the **calling client** may do. Access is allowed only when both say yes:\n\n> **effective access = the actor's ACL ∩ the token's scopes**\n\nScopes can only narrow access, never widen it. A service can never do more than the actor it acts for, and an actor reached through a service can be held to less than that actor could do directly.\n\n## What the ACL is today (`gaia/docs/architecture/rbac/decisions.md`)\n- **Roles are global** (`user_roles`). Each permission has a read/create/update/delete flag (D-013), and there are field-level rules (D-030 to D-037).\n- **The only per-record limit is the project:** with `project.get = members`, a user sees only rows in projects they belong to (D-060).\n- **The default resolution mode is permissive** (D-011, D-014). A missing permission row means *allow*, and a user with no roles is unrestricted. The decisions doc lists this as an open question.\n- The "system user" is found by the role name `Admin`. Separating it out has been deferred.\n\nThat third point matters most here. Today, an agent created without a role, or a role missing a newer permission, gets full access.\n\n## Case 1: Talos with no user identity (service token)\n\nThis is what a runtime needs before it acts as anyone: the agents it may run, their specs, and its own run requests.\n\n- **Option A (recommended): service tokens never go through RBAC.**\n  - A service client carries a short allowlist of scopes, such as `runtime.get`, `agentspec.get` and `token.introspect`.\n  - Anything not on the list is denied, whatever the resolution mode.\n  - The records are also limited to the runtime's own: it sees only the agents bound to it and only its own run requests.\n  - The endpoints stay standard Gaia resources, so the CRUD-only rule holds.\n- **Option B: give service clients ACL roles like users.**\n  - Not recommended. It is exactly the services/agents confusion you want to avoid.\n  - Under permissive mode, every permission added to the catalog later would quietly be granted to every service.\n\n## Case 2: Talos with an agent's identity (delegated token)\n\nThe token carries the agent as the actor and the runtime as the client (`sub = agent`, `act = runtime`). Three checks stack:\n\n1. **The agent's own ACL:** its roles, project memberships and field rules, exactly as if the agent called Gaia directly.\n2. **The binding's scopes:** when the sponsor allows the agent on a runtime, they choose a ceiling, for example `issue.*`, `comment.*` and `timelog.create`, with no `agent.*` and no `agentcredential.*`.\n3. **The token's own scopes:** Talos asks for a subset of the binding's scopes per run (later per workflow step, if we want).\n\n**Scope names = the existing permission names** (`issue.get`, `comment.create`, `agentspec.get`), plus `*` wildcards. That way there is one vocabulary, admins already know it, and the check sits next to the existing permission check in `Acl`.\n\n**Hard rules for delegated tokens:**\n- They can never manage credentials, runtimes, bindings or roles, even if the agent's role allows it. This stops a runtime from extending its own reach.\n- They are evaluated **restrictively**: a missing permission row means deny for agents. This closes the permissive gap without changing anything for people.\n\n## Example\n\n| Request | Token | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| Talos lists its agents | runtime service token, `runtime.get` | Only agents bound to this runtime |\n| Talos reads an issue | runtime service token | **403**: no user, and `issue.get` isn't on the service allowlist |\n| Agent Hera (via Talos) reads issues in project X | delegated token | Allowed if Hera is a member of X, has `issue.get`, and the binding includes `issue.*` |\n| Same token reads project Y | delegated token | Not visible: Hera isn't a member of Y |\n| Same token creates a credential | delegated token | **403**: hard-excluded for delegated tokens |\n| The sponsor (a person) reads the same issues | user token | Unchanged: their roles only, no scopes on people's tokens |\n\n## What it touches\n- **Gaia framework (complex):**\n  - the scope check alongside `checkAccess`\n  - client kinds (app, agent, service)\n  - restrictive evaluation for agent actors\n  - the delegation grant\n  - a record limit to "own runtime" for service tokens\n  - decision-doc entries for each\n- **Gaia resources:** runtimes, agent ↔ runtime bindings with scope ceilings, and an audit trail of issued tokens.\n- **Hermes:** run requests today go to the agent's user room (D-017). With runtimes, they could go to a runtime room that only that runtime's service token may join. That's a new Hermes ADR.\n\n## Decisions I need\n1. **Service-only access:** Option A, a deny-by-default scope allowlist outside RBAC? (recommended: yes)\n2. **The permissive gap for agents:** evaluate agent actors restrictively (recommended), or only require every agent to have at least one role? The first one closes the gap; the second just makes it less likely.\n3. **Scope granularity on day one:** a ceiling per runtime binding, with Talos asking for the full ceiling per run (recommended)? Per-workflow-step scopes would come later.\n4. **Audit:** is a log of issued delegated tokens enough for now, or should each record also store the runtime as well as the agent in `modifiedUser`? I recommend the log first; storing it on every record touches every model.	message	["Searching the code in gaia/docs/architecture/rbac/decisions.md", "Reading gaia/docs/architecture/rbac/decisions.md", "Searching the code in gaia/core/libs/security/Acl.php", "Reading gaia/core/libs/security/Acl.php", "Reading gaia/core/libs/security/Acl.php", "Reading gaia/docs/architecture/rbac/decisions.md", "Updating the knowledge map"]	\N	0.277706	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-11-08 19:17:39	f	\N
aa14b9e76af0-44cc-e4ce-790b2cb12322	c31674596af0-6d25-f538-e6760785c02e	First verify the permissive flag. the flag is nothing more that a flag to save Allowed against a new role being created. No permission record still means no permission.	message	[]	\N	\N	1	Hammad Hassan	2026-11-08 19:42:34	f	[]
ad6aefb86af0-44cc-e4ce-de4445397650	c31674596af0-6d25-f538-e6760785c02e	Agent Nestor is working on it.	working	\N	\N	\N	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-11-08 19:42:37	f	\N
da7cc4f96af0-44cc-ba3c-4afbfd93615c	c31674596af0-6d25-f538-e6760785c02e	I checked, and it's more than a seeding flag. In the Gaia code I can read, a missing permission row means **allow** when the mode is permissive, and permissive is the default.\n\n**What the code does:**\n- **When a role is created:** the mode sets the stored values. Permissive writes Allowed for everything (`RolePermissionSeeder.php:56`, `$allowAll = $resolutionMode === Acl::RESOLUTION_PERMISSIVE`). That part matches what you said.\n- **When a request is checked**, the mode is used again:\n  - `Acl.php:662-666`, `isResourceAllowed`:\n    ```php\n    if (!Permission::hasResource($resourceName)) {\n        return self::$resolutionMode === self::RESOLUTION_PERMISSIVE;\n    }\n    ```\n  - `Permission::hasResource` (`app/models/Permission.php:134`) only checks whether the user's roles have *any* row for that resource. No row means it falls through to the mode, so permissive allows.\n  - `getEffectiveScope` (`Acl.php:730-733`) does the same for `project.get`: no row under permissive gives `SCOPE_ALL`, not members.\n- **The default is permissive:** `Acl.php:74` and `:124`, and nothing in Gaia's config sets `resolutionMode`.\n- **A test locks it in:** `tests/libraries/security/AclLockoutGuardTest.php:228` says "A role with no permission rows at all is capable: missing rows allow".\n- **The decision record says the same:** RBAC D-011 and D-014, "Missing rows allow."\n\n**Where this would actually let something through:**\n1. A user or agent with no roles, which D-053 allows.\n2. A permission added to the catalog after a role was created, since existing roles are not backfilled.\n3. `project.get` missing for a role: the user sees all projects, not just their own.\n\n**One caveat:** my local `gaia` checkout may be behind `dev`, and I can't see the git history. If this was changed recently on `dev`, your description may already be right. The quickest check on your side is `php tools/gaia-dev/bin/gaia-dev acl issue` as a user with no roles, or `grep -n "hasResource" core/libs/security/Acl.php` on `dev`.\n\n**What it means for the plan:**\n- **If `dev` matches what I see:** "No row = no permission" is a small, separate Gaia change: always deny when no row exists, whatever the mode. It needs the D-011/D-014 entries updated, the lockout guard and its test adjusted, and a check that existing roles have rows for everything they rely on. Once that's in, my earlier "evaluate agents restrictively" point goes away, because everyone, people and agents alike, gets deny-by-default.\n- **If `dev` already does this:** nothing extra is needed. Effective access stays the actor's permissions limited by the token's scopes.\n\nWhich one is it?	message	["Searching the code in gaia/core/libs/security", "Searching the code in gaia/core/libs/security/Acl.php", "Listing .runs/*/gaia/core/libs/security/Acl.php in dev-tools", "Listing */gaia/core/libs/security/Acl.php in .runs", "Searching for \\"resolutionMode\\" in gaia", "Searching the code in gaia/app/models", "Searching the code in gaia/tests", "Updating the knowledge map"]	\N	0.297187	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-11-08 19:43:22	f	\N
6ac05a736af0-871c-7b74-d0cc6f28a9f2	c31674596af0-6d25-f538-e6760785c02e	All 21 issues now go in the milestone **aga-Identity**. A Gaia doesn't have that milestone yet, so it will be created when you approve; the plan card marks it as new. I couldn't check A Hermes, A Dev Tool or A Prometheus. Any of them missing it gets it created the same way.\n\nNothing else changed. Gaia-declared agents get their work only from Gaia workflows, and Hermes keeps its enrolled secret in a file, as discussed.	message	["Gaia: list milestones agaia", "Gaia: list milestones ah", "Gaia: list milestones adt", "Gaia: list milestones aprom", "Gaia: project options agaia", "Updating the knowledge map"]	69ad28596af0-871c-7b74-a31ef7f86660	0.406488	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-11-08 20:28:26	f	\N
96a9ee766af5-4088-65e3-168672cef0b9	361eb9326af4-3b69-e9a2-8e04f96d31a4	The design's AI list covers **writing** help: draft, summarize, improve wording. AI can also help with the hard parts of **working together**: catching up, staying out of each other's way, reaching decisions, and keeping the doc in step with the project. Here are the ideas, grouped by the problem each one solves, with how it would run here.\n\n## 1. Catching up\n- **"What changed since you were last here."** When you open a doc, a short box sums up changes since your last visit: "Hamza rewrote *Persistence*; 3 suggestions waiting for you; 1 thread mentions you." It runs through Ares, comparing the version you last saw with the current one. Only the summary is new; the version history and per-user "last seen" are cheap.\n- **Thread summaries.** A long comment thread collapses to "Agreed: …; still open: …" before someone resolves it. The summary can be kept as the closing note, so decisions survive the resolve. Runs through Ares.\n- **Version labels.** Automatic versions get a one-line description ("Added sharing section, removed export") instead of only a timestamp. That makes History usable. Runs through Ares when a version is saved.\n\n## 2. Co-editing without stepping on each other\n- **Overlap hints.** If two people rewrite the same section at once, show "Hamza is also rewriting this section". The CRDT merges keystrokes, but it can't merge two rewrites of the same section's meaning. This mostly needs no AI: presence already knows who is in which block. AI helps only to say whether the two edits actually **contradict** each other.\n- **Suggestions that conflict.** When two pending suggestions change the same text in different ways, flag them as conflicting and offer a merged version as a third suggestion. Runs through Ares when a suggestion overlaps another one.\n\n## 3. Review and decisions\n- **Review readiness.** Before someone asks for review, a check lists unresolved threads, TODOs, empty sections and statements nobody agreed on. This is the design's "find open questions", made into a gate. Runs through Ares, on demand.\n- **Decision extraction.** Pull "We decided X" sentences into a Decisions list on the doc, each linked to its paragraph and thread. That fits how this team already keeps decision logs. Runs through Ares.\n- **Smarter mentions.** When a comment asks a question, suggest who to @mention: the people who wrote that section, or the assignee of the linked issue. History and Gaia data already have that; AI ranks it.\n\n## 4. Keeping the doc and the project in sync\nThis is where Projects4Me can beat Google Docs, because the doc lives next to the issues.\n- **Drift check.** The doc says one thing and the linked issues or merged code say another, e.g. "this section says Docstate, but agaia-XXXX shipped Doccontent". This would be Talos running the agent on a schedule or on issue changes. It leaves a **comment** on the paragraph, never an edit.\n- **Text to issues, both ways.** Selected text becomes issues (already in the design). The reverse: when a linked issue closes, the agent suggests ticking the matching checklist item or updating the status wording.\n- **Meeting notes to actions.** Paste notes, get proposed issues with assignees and a milestone, then confirm. This is the existing "create issues" action with better inputs.\n\n## 5. Quality and reach (cheap, lower value)\n- Consistent terms ("Doc" vs "document" vs "page"), glossary hints, broken-link and stale-issue-reference checks.\n- Translate a section for a reader, shown only in their view and never written into the doc.\n\n## Ground rules for all of these\n- **Ask, don't interrupt.** Everything runs on demand, or shows as a quiet box or comment. Nothing pops up while people type. Each feature can be turned off per doc.\n- **Permissions:** AI only sees what the requesting person can see. A viewer of a "no copy" doc gets no full summary they could copy out. The rule is decided once, in Gaia.\n- **Write rules:** AI writes only suggestions or comments, with the agent and `actedFor` recorded (as in the screens).\n- **Cost:** summaries are cached per version, so ten people opening the doc means one Ares call, not ten.\n\n## What I'd do first\n1. **Catch-up summary**: the most visible gain for real-time teams, and cheap once version history exists.\n2. **Thread summary on resolve**: small, and it keeps decisions from getting lost.\n3. **Drift check**: our unique angle, but it needs issue links and Talos agents first, so it comes later.\n\nAll three fit the phases we discussed. 1 and 2 use the Option 1 path (Ares through Gaia) after history and comments are built. 3 uses the Option 2 path. None of them changes the first slice I planned, so that plan stands. My earlier questions are still open: `Doccontent` instead of `Doc.body`, block ids now, Talos and `gaia-mcp` for agents, and the milestone. Once you answer those, I'll send the revised plan.	message	["Updating the knowledge map"]	\N	1.017506	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-11-12 09:27:50	f	\N
bbbd72666af0-5335-ba3c-cd59530578a2	c31674596af0-6d25-f538-e6760785c02e	bugger, at least the flag can be set to restrictive, we will just have to set that to restrictive before going to production.\n\nLets get back the rest of the decision.\n**Runtime ↔ agent binding:**  Do we really have to worry about this. Agent management is an ACL so as long as that is set the person creating the Agent and its credentials is effectively the sponsor so if they mark the agent as active, that 'sponsor' has allow the runtime.\n**Delegated token lifetime:**  if we keep to the recommendation, what are we looking at 100 workflow in the worst case mean 100 tokens being issued every hours?\n**Scope of the first round:** Explain what we stand to loose if we go with your recommendation\n**Service-only access:** lets leave this for now.\n**Scope granularity on day one:** Go with the recommendation and not the followup\n**Audit:** An agent preforming any action needs to be logged, we are getting that right now, we created Agents as extension of users so that we didn't have to deal with changing the models. Are you saying that is not the case?\n\n<br>\n<br>\n<br>\n<br>	message	[]	\N	\N	1	Hammad Hassan	2026-11-08 19:55:39	f	[]
bff122a06af0-5335-ba3c-643bcf9fa916	c31674596af0-6d25-f538-e6760785c02e	Agent Nestor is working on it.	working	\N	\N	\N	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-11-08 19:55:43	f	\N
fad37ee16af0-5335-ba3c-77d1a82981bc	c31674596af0-6d25-f538-e6760785c02e	Agreed: switching to restrictive before production closes that gap, so I won't plan an issue for it. Your points in order:\n\n## Runtime ↔ agent binding\n**Agreed, we can drop it.** The rule becomes: any registered, active runtime may get a token for any active agent. Marking the agent active is the sponsor's consent, and agent management is already controlled by the ACL.\n\nThe one thing we give up: a runtime that gets taken over (a leaked runtime secret on someone's laptop) can act as **every** active agent, not just the ones meant for it. The guard is that you can revoke the runtime in one place. If that becomes a worry later, a field on the agent ("runs only on runtime X") can be added without redesigning anything.\n\n**One consequence:** with no binding, the scope ceiling has to live on **the agent**. The sponsor sets "delegated tokens for this agent may at most do `issue.*`, `comment.*`, …" next to the agent's other settings.\n\n## Delegated token lifetime\nTokens are issued **per agent per runtime, not per run.** Talos keeps one token per agent and shares it across all that agent's runs. It asks for a new one about 5 minutes before the hour runs out.\n\n| Case | Tokens per hour |\n|---|---|\n| 100 workflow runs, 5 distinct agents | 5 |\n| 100 runs, 100 distinct agents (worst case) | 100 |\n\nEach token is one row, the same as every Prometheus session refresh creates today. That's negligible. One token per run only made sense with per-step scopes, which you've dropped.\n\n## Audit\nAgent actions are already logged correctly. Agents are users, so `createdUser` and `modifiedUser` on records, and the activity entries, already name the agent. Nothing about models changes.\n\nWhat I meant was narrower: the record says *Hera did it*, not *which runtime* Hera was running on. We get that for free. Gaia's token table (`oauth_access_tokens`) stores the client, the user, the scope and the expiry of every token. If we keep those rows instead of purging them, "runtime R held a token for Hera from 10:00 to 11:00" is answerable. So: no new table and no model change.\n\nSide note: that table stores the raw access token. Anyone who can read the database can use any live token. That's worth its own issue later (store a hash), but it isn't part of this idea.\n\n## What we lose with the first-round scope\nFirst round = **service clients** (separate from agents) + **introspection that says what kind of actor a token belongs to** + **Talos runtime delegation** + **Hermes option A**. What stays exposed or missing until later:\n\n1. **Gaia → Hermes publishing keeps the shared secret.** If it leaks, someone can push fake events to browsers. Most events only make Prometheus reload from Gaia, so they cause noise but don't leak data. However, `notification.created` carries its own text, and Prometheus shows it as is. A forged event could put phishing text in someone's notification list.\n   - **Fix:** a small Hermes-only hardening issue in the first round, running in parallel with the rest: timing-safe comparison, and refuse to start outside local dev with the default secret.\n2. **Ares keeps its shared secret** for introspection. A leak lets someone check whether tokens are valid and read agents' tool grants. It doesn't give access to any data.\n3. **No service-only reads** (you deferred them). A runtime can't list agents itself, so Talos keeps naming its agents locally (`gaiaAgent` in `agent.json`, as today). It receives run requests through each agent's own delegated token, which works because `workflow.agent.run.requested` is already sent to the agent user.\n4. **People's tokens get no scopes.** Prometheus and gaia-mcp-as-a-person keep full user access, limited only by the ACL. That's fine: scopes exist to limit services.\n5. **Hermes still sees users' access tokens** (option B, the one-time socket ticket, isn't done). Hermes is ours and doesn't log them, so the risk is low.\n6. **Any runtime may run any active agent** (your binding decision above).\n\nNothing in this list blocks Talos running Gaia agents. Items 1 and 2 are the real exposure, and the hardening issue covers the worst of 1.\n\n## Before the plan\n1. **The scope ceiling on the agent:** a `delegationScopes` list (permission names with `*`) that the sponsor edits next to `modelConfig`. If empty, does the agent get no delegated tokens (recommended), or the agent's full ACL?\n2. **Registering runtimes:** an admin creates a runtime in Gaia (Settings, like Agents) and copies its secret once into Talos' local config, the same pattern as agent credentials (agaia-4028). OK?\n3. **The Hermes hardening issue** goes in the first round. OK?\n\nWith those answered I'll propose the plan: Gaia → Hermes → dev-tools issues, with the dependency order spelled out.	message	["Searching the code in gaia/docs/generated/models/Oauthaccesstoken.md", "Searching the code in dev-tools/talos", "Reading gaia/docs/generated/models/Oauthaccesstoken.md", "Updating the knowledge map"]	\N	0.210585	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-11-08 19:56:42	f	\N
53cd03f46af0-21ad-689a-ec25de6f11d1	c31674596af0-6d25-f538-e6760785c02e	1. **No service-only reads** (you deferred them). A runtime can't list agents itself, so Talos keeps naming its agents locally (`gaiaAgent` in `agent.json`, as today). It receives run requests through each agent's own delegated token, which works because `workflow.agent.run.requested` is already sent to the agent user.\nthis is not acceptable, this is the whole reason this thread got started.\n\n**The scope ceiling on the agent:**  Sure, if empty then no delegation\n**Registering runtimes:**  Not Ok, no manual copies, I am trying to get Talos to a state where it can be integrated into Ares.\n\n**The Hermes hardening issue:** OK\n\n<br>\n<br>	message	[]	\N	\N	1	Hammad Hassan	2026-11-08 20:06:43	f	[]
5743f0f66af0-21ad-689a-c1a95bb905ff	c31674596af0-6d25-f538-e6760785c02e	Agent Nestor is working on it.	working	\N	\N	\N	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-11-08 20:06:47	f	\N
998ac4b76af0-21ad-689a-7f7d92839309	c31674596af0-6d25-f538-e6760785c02e	Understood. I'm putting service-only reads back in and dropping manual copies. Here's how both work together.\n\n## Service-only reads (back in)\nA runtime's own service token, with no user behind it, can read through a fixed allowlist:\n- **The agents it may run.** That means active agents whose delegation scope list is not empty, which ties in with your "empty = no delegation".\n- **Each agent's spec:** role prompt, model settings, limits.\n- **Each agent's tool and skill grants.**\n\nThe rules:\n- Never credentials or secrets.\n- Nothing outside the list, whatever the ACL resolution mode says.\n- Every other request with a service token gets a 401.\n\nTalos then needs nothing local. It asks Gaia which agents exist, loads their specs, and gets a delegated token when one of them has work.\n\n**Receiving work:** today run requests go to each agent's user room (Hermes D-017). I recommend a **runtime room**, which a service token may join and a person's token may not. Gaia also sends `workflow.agent.run.requested` there, so one socket per runtime sees all the work. This is a Hermes contract change, so it needs an ADR. The alternative, one socket per agent opened with delegated tokens, needs no Hermes change but grows with the number of agents.\n\n## No manual copies: enrollment\nThe first credential has to come from somewhere. These are the options:\n\n| | How it works | Fits Talos (stdlib-only Python) | Fits Ares (deployed by CD) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **A. Approve on screen (recommended)** | On first start the service asks Gaia to enrol and shows a short code (`KQ7-3MX`). An admin sees the request in Gaia (Settings → Services) with the same code and clicks Approve. The service, which has been polling, then receives its secret straight from Gaia. Same idea as the device login on a TV (RFC 8628). Gaia keeps only a hash of the secret. | Yes | Needs someone to click once per deployment |\n| **B. Single-use enrollment token** | The deploy pipeline, which already holds deployment secrets, asks Gaia for a token valid once for 15 minutes and passes it to the container. The service enrols with it automatically and gets its own secret. Nobody copies anything long-lived. | Yes | Yes, unattended |\n| C. Key pair (private_key_jwt) | The service makes its own key pair. Gaia stores only the public key, so nothing secret ever leaves the service. The strongest option. | **No.** dev-tools is standard-library only, and Python's standard library can't sign with RSA or Ed25519 | Yes (Ares can use `cryptography`) |\n| D. Mutual TLS certificates | Infrastructure-heavy, needs a certificate authority | No | Possible later |\n\n**Recommendation:** A and B together.\n- A for Talos running on a machine today.\n- B for Ares and any other unattended deploy.\n- C later, once Talos lives inside Ares and can use a crypto library. The enrollment flow stays the same; only the credential type changes.\n\nOn top of that, services rotate their own secret on a schedule using their current token, so no secret lives forever and nobody handles rotation.\n\n**On "Talos inside Ares":** this makes Ares a service client of the same kind, so it can use the same enrollment. Ares currently has **no inbound authentication at all**: `ares/app/routers/planissue.py` takes any call, and agaia-4029's shared secret exists only on Gaia's side. Gaia → Ares will need the same treatment. I'd leave that for the next round, but flag it as a gap now.\n\n## Open points before the plan\n1. **Enrollment:** A + B on day one (recommended), or A only, with B when Ares is deployed?\n2. **Run requests:** a runtime room in Hermes (recommended), or one socket per agent?\n3. **Ares inbound authentication:** next round (recommended), or this one?	message	["Searching the code in ares", "Searching the code in gaia/core/libs", "Searching the code in ares/CLAUDE.md", "Listing ares/**/*.py in .", "Updating the knowledge map"]	\N	0.23