2472df306af0-e191-7b74-8ba71d685f56	c31674596af0-6d25-f538-e6760785c02e	Noted: Gaia-declared agents get their work only from Gaia workflows.\n\nOn Hermes getting a new token on each restart: that's fine for its **access token**, which it fetches with its client secret at every start anyway. The **client secret** is different. If Hermes keeps nothing between restarts, an unattended restart can't get one:\n\n- Enrollment tokens are single-use and last 15 minutes. After a crash, the container starts with a token that is already used or expired.\n- On-screen approval (option A) needs a person to click Approve after every restart.\n- The only fully automatic alternative is an enrollment token that can be reused and doesn't expire. That's a long-lived shared secret under a new name, which is what we're replacing.\n\nSo something long-lived has to survive restarts. The safest thing to keep is Hermes' own enrolled credential, as `h-service-identity` already does:\n- It's a file on a mounted volume, readable only by the Hermes process (mode 600).\n- Hermes replaces the secret weekly by itself.\n- Gaia stores only a hash of it.\n- An admin can disable it in one click.\n\nThe enrollment token is then only needed on first deploy, or after the volume is wiped.\n\n**Recommendation:** keep the plan as proposed. Approve it and Talos creates the 21 issues. If you'd rather go without the file anyway, tell me and I'll change `h-service-identity` to re-enroll on every start with a reusable enrollment token. It would also add a rule so the new enrollment replaces the old Hermes client instead of piling up stale ones. That brings back a long-lived shared secret, though.	message	["Updating the knowledge map"]	\N	0.196468	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-11-08 20:23:00	f	\N
