712d6c716af1-b1fb-36e2-555d5eb70161	c82823c16af0-9a8b-6f54-9c6fbacd2282	Downsides of reloading the list in place, roughly from most to least serious:\n\n1. **The URL and what's on screen can drift apart.**\n   - With `refreshModel: false`, Ember updates the URL but nothing reloads on its own; we have to watch for every change ourselves.\n   - Back/forward, links that carry these params, and a link from a notification all go through the `routeDidChange` hook. If that hook misses a case, the URL says page 3 while the list shows page 1. This is the most likely kind of bug to come out of this approach.\n\n2. **No route-level loading or error handling.**\n   - Ember's loading screens and error routes no longer cover these reloads, so the loader has to handle loading, errors and retry itself. The issue already asks for that.\n   - Every future list has to wire the same pieces, or it ends up half done.\n\n3. **Ember's own guarantees around a transition are lost.**\n   - Ember doesn't settle the transition until the model has loaded. With the loader, the list fills in after the page has rendered.\n   - Tests have to wait for the loader, and the screen briefly shows skeleton rows.\n\n4. **Two places load ideas.** The route loads the first page and the controller loads the rest. If their query building drifts apart (scope, archived, default sort), page 1 after a full reload would differ from page 1 after a click.\n   - **Mitigation:** both use one `buildRequest`. I'd make that explicit in the issue.\n\n5. **Stale and overlapping requests.**\n   - The request token drops a slow old response. A live (Hermes) reload can still race a click, and the last request to start wins. That's acceptable, but it can feel like a jump.\n   - Old requests aren't cancelled on the server, so quick clicking still sends several queries.\n\n6. **Live updates on later pages.** Reloading the current page when an idea is created or archived can shift rows between pages: something you just saw moves to page 2. Route refresh does the same thing, so this isn't new.\n\n7. **Controller state lives across visits.** Ember controllers are singletons, so the loader and its items stay around after you leave. Coming back could flash the previous project's ideas.\n   - **Mitigation:** reset or reseed the loader in `setupController`/`resetController`. The issue doesn't say this yet.\n\n8. **Saved searches and the "edited" state.** These read the `query` param, and they relied on the route refresh to rerun the search. They must now trigger `load()` too, or picking a saved search changes the bar but not the list.\n\n9. **More code than "just refresh".** A loader class, an action per param, the back/forward hook and the extra tests. That's more than adding a loading template.\n\n**Cheaper alternative.** Keep `refreshModel: true` and add an `idea-loading.hbs`. That would contain the loading screen to the idea area, but the outlet and the open idea page would still redraw, and the loading screen would replace the browser rather than just its list. Moving the list into a child route instead would be a bigger restructure. So I still recommend the loader.\n\nIf you agree, I'll add points 4, 7 and 8 to the in-place reload issue as explicit acceptance criteria:\n- one shared `buildRequest` for the route and the loader;\n- reset the loader on leaving and on a project switch;\n- a saved search triggers a reload.	message	\N	\N	0.142391	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-11-09 17:10:09	f	\N
