f5d586c86af4-3b69-e9a2-22f50a182c40	361eb9326af4-3b69-e9a2-8e04f96d31a4	You want one backend architecture that covers the whole Docs design: storage, real-time co-editing, review, sharing, AI agents, history and search. Then a plan to build it on top of the `Doc` resource that already exists. Here is what I recommend.\n\n## The core decisions\n\n| # | Decision | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **The doc's content is a Yjs CRDT document.** Gaia stores its latest state (base64 in a `content` text column; Gaia metadata has no binary type). Next to it Gaia stores two copies made from that state: `body` (HTML) and `bodyText` (plain text, full-text indexed). | A CRDT is what gives live co-editing, offline edits that merge, cursors and autosave. The HTML and plain-text copies cover search, export, AI, the issue/project link boxes and a read-only fallback, so nothing outside the editor needs to understand Yjs. |\n| 2 | **The editor is Tiptap (ProseMirror), replacing Toast UI for Docs.** | Toast UI can't use a CRDT. Tiptap has ready extensions for tables, task lists, images, headings/outline, Yjs and cursors. |\n| 3 | **A new collab server (Hocuspocus, Node) runs as its own process and container, in the hermes repo (`hermes/collab`).** | Hermes is a stateless relay (D-001) and browsers never publish to it (D-002). Co-editing needs a stateful server that holds the doc in memory, so it can't go in the relay. Putting it in the same repo reuses the Gaia session lookup (D-011/D-012) and the test setup, and gives it a tracker project now. |\n| 4 | **Gaia owns all data and every permission decision.** On connect, the collab server checks the user's token against Gaia; people who can't edit get a read-only connection. It saves the content back to Gaia through a dedicated service account, debounced and when the last person leaves. Only that account may write `content`, `body` and `bodyText` (field-level ACL). | This keeps the house rule that no service bypasses Gaia. |\n| 5 | **Only editors write to the CRDT.** Comments, replies and suggestions are Gaia records, pinned to the text by Yjs relative positions. Accepting a suggestion is an edit an editor makes in the editor. | The server can't safely check what a raw Yjs update changes, so commenters, viewers and **agents never get write access to the CRDT**. "Agent changes always arrive as suggestions" then holds by design, and each suggestion records `actedForUserId`. |\n| 6 | **Presence, live cursors, selections and "is typing" use Yjs awareness on the collab connection.** | They never touch Gaia or Hermes and never need saving. |\n| 7 | **The editor's extension list is a contract between Prometheus and the collab server.** The server needs the same schema to produce the HTML and text. | A new kind of block, such as an issue link, needs a change in both repos. |\n\n## How the rest of the design maps onto this (later phases)\n\n- **Managing the doc:**\n  - Nesting, move, duplicate, archive/restore and templates: new `Doc` fields (`parentId`, `status`, `isTemplate`).\n  - Duplicate copies the stored content.\n  - Tags reuse Tag/Tagged.\n  - File attachments reuse `Upload` (`relatedTo: doc`).\n  - Star/follow: a follower record.\n  - Lock: the collab server makes every connection read-only.\n- **Sharing:** a `Docmember` record (user and role: viewer, commenter or editor), plus `linkAccess` and `allowCopy` fields on the doc. Granting access per document goes beyond today's project-based ACL, so it is **Gaia ACL framework work** and gets its own complex issue.\n  - "Anyone with the link" for people who aren't signed in has no read path in Gaia today. I'd make it "any signed-in user with the link" for now.\n  - Blocking download and copy can only discourage people in the UI; a viewer can still copy from the page.\n- **Review:** `Doccomment` threads with an anchor and a resolved flag; mentions and notifications reuse the existing polymorphic `Mention`. `Docsuggestion` records support accept and reject.\n- **History:** a `Docversion` record holding a snapshot (state + HTML), saved automatically and by name. Compare is a diff of the HTML of two versions in the browser. Restore is an editor applying the old version as a new change.\n- **Project connection:**\n  - A `Doclink` record links a doc to an issue or milestone.\n  - An issue-link block shows the issue's live status from the issue events Hermes already sends.\n  - "Turn text into an issue" creates the issue, then inserts the link.\n- **AI:**\n  - An Ares endpoint for the assist actions (draft, summarize, open questions, improve wording), called through Gaia's `LLM.php`.\n  - @mentioning an agent hands the work to Talos/MCP. The agent can only create comments and suggestions in Gaia.\n- **Search and export:** full-text search on `bodyText` across docs, find inside a doc in the editor, and print/PDF from a print stylesheet. Word export comes later.\n\n## Three questions (my answer in brackets)\n\n1. **Where should the collab server live?** [In the hermes repo as a separate process with its own ADR, issues in A Hermes. A new repo would need a new tracker project first.]\n2. **Can we add the new dependencies?** These are Tiptap, `yjs`, `y-prosemirror`, `@hocuspocus/server` and `/provider`, and `y-indexeddb` for offline recovery. [Yes. The architecture depends on them, and the house rules require your approval.]\n3. **Does this plan replace the series ideas Docs 2/10 to 4/10 (editor, realtime, presence)?** [Yes, so those ideas don't repeat it. The later phases above then go into the matching series ideas.]\n\n## The plan: phase 1, the co-editing backbone\n\nThere are six issues:\n\n1. The ADRs.\n2. The Gaia content fields.\n3. The collab server with sign-in and permission checks.\n4. Saving to Gaia, which seeds docs written before this from their existing `body`.\n5. The Tiptap editor.\n6. Presence and live cursors.\n\nEverything else is left out of this phase and listed above.\n\nI couldn't run `ai kb` or open the idea in this session, so I haven't checked the number or state of the Docs 1/10 foundation issue. The `Doc` resource is in the code, so I treat it as available. I left the milestone out (it defaults to Claude-v1); say if these should go in **Docs MVP** with the rest of the series.	message	["Gaia: get idea", "Reading dev-tools/talos/state/nestor/map.md", "Running cd /home/rana.noman/web/hammad-hassan/Pr4M; dev-tools/ai/ai kb search \\"Docs\\" --project agaia 2>&1 | head -40; dev-tools/", "Listing gaia/docs/architecture/docs/**", "Searching the code in gaia/app", "Reading gaia/docs/architecture/docs/decisions.md", "Reading gaia/app/models/Doc.php", "Listing gaia/app/metadata/model/Doc*.php", "Searching the code in gaia/app/metadata/model/Project.php", "Listing hermes/docs/**/*.md", "Reading gaia/app/metadata/model/Doc.php", "Searching the code in hermes/docs/architecture/decisions.md", "Searching the code in gaia/app/metadata/model", "Searching for \\"'fts' => true\\" in gaia/app/metadata/model", "Listing gaia/app/metadata/model/{Notification,Mention,Issuemention,Upload,Fileattachment,Membership}*.php", "Searching the code in gaia/app/metadata/model/Mention.php", "Searching the code in gaia/app/metadata/model/Upload.php", "Updating the knowledge map"]	f5220da96af4-3b69-e9a2-525823ee6a6a	0.697602	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-11-11 15:12:53	f	\N
c04477b66af4-6cc7-77af-d2724006c579	d98352c16af4-9ee0-e9a2-4dddf1b444ee	Yes, we need to remove the Elasticsearch code, we are not using it.\nI would love to do side-side, it will have to go into its own branch upgradephp8512 in git. I am not sure if the other agents are capable of handling that yet. As we might have to get another deployment done to have it run side-by-side\nRegarding SMTP password, we might need to remove that as we are not relying on hard coded SMTP anymore, we have a settings page now.\nHowever, before coming up with an updated plan. I would like to understand the side-side strategy a bit more. Give me step by step on how you will manage that?	message	[]	\N	\N	1	Hammad Hassan	2026-11-11 15:16:16	f	[]
