049b13806af4-8f98-77af-5a67d15dab29	d98352c16af4-9ee0-e9a2-4dddf1b444ee	## How side by side would work\n\n"Side by side" here means **one codebase, two runtime images, two CI jobs**. It doesn't need a second running deployment. Production keeps running today's image until a single switch-over PR. The only extra environment is a one-off test against a copy of production data just before the switch (step 8).\n\n### Step by step\n\n**1. Build two images from one Dockerfile in the repo.**\n- `gaia-env:current` (PHP 7.4, Phalcon 4) rebuilds today's `rananoman/gaia:env-dev`.\n- `gaia-env:next` (PHP 8.5, Phalcon 5).\n\nOnly the build arguments differ. The reviewer publishes both tags.\n\n**2. Get the current stack fully tested.**\nThe whole PHPUnit suite runs in CI and passes. That's the baseline: from then on, a failure on `next` really comes from the upgrade.\n\n**3. Add the measuring tools to `dev`.**\nThese all run on today's stack and don't change how Gaia behaves:\n- `migrate --dry-run` and schema snapshots;\n- the api-tester saving response bodies, and the tool that compares two runs;\n- the deprecation report;\n- the compatibility scan.\n\n**4. Make fixes that work on both stacks, on `dev`.**\nFor example `#[\\AllowDynamicProperties]` on the base model, replacing `Phalcon\\Text`, and return-type attributes. Today's stack doesn't notice them; they just make the new stack's list shorter.\n\n**5. Add one compatibility class for startup.**\nIt's the only place that checks the Phalcon version:\n\n```php\n// core/libs/compat/PhalconCompat.php\npublic static function loader() {\n    return class_exists('Phalcon\\Autoload\\Loader')\n        ? new \\Phalcon\\Autoload\\Loader()   // Phalcon 5: setNamespaces()\n        : new \\Phalcon\\Loader();           // Phalcon 4: registerNamespaces()\n}\n// same for Logger, Config, Di and the session adapter\n```\n\n- `composer.json` allows `"php": "^7.4 || ^8.5"`. There's no lock file, so each image installs package versions that suit it.\n- The vendor cache is kept separately per image.\n- If some package has no version that works on both stacks, side by side stops working for that package. We'd find that out at this step, early, not at the end.\n\n**6. Two CI jobs on every PR.**\n- The **current** job blocks merging, as today.\n- The **next** job runs the same steps on `gaia-env:next` and never blocks. It posts one summary, for example:\n\n```\nNext stack (PHP 8.5.x / Phalcon 5.x)\nPHPUnit           212 passed, 37 failed                 [junit]\nmigrate dry-run   3 ALTER, 0 DROP planned               [log]\nschema diff       2 columns differ (issues.estimate type) [diff]\napi-tester        881/904 passed                        [report]\nresponse diff     14 endpoints changed (int→string: 9)  [diff]\ndeprecations      41 unique (Gaia 12, vendor 29)        [report]\n```\n\n**7. Fix in a loop.**\nEach fix issue takes one group from that summary, such as "Behaviour signatures" or "response types on /issue". It goes as a normal `claude-issue-NNNN` PR to `dev`, written so it works on both stacks. The current job stays green and the next summary's numbers go down. Agents work exactly as they do today.\n\n**8. Check against real data before switching.**\nWhen the next summary is clean, run `gaia-env:next` once against a **copy** of the production database:\n- `migrate --dry-run` must plan nothing;\n- the schema snapshot must match production's;\n- a quick check through Prometheus.\n\nA local `gaia-next` container on port 8083 is enough for this. A staging deployment is only needed if you want people to click through it.\n\n**9. Switch in two PRs.**\n- **Switch:** the Dockerfiles, compose, CI and CD use `next`, and composer requires PHP `^8.5`. The compatibility class stays, so rolling back is just redeploying the old image.\n- **Clean-up, about a week later:** remove the compatibility class, the current job and Phalcon 4 references.\n\n### Compared with an `upgradephp8512` branch\n\n| | Side by side on `dev` | `upgradephp8512` branch |\n|---|---|---|\n| Agents | Work as today | Can't target it yet: the base branch is hard-coded per repo (`dev-tools/ai/lib/common.py:35`, gaia → `dev`). Needs a dev-tools issue to allow a per-issue base branch. |\n| Keeping up with `dev` | Nothing to do | Regular merges from `dev`. Conflicts in `docs/generated/**` and `graph.json`, which CI checks for drift. |\n| Visibility | Every PR's summary | Only PRs to the branch |\n| Deployment | One, plus a one-off check against a production copy | A branch deployment to test, then one big merge |\n| Rollback | Swap the image | Revert the big merge |\n\n**My recommendation:** keep all of it on `dev`. The point of the compatibility class is that no long-lived branch is needed, and that removes both your concerns: agents work unchanged, and there's no second deployment.\n\nIf you still want the branch, a middle way works too:\n- steps 1 to 4 go to `dev` anyway, since they're harmless on today's stack;\n- only the new-stack work (step 5 onward) goes to `upgradephp8512`;\n- the plan gets one more issue in **A Dev Tool** so agents can branch from, and open PRs against, a branch other than `dev`.\n\n### Your other two answers\n- **Elasticsearch:** removing it stays in the plan.\n- **SMTP:** the issue becomes "remove the environment-variable SMTP setup". That means the Gmail username and password in compose, and the `getenv` fallback in `core/config/smtp.php`, which defaults to `smtp.gmail.com`.\n  - One catch: the `gaia-test` containers rely on `SMTP_HOST=127.0.0.1:9` so test runs never reach a real mail server. The issue must keep that guarantee: with empty Settings, no mail is sent.\n  - The Gmail app password is still in git history, so it needs rotating whatever we do.\n\n**Which do you want: everything on `dev`, or the middle way with `upgradephp8512` plus the dev-tools issue?** I'll send the updated plan once you choose.	message	["Searching the code in dev-tools/ai/lib", "Searching the code in gaia", "Updating the knowledge map"]	\N	0.330203	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-11-11 15:17:24	f	\N
