31d9ab936af4-343a-77af-73a8c583acca	361eb9326af4-3b69-e9a2-8e04f96d31a4	A stateful server isn't strictly required. Yjs merges edits in the browsers, so a server only has to pass the changes along and store them. You could build it without one. The trouble is the traffic, which doesn't fit Gaia or Hermes as they are designed.\n\n## The stateless version\n- Each browser posts its Yjs changes to Gaia as a standard create on a `Docupdate` resource, which is an append-only log.\n- Gaia saves each change and publishes `doc.updated` through Hermes. The other browsers apply it.\n- Opening a doc loads every logged change and merges them in the browser.\n\n## Where that breaks down\n1. **Cursors, selections and "is typing" have no route.** They change many times a second and should never be stored. Browsers can't publish to Hermes (D-002), so each cursor move would be a PHP request that writes nothing. Supporting them means rewriting D-002 to give browsers a publish door, which is the Hermes ADR fight from the earlier Wiki idea.\n2. **Keystroke traffic goes through PHP.** Even with changes batched every 300 ms, every active editor makes several Gaia requests a second. Each one runs auth, ACL, a DB write and a Hermes publish. A stateful server keeps the doc in memory and writes to Gaia once every 2 to 10 s.\n3. **The log needs merging.** Someone has to merge the log into a snapshot, or opening a doc gets slower over time. Without a server, a browser does it and races other browsers.\n4. **Opening a doc is slower.** The browser downloads and replays the whole log instead of getting one merged state in a single message.\n5. **Two browsers write the HTML copy.** `Doc.body` would be written by whichever browser saves last, and that copy feeds search, export and agents.\n6. **Agents need somewhere to edit.** An agent's suggestions must land in the live doc. With a server, the agent joins as a server-side participant. Without one, an agent has to act like a browser.\n\n## What "stateful" means here\nThe in-memory doc is only a working copy. Gaia's `Docstate` stays the stored copy, so the server can restart or crash and lose at most a few seconds of edits. Browsers also keep unsent edits locally and resend them. One instance is enough for now. To scale, you route each doc to one instance by document name.\n\n## My recommendation\nKeep the separate collab server: it's one small process that handles all six points. If a new process is the real worry, the fallback is the stateless version. It needs a Hermes ADR giving browsers a rate-limited publish door, the `Docupdate` log in Gaia, and merging in the browser. That's more issues and touches two locked decisions.\n\nThe plan stays as it is unless you'd rather go stateless. Say so and I'll redo it.	message	\N	\N	0.108199	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-11-11 15:26:41	f	\N
