af8f6cf16af4-c7eb-1971-effb4b3e9226	361eb9326af4-3b69-e9a2-8e04f96d31a4	My recommendation is the hermes repo, but **in its own folder that can be moved out later**. It costs almost nothing now, and you can still move it into its own repo once it grows. Here are the trade-offs.\n\n## Inside the hermes repo\n**Pros**\n- **No setup.** The A Hermes project, CI, Docker build, `p4m` stack, `ai test`/`ai ship`, Talos repo sync and Odysseus remotes all work already. The first Docs code can start right away.\n- **Shared foundations.** Node version, lint and test setup, plus the Gaia sign-in code in `src/auth/gaia-session.js`.\n\n**Cons**\n- **It blurs what Hermes is.** Hermes' CLAUDE.md and D-001 describe a locked, stateless relay. Agents and reviewers working on Hermes will now also see a stateful server and need clear guidance to keep the two apart.\n- **Heavy dependencies.** Yjs, Hocuspocus and the Tiptap/ProseMirror packages would bloat the relay's `package.json` and image if they're shared. Upgrading them would also be tied to the relay.\n- **Shared releases.** A relay fix rebuilds and redeploys the collab server, and the other way round. Nothing is lost, because docs are saved to Gaia and kept in the browser, but open docs reconnect.\n- **The editor schema lives in Hermes.** The Tiptap schema shared with Prometheus would sit in a repo named for a relay.\n- **They scale differently.** The relay can simply add instances. The collab server needs each doc pinned to one instance. Separate containers handle this either way.\n\n## A separate repo\n**Pros**\n- **Clean boundary.** Its own CLAUDE.md, ADRs, dependencies, release schedule and scaling. The relay stays exactly as D-001 describes it.\n- **Room to grow.** The roadmap gives this server more work: agents editing as participants, version snapshots, possibly PDF/Word export (Node has better libraries than PHP). It becomes the server side of Docs, not just a sync process.\n\n**Cons**\n- **Setup before any Docs code.** It needs:\n  - a GitHub repo and a workspace submodule;\n  - a new Gaia project, which I can't plan issues in until it exists;\n  - CI and a Docker image;\n  - dev-tools changes in `installer/p4m.py`, `ai/config.json`, `ai/lib/tests.py`/`ship.py` and Talos' repo handling.\n\n  That's roughly one or two A Dev Tool issues plus admin work only a person can do. Real-time editing would be delayed by that.\n- **Duplicated sign-in code.** It needs its own copy of the Gaia sign-in helper (small).\n\n## The middle path I recommend\nPut it in hermes as **`collab/`**, set up so moving it out later is just moving the folder:\n- its own `package.json`, its own Dockerfile target and container, and its own README and ADR;\n- **no imports from the relay's `src/`**: it gets its own copy of the small Gaia sign-in helper;\n- the ADR says it moves to its own repo when it takes on agents or export, or when its release schedule starts to conflict with the relay's.\n\nYou get the clean boundary in the code now and pay for a new repo only when the growth actually happens.\n\nIf you agree, I'll update the `collab-server` issue to match. It currently says to reuse `src/auth/gaia-session.js`; it would instead copy the helper, add its own `package.json` and container, and include the move-out trigger in the ADR. If you'd rather have a separate repo now, create the repo and its Gaia project. I'll then plan the dev-tools wiring as A Dev Tool issues and move the collab issue there.	message	["Running grep -rlI --exclude-dir=state --exclude-dir=node_modules --exclude-dir=tests -i \\"hermes\\" dev-tools | grep -v \\"\\\\.md$\\" | h", "Searching for \\"hermes\\" in dev-tools", "Updating the knowledge map"]	\N	0.156587	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-11-11 15:37:19	f	\N
