1e8ff4416af5-e77a-b9d7-64b77fb183e7	361eb9326af4-3b69-e9a2-8e04f96d31a4	The design asks for two different kinds of agent work, and they suit different setups:\n\n- **Quick help:** "improve this paragraph", "summarize", "find open questions". The person waits a few seconds and expects one answer.\n- **A collaborator:** "@Odysseus draft the API section from issues 4488–4492". This takes minutes, uses tools, and should look like another person in the doc.\n\n## Option 1: Quick help through Ares (the user's own browser applies it)\nHow it works:\n1. The user selects text and picks an action in the editor.\n2. Prometheus creates a request through a Gaia resource (Prometheus may only talk to Gaia).\n3. Gaia calls Ares, the same way `LLM.php` calls `/planissue` today.\n4. Ares returns the proposed text, and Prometheus shows a preview.\n5. On "Insert", the user's own editor writes it in as **suggestion marks**, attributed to the agent with `actedFor` = the user.\n\n- **Pros:** no new runtime. Fast. Suggestion-only and attribution come for free, because the text goes through the same editor as everything else. It covers drafting, summarizing, finding open questions and better wording.\n- **Cons:** the agent isn't "in" the doc. It can't take on longer tasks or answer an @mention.\n- "Create issues from text" fits here too. Ares proposes issues, the user ticks the ones to create, Gaia creates them, and the selected text becomes issue links.\n\n## Option 2: The agent joins the doc as a live participant (Talos + collab server)\nHow it works:\n1. A comment mentions an agent, and Gaia publishes the comment event through Hermes.\n2. **Talos**, which already runs our agents on assignments, ideas and Hermes events, starts that agent with the comment as its task.\n3. The agent connects to the **collab server** with its own Gaia account (agents are Gaia `Agent` users) and works through a small set of tools:\n   - `doc_read`: the doc as Markdown blocks, each with a **stable block id**;\n   - `doc_propose(blockId, newMarkdown)` and `doc_insert_after(blockId, markdown)`;\n   - `comment_reply(threadId, text)`.\n4. The collab server turns each proposal into suggestion marks in the live doc.\n\nDesign points that matter:\n- **Address by block, not by character.** Language models are bad at character offsets and good at rewriting a paragraph. The server compares the agent's new Markdown with the block it read and makes exact suggestions. If a person changed that block in the meantime, the server either reapplies the change on the current text or asks the agent to re-read.\n- **The server forces suggestion-only, not the prompt.** Agent connections may only add suggestion marks, comments and presence. Any other change is rejected by the collab server.\n- **Attribution:** every mark carries `agentId`, `actedFor` (the person who asked) and a request id. Gaia records an Activity entry, and "Reject all from this request" is possible.\n- **Permissions are the lower of the two:** an agent never gets more than the person who called it. A viewer can't use an agent to write.\n- **It feels live:** the agent shows in the avatar list with "Odysseus is writing…" and a cursor, and suggestions stream in. People can keep typing, because the CRDT merges. There is also a **Stop** button.\n- **Prompt injection:** doc text is untrusted input to the agent. Suggestion-only plus a human accepting each change is the protection.\n\n- **Pros:** this is the "agents as teammates" experience, and it reuses the Talos agent runtime.\n- **Cons:** the most work: block ids in the editor, an agent API on the collab server, a mention trigger in Talos, and doc tools in an MCP server.\n\n## Option 3: Agents only through Gaia rows (turned down)\nThe agent posts "proposed change" rows to Gaia and the collab server applies them later. Nothing is live and everything takes an extra hop. It only makes sense if we never want agents connected to the collab server. I'd skip it.\n\n## My recommendation\n1. Build **Option 1 first**. It covers most AI items in the design cheaply.\n2. Then build **Option 2** for @mentions and longer tasks, block-based and suggestion-only enforced by the server.\n3. Both depend on the earlier phases: **suggestions** (track-changes marks with author and `actedFor`), **comments**, and **stable block ids** in the editor schema. The block ids are worth adding to the editor schema from the start (`doc-editor`), so existing docs already have them when agents arrive.\n\n## Questions (my recommendation in bold)\n1. Should Talos run the doc agents (**yes**: it already runs our agents and logs time and cost), or should Ares grow its own agent loop?\n2. Agent doc tools: **new tools in `gaia-mcp`** that call the collab server's agent API, or a separate docs MCP server? The dev-tools rule says the tracker is reached only through `gaia-mcp`. The collab server isn't the tracker, but one MCP server is simpler for agents.\n3. Shall I add **stable block ids** to the `doc-editor` and `collab-server` issues now? It's small (one unique-id attribute in the shared schema).\n\nMy question from the storage discussion is also still open: do you agree with renaming `Docstate` to `Doccontent` and dropping `Doc.body`? Once you answer, I'll send the full revised plan with all of it.	message	["Searching for \\"mention\\" in dev-tools/talos/lib", "Running ls ares/app/routers ares/app/services; dev-tools/ai/ai kb search \\"agent mention comment trigger\\" 2>&1 | grep \\"^#\\" | head", "Updating the knowledge map"]	\N	0.869866	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-11-12 07:05:02	f	\N
