ed6b21856af7-a85f-40f4-ea1422bdc6a2	361eb9326af4-3b69-e9a2-8e04f96d31a4	Here is the backend design. In short: everything in a doc syncs live over the collab server, changes to the doc's own record (title, lock, delete) go out through Hermes, and Gaia stays in charge of storage and permissions. Making it all real-time needs three gaps closed, listed under "Changes to the plan".\n\n## Components\n\n```\n                    ┌─────────────────────────────── Browser (Prometheus) ───────────────────────────────┐\n                    │ Tiptap editor ── Y.Doc ── IndexedDB (offline)       doc-ai service    hermes service │\n                    └────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────┬──────────────┬─────────┘\n          ① WebSocket (Yjs)  │ updates, awareness, server messages      HTTPS     │   ③ Socket.IO │ doc.* events\n                             ▼                                           (REST)   ▼               ▼\n                 ┌──────────────────────┐   ② content saves      ┌──────────────────┐  publish  ┌──────────────┐\n                 │ Collab server        │ ─────────────────────▶ │ Gaia (PHP)        │ ────────▶ │ Hermes relay │\n                 │ hermes/collab        │   (user's token)       │ Doc, Doccontent,  │           │ (unchanged)  │\n                 │ Hocuspocus, Y.Docs   │ ◀───────────────────── │ Docassist, ACL    │           └──────────────┘\n                 │ in memory            │   ④ access changed     └────────┬─────────┘\n                 └──────────────────────┘   (lock/delete, secret)        │ HTTP\n                                                                         ▼\n                                                                ┌──────────────────┐\n                                                                │ Ares (FastAPI)    │ /docs/ask|complete|review\n                                                                └──────────────────┘\n                                                                Postgres 16: doc, doccontent, docassist\n```\n\n## The real-time channels\n\n| What changes | Channel | Who sees it, and how fast |\n|---|---|---|\n| Text, formatting, tables | ① collab WebSocket (Yjs updates) | everyone in the doc, about 50–100 ms |\n| **Suggestions** from Ask AI and the Reviewer, accept/reject | ① (they're marks inside the Y.Doc) | everyone, live |\n| **Reviewer notes** and resolving them | ① (a shared `notes` map in the **same Y.Doc**) | everyone, live (**changed**, see below) |\n| Presence, cursors, selections, "is typing" | ① awareness | everyone, live, never stored |\n| **"Rana is asking AI…" / "Reviewing…"** | ① awareness (`ai` flag) | everyone, live (**new**) |\n| Lock, delete, losing access | ④ Gaia → collab server, then ① server message to each client | open editors switch to read-only or close within a second (**new**) |\n| Title, lock, archive, new or deleted doc | ③ Hermes `doc.created/updated/deleted` | All Docs list, doc header and other open tabs (**new**) |\n| Ghost text | HTTPS, one user only | per user, about 0.5–1.5 s (Haiku) |\n\n## How the main flows work\n\n**Opening a doc**\n1. Prometheus loads the `Doc` record (title, lock, owner) over REST.\n2. It opens the WebSocket to `doc:<id>` with the user's token.\n3. The collab server checks the user with Gaia `user/me` (`doc.get`, `doc.update`) and `GET doc/<id>` (lock, project). The result is either rejected, read-only, or editable.\n4. If the doc isn't in memory, the server loads `Doccontent.state` once. Every later visitor gets the in-memory copy.\n5. The browser merges in anything it still has from an offline session (IndexedDB) and starts syncing.\n\n**Editing**\n1. Each keystroke becomes a small Yjs update. It goes to the server, which forwards it to the other clients. Read-only connections' updates are dropped by the server.\n2. Saving: every 2–10 s the server writes `Doccontent.state`. After 30 s idle, or when the last person leaves, it also writes `html` and `plainText`. Each save uses the token of a connected editor.\n3. A doc nobody is in is saved and dropped from memory.\n\n**Ask AI and the AI Reviewer**\n1. The browser sends a request to Gaia (`POST /docassist`). Gaia checks `doc.update`, the rate limit and the input size, calls Ares, logs the row and returns the result. Timeouts: complete 5 s, ask 30 s, review 60 s.\n2. While waiting, the requester's awareness shows `ai: 'ask'` or `'review'`, so others see "Rana is asking AI…".\n3. The requester's editor writes the result into the Y.Doc as suggestions, plus notes for the reviewer. It all reaches everyone through ①, so AI output needs no separate real-time path.\n4. Trade-off: if the requester closes the tab mid-request, the result is still logged in `Docassist` but not inserted. That's acceptable now. Later, when agents edit on their own, the collab server applies results itself.\n\n**Lock or delete**\n1. Gaia saves the change. It then publishes `doc.updated`/`doc.deleted` to Hermes for lists and headers, and calls the collab server's internal endpoint `POST /internal/docs/<id>/access` with a shared secret.\n2. The collab server re-checks every connection to that doc with Gaia. It switches connections to read-only, or closes them, and sends each client a message such as "This doc was locked by Hamza".\n3. Without this, an editor that missed the lock keeps pushing edits. The collab server would spread them to the others in memory, but Gaia would refuse to save them, so everyone would see text that never gets saved.\n\n## Storage (as discussed)\n- `doc` holds the small metadata row and fires Hermes events.\n- `doccontent` holds the Yjs state (base64), `html` and `plainText`, with no audit trail. The reviewer notes are saved as part of `state`, since they're in the same Y.Doc.\n- `docassist` logs every AI request (who, when, kind, status, result).\n- Presence and ghost text are never stored. Offline edits live in the browser's IndexedDB.\n\n## Failure behaviour\n\n| Down | What users see |\n|---|---|\n| Collab server | "Offline – changes kept on this device". Editing continues locally and syncs when the server returns. |\n| Gaia | Real-time editing continues. The server keeps the doc in memory and retries saves with back-off. After 2 min unsaved, every client sees "Not saved to server yet". |\n| Ares | That AI request fails with a message, and ghost text quietly backs off. Editing is unaffected. |\n| Hermes | Lists and headers don't refresh live until the page is reloaded. Nothing in the doc is affected. |\n\n## Security and limits\n- The WebSocket checks the request's origin. Each Yjs update is size-capped and each connection is rate-limited. The server enforces read-only, not just the UI.\n- Tokens expire. The client refreshes and reconnects before expiry. A save that fails with 401 retries with another connected editor's token.\n- Gaia's access checks decide everything. The collab server only caches the answer per connection and asks again when Gaia tells it something changed.\n- AI suggestions are inserted by the requester's own editor, so the server can't tell them apart from that person's typing. That's acceptable because a person asked and could edit anyway. When agents edit on their own later, "suggestion-only" will be enforced by the server.\n\n## Scaling\nOne collab server instance to start, with each doc in memory only while someone has it open. To grow, run several instances and route each doc to one of them by its name, coordinated through Redis (Hocuspocus has a Redis extension). That's a later change.\n\n## Changes to the plan\n1. **New, A Gaia: doc live events and the collab notification.**\n   - Publish `doc.created`, `doc.updated` (changed fields) and `doc.deleted` through a `DocLiveEventsComponent`, following `docs/generated/bundles/E.md` and the `UserAccessLiveEventsComponent` example.\n   - Add a `CollabNotifier` class (secret from env, failures logged and never blocking the save) that calls the collab server on a lock or delete.\n2. **New, A Hermes (relay): add `doc.*` to the allowed event names** in `src/contract/names.js`, matching `EventNames.php`, with an ADR entry.\n3. **New, A Hermes (collab): the internal access endpoint.** It re-checks the doc's connections, switches them to read-only or closes them, sends the client messages, shows "not saved" after Gaia has been failing, and caps update sizes and rates. This is separate from `collab-server` so that issue stays within size.\n4. **New, A Prometheus: live doc metadata.**\n   - Register for the Hermes `doc.*` events on the All Docs list and the doc page.\n   - Handle the collab server's messages (switch to read-only, "doc deleted", "not saved").\n5. **Changed: reviewer notes move into the Y.Doc** (a `notes` map, anchored by Yjs relative positions so they move with the text). Resolving a note is shared and live. Loading "the latest review" from `Docassist` is no longer needed. This changes `doc-ai-review` and `doc-suggestions`.\n6. **Changed: the AI busy flag** is set by `doc-ask-ai` and `doc-ai-review`, and shown by `doc-presence`.\n\nDoes this match what you have in mind? If so, I'll send the full revised plan, about 17 issues. The milestone question is still open: Claude-v1, or "Docs MVP" like 4488?	message	["Searching the code in gaia/app/api/v1/controllers/components/events/support/EventNames.php", "Running ls gaia/docs/generated/bundles/; ls gaia/app/api/v1/controllers/components/events/", "Running grep -l -i \\"live event\\" gaia/docs/generated/bundles/*.md; head -5 gaia/app/api/v1/controllers/components/events/UserAcce", "Updating the knowledge map"]	\N	0.487369	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-11-13 13:38:53	f	\N
688fba896af7-32e9-40f4-ab52e79602ba	361eb9326af4-3b69-e9a2-8e04f96d31a4	Let's say there are multiple documents for a project and a user searches against a keyword. How good is our data structure is for searching or should we go with someother data structure?	message	[]	\N	\N	7ea8b586623c-5e99-bfcb-d51d01a7a16b	Rana Nouman	2026-11-13 13:49:28	f	[]
