f0861f206afb-b5db-dcfe-299093cf225c	1cecd6c76afb-1ee8-c328-6f16c99cd135	Got it: `parentId` stays for now, blocking isn't enforced (workflows will do that later), and this idea covers only Gaia. The hard part is the ACL for links between projects, so here's what I found and what I propose.\n\n## How project ACL works today\n- Project-scoped models list `'acl' => ['groups' => ['Project']]`. Under the "members" scope (D-060), each row is filtered through **one** project key: a direct `projectId`, or a `groupKeys` path that picks one path per row (see `Activity.php:169`).\n- **A link between two projects doesn't fit that.** It belongs to two projects, and the framework can't express that yet.\n- **One unknown:** D-021 removes forbidden relations for a whole model, not per row. I couldn't confirm whether an embedded `Issue` from a project you're not a member of is filtered out row by row. The ACL issue has to check this and fix it if needed.\n\n## Proposed ACL rules for `Issuelink`\n- **What it stores:**\n  - `sourceIssueId`, `targetIssueId` and `type`.\n  - Both `projectId` (the source's project) and `targetProjectId`, always filled in by the server from the two issues, never taken from the request body. They stay in step if an issue moves to another project.\n- **Read:** you can see a link if you're in scope for **either** project. A member of B needs to see that their issue is blocked by something in A.\n  - The issue on the side you can't read is left out, but `targetProjectId` / `projectId` stay. That's enough for the UI to show "blocked by an issue in another project" later without leaking any of its data.\n- **Create:** you need `issuelink.create`, read scope on **both** issues (you can't link to what you can't see) and write scope on **at least one** of the two projects.\n- **Delete:** the same write rule.\n- **Cycle checks look at the whole graph**, including issues you can't read. The error message never names those issues.\n\n## Graph rules (422 when broken)\n- **For every link type:** no self-links, and no duplicates. `relates` counts as a duplicate in either direction.\n- **`blocks`:** no cycles in the chain.\n- **`splits`:** no cycles either, and an issue can be split from only one source.\n- **Parent/child:** the same cycle check on `Issue.parentId`. Today that check doesn't exist anywhere, so it's a real fix.\n- Link writes are serialised with a transaction-level advisory lock, so two writes at the same moment can't create a cycle between them.\n\n## Issues\nThere are three Gaia issues. The ACL capability and the graph service can go in parallel; the resource needs both.\n- **Left out:**\n  - Live events for links.\n  - A workflow trigger or condition for "blocked" (that comes with the workflow enforcement you mentioned).\n  - gaia-mcp tools, so Talos can't use links yet.\n  - Moving `parentId` into `Issuelink`.\n  - All Prometheus work.\n- **For you to review:** the read rule ("either project") and the write rule ("read both, write either") are my proposal. They're written into the ACL and resource issues, so please confirm them or change them.	message	["Searching the code in gaia/core/libs/security/Acl.php", "Searching the code in gaia/docs/architecture/rbac/decisions.md", "Reading gaia/core/libs/security/Acl.php", "Reading gaia/core/libs/security/Acl.php", "Reading gaia/docs/architecture/rbac/decisions.md", "Reading gaia/docs/architecture/rbac/decisions.md", "Searching for \\"groupKeys\\" in gaia", "Searching the code in gaia/app/metadata/model", "Reading gaia/app/metadata/model/Activity.php", "Searching the code in gaia/docs", "Updating the knowledge map"]	ed55c6206afb-b5db-dcfe-b7e74ca23f6b	0.43882	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-11-16 21:13:20	f	\N
ad443ae06afc-3b8d-6053-c92ba7a7ca9a	1cecd6c76afb-1ee8-c328-6f16c99cd135	I think before we go into this, we need to see how to link projects. If we do that then handling the issues cross-projects will be easier to handle.	message	[]	\N	\N	1	Hammad Hassan	2026-11-17 09:04:45	f	[]
