de5e1c516afc-3b8d-6053-02aff979831b	1cecd6c76afb-1ee8-c328-6f16c99cd135	Agreed. If two projects are linked first, issue links between them stop being an ACL edge case and become something the projects have chosen.\n\n**What exists:** nothing. `Project` only relates to its own children (issues, milestones, memberships and so on). There's no project-to-project relation and no issue about one in the tracker.\n\n**My proposed shape:** a `Projectlink` CRUD resource between two projects. Project A requests it and project B accepts it, so one project can't attach itself to another. Issue links across projects are then allowed only between linked projects with status `accepted`. Within one project, no project link is needed.\n\nBefore I plan it, three questions (my recommendation in bold):\n\n1. **What does a project link grant?**\n   - **(a) Permission only:** issues in A and B may be linked to each other, and visibility stays purely by membership.\n   - (b) Permission plus limited visibility (see question 2).\n   - (c) A project hierarchy (program, sub-project).\n\n   **I recommend (a) plus question 2.** I'd leave hierarchy out, because it's a different feature.\n2. **What does a member of B see of an issue in A that's linked to their issue?** **A fixed summary: the project's short code, the issue number, subject and status, and nothing else.** It's readable only through the link, and only while the projects are linked. Without it, "blocked by an issue in another project" is useless to the person who is blocked.\n   - This is a new ACL concept ("a link grants a summary of the other side"), so it gets its own framework issue.\n   - The alternative is to show only the project and the fact that an issue exists. That's simpler, but much less useful.\n3. **Who requests and accepts, and what happens when a link is removed?**\n   - **Requesting:** anyone with `projectlink.create` who is in scope for project A.\n   - **Accepting or declining:** someone with `projectlink.update` who is in scope for project B.\n   - **One symmetric link per pair** of projects.\n   - **Unlinking:** issue links between the two projects stay stored, but the summary from question 2 stops being visible. Nothing is deleted, and linking again restores it.\n\nOnce you answer these, I'll replace the earlier plan. Project linking would come first (resource, then the handshake rules), then the summary-visibility ACL capability, then the issue graph and the `Issuelink` resource as before.	message	["Searching the code in gaia/app/metadata/model/Project.php", "Running cd D:/Claude/Workspace/Projects/Pr4M && dev-tools/ai/ai kb search linked projects related project 2>&1 | grep -A1 '^#' |", "Updating the knowledge map"]	\N	0.771335	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-11-17 09:05:34	f	\N
371b6fe86afc-ae59-6053-65eb60716323	368036d36afc-ae59-6053-37801a47463a	The current implementation of the Navbar has multiple issue.\n1\\. The project selector is in the Navbar so when the navbar is hidden which project is currently loaded is not visible\\.\n2\\. There is the use image and live status\\, the profile image is redundant\\, the image is already available in the header\\, can should move the live status in the header as well\\.\n3\\. The Dashboard and Projects works across projects but Issues\\, Task Board\\, Gantt\\, Threads are project dependent\\. We should group the application wide actions like Dashboard and Projects up top and the rest below them\n4\\. The Dashboard\\, once opened sticks around\\, this is not very handy on mobile\\.\n\nLets create screens to see how the new navbar and header will work for both desktop and mobile phone.	message	[]	\N	\N	1	Hammad Hassan	2026-11-17 09:15:35	f	[]
