dcf21b816afc-07a6-74f5-d3465cc4d13f	411da3826afc-9a36-ac6f-3a7c323d23c4	I've applied both points.\n\n**1. Ares now sends the hash, and Gaia stores the vector only if it still matches (otherwise 409, and Ares drops it).** Two details make this reliable:\n- **One place builds the text.** If Gaia (PHP) and Ares (Python) each strip HTML and cut the text themselves, a tiny difference would make every PATCH a 409. So Gaia builds the canonical semantic text once (`SemanticText`). It offers two read-only computed fields, only to the ares service: `searchSemanticText` and `searchSemanticHash`.\n  - Ares embeds exactly that text and sends back that hash.\n  - Ares's dirty check becomes `searchSemanticHash` ≠ `searchEmbeddingHash`.\n  - The event payload shrinks to `{type, resource, recordId}`.\n- **Check and write happen together.** Gaia locks the row (`SELECT … FOR UPDATE`), recomputes the current hash, and writes only if it equals the one Ares sent, all in one transaction. A subject edit can't slip in between the check and the write.\n- The daily reindex also compares hashes now, instead of dates.\n\n**2. The event is now `search.semantic.changed`** in Gaia, Hermes and Ares.\n\n`SemanticText` now lives in `search-metadata` (8 files), because the service write and the reindex both need it. There are still 16 issues.	message	["Updating the knowledge map"]	dc0c6a366afc-07a6-74f5-1e90e0dcdb01	0.484766	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-11-17 15:12:29	f	\N
