8e35ddc26afc-b85d-5f9c-1024300b751a	411da3826afc-9a36-ac6f-3a7c323d23c4	I recommend storing the semantic text and its hash instead of building computed-field support. Gaia keeps the canonical text and hash as two ordinary system columns, written in the same save as the change. Ares reads them like any other field, and nothing in Gaia needs to compute values at read time.\n\n- **Two new system columns per searchable model:** `searchSemanticText` (the canonical text, up to 4000 characters) and `searchSemanticHash` (its sha256).\n- **Same write as the edit:** when a record is created or a semantic field changes, `searchIndexBehavior` sets both columns before the save, then publishes `search.semantic.changed` after it.\n- **A simpler 409 check:** Gaia locks the row and compares the hash Ares sent with the stored `searchSemanticHash`. It no longer recomputes anything during the PATCH.\n- **Existing rows get filled by reindex:** `search reindex` fills in text and hash where they're missing and recomputes them if the text rule changes. It then re-publishes every record whose `searchEmbeddingHash` differs from `searchSemanticHash`.\n\nThe cost is a copy of up to 4 KB of text on each searchable row. That's small next to the 3 KB vector already stored there.\n\nThe other option is general computed-field support in the framework: one more complex issue that touches serialization, `fields=` and the access checks. I'd only build it if you want computed fields for other things too.\n\n**Changes from the last version:**\n- `search-metadata` adds the two columns.\n- The behaviour moves into `search-events`, so `service-search-access` now depends on it.\n- Ares reads stored fields; the rest is unchanged.\n\nThere are still 16 issues.	message	["Updating the knowledge map"]	8d25cf846afc-b85d-5f9c-933b67487895	0.476885	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-11-17 16:02:22	f	\N
