e406ab836ad9-025d-c4b2-2c17546bcc8c	48d0b1d36ad9-bd7c-a941-71476aac218e	1	{"summary": "Postgres full-text search driven by model metadata. Gaia framework first: a field-level `search` metadata key that makes the migration build a GIN tsvector expression index per model (agaia, complex), then a MATCHES query operator on the `Model.search` pseudo-field that uses that index with prefix matching and relevance ordering (agaia, complex). Issue's subject and description are then marked searchable (agaia, simple), and the Prometheus header search switches to the new operator (aprom, simple). The chain is sequential because each step needs the previous one.", "issues": [{"key": "search-index", "project": "A Gaia", "projectCode": "agaia", "subject": "Metadata: `search` field flag and GIN full-text index in the migration", "description": "<p>Global search runs unindexed <code>LIKE '%q%'</code> scans OR-ed across fields. We want Postgres full-text search, declared in model metadata and created by the database sync. This issue adds the metadata key and the index; the query operator is a separate issue.</p><ul><li>New optional field key in <code>app/metadata/model/&lt;Model&gt;.php</code>: <code>'search' =&gt; ['weight' =&gt; 'A'|'B'|'C'|'D']</code> (weight defaults to D). Do <strong>not</strong> reuse the legacy <code>fts</code> key (Elasticsearch era, set on far too many fields); leave it untouched.</li><li>One class (e.g. <code>Gaia\\\\Libs\\\\Search\\\\SearchVector</code> under <code>core/libs</code>) that reads a model's metadata and returns its searchable fields and the vector SQL expression, e.g. <code>setweight(to_tsvector('simple', coalesce(\\"subject\\", '')), 'A') || setweight(to_tsvector('simple', coalesce(\\"description\\", '')), 'B')</code>, cast non-text fields to text. The query operator issue reuses this class, so the index and the query use exactly the same expression.</li><li>Migration: for a model with searchable fields, create a GIN expression index <code>{table}_search</code> with <code>CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS ... USING GIN (&lt;expression&gt;)</code>, both for new tables (<code>core/libs/meta/migration.php</code> <code>migrateTable</code>) and existing ones (<code>core/mvc/db/migration/Postgres.php</code> next to <code>syncIndexes</code>). When the searchable field set or weights change, drop and recreate the index (compare against <code>pg_indexes.indexdef</code> or a hash in the index name). When no fields are searchable any more, drop it.</li><li>Use an expression index, not a stored tsvector column: <code>syncExistingTable</code> drops columns missing from metadata and the ORM would expose the column.</li><li>Dictionary <code>'simple'</code> (no stemming: works for code, ids and any language).</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> a model with <code>search</code> fields gets the GIN index on a fresh and on an existing database; re-running migrate is a no-op; changing a weight recreates the index; unit tests for the expression builder; existing migrations unaffected.</p><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> the query operator, marking real models searchable, pg_trgm/typo tolerance, removing the <code>fts</code> key.</p>", "type": "Task", "priority": "medium", "complexity": "complex", "dependsOn": [], "milestone": "Claude-v1", "newMilestone": false, "screens": []}, {"key": "search-operator", "project": "A Gaia", "projectCode": "agaia", "subject": "Query language: MATCHES operator for full-text search with relevance order", "description": "<p>Lets API clients search all searchable fields of a model at once with the GIN index from the metadata <code>search</code> key, instead of OR-ing <code>CONTAINS</code> clauses.</p><ul><li>New operator in <code>core/mvc/models/query/Clause.php</code> <code>prepareWhere</code> on the pseudo-field <code>&lt;Model&gt;.search</code>: <code>((Issue.search MATCHES 'logi bug'))</code>. Reject it with a clear error for models without searchable fields or on any other field.</li><li>Build the tsquery from user input: split on whitespace, drop tsquery special characters, each word becomes a prefix term joined with AND (<code>logi:* &amp; bug:*</code>), via <code>to_tsquery('simple', :param)</code>. The value <strong>must be a bound parameter</strong>, never concatenated (the existing operators concatenate; don't copy that).</li><li>Match with the exact vector expression from the <code>SearchVector</code> class of the index issue so the planner uses the <code>{table}_search</code> index. PHQL can't express <code>@@</code>: decide between an inlinable <code>LANGUAGE sql IMMUTABLE</code> wrapper function created through the metadata <code>functions</code> / <code>migrateFunctions</code> mechanism and another route, and verify with <code>EXPLAIN</code> that the GIN index is used.</li><li>Ordering: when a MATCHES clause is present and the request has no <code>sort</code>, order by <code>ts_rank(vector, query)</code> descending; an explicit <code>sort</code> wins.</li><li>Works together with other clauses (<code>AND</code>/<code>OR</code>), <code>rels</code>, paging and the existing ACL/project scoping, which must still apply.</li><li>Document the operator in the <code>Clause.php</code> docblock list.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> unit tests for tsquery building (special characters, empty input, quotes); EXPLAIN on a seeded table shows a bitmap index scan on the GIN index; results ordered by rank; injection attempts are inert.</p><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> typo tolerance, highlighting/snippets, fixing the concatenation in the existing operators.</p>", "type": "Task", "priority": "medium", "complexity": "complex", "dependsOn": ["search-index"], "milestone": "Claude-v1", "newMilestone": false, "screens": []}, {"key": "issue-searchable", "project": "A Gaia", "projectCode": "agaia", "subject": "Issues: make subject and description full-text searchable", "description": "<p>Apply the new metadata <code>search</code> key to Issue so the header global search can use <code>Issue.search MATCHES</code>.</p><ul><li><code>app/metadata/model/Issue.php</code>: <code>subject</code> gets <code>'search' =&gt; ['weight' =&gt; 'A']</code>, <code>description</code> gets <code>'search' =&gt; ['weight' =&gt; 'B']</code>. Leave the legacy <code>fts</code> keys alone.</li><li>Run migrate locally; the <code>issues_search</code> GIN index is created.</li><li><code>tools/api-tester</code> cases: <code>GET /issue?query=((Issue.search MATCHES 'word'))</code> finds by subject word, by description word and by word prefix; subject hits rank above description-only hits; a user only gets issues from projects they can access (ACL case).</li></ul><p><strong>Contract:</strong> new query capability Prometheus will use (<code>Issue.search MATCHES</code>); no change to the resource shape.</p><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> other models (projects, wiki, comments).</p>", "type": "Task", "priority": "medium", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": ["search-operator"], "milestone": "Claude-v1", "newMilestone": false, "screens": []}, {"key": "header-search", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "Header global search: use full-text MATCHES query", "description": "<p>The header search ORs three <code>CONTAINS</code> clauses (unindexed LIKE scans, no ranking). Gaia now supports <code>((Issue.search MATCHES 'q'))</code> over subject and description, with prefix matching and relevance order.</p><ul><li><code>app/components/app-layouts/application-header.js</code> <code>loadSearchData</code>: query <code>((Issue.search MATCHES 'q'))</code>; when the input is a whole number, OR it with <code>(Issue.issueNumber : q)</code> so issue numbers still work. Escape apostrophes in the input as the Gaia query language requires (<code>\\\\'</code>).</li><li>Don't send <code>sort</code>, so Gaia returns results by relevance. Keep the limit of 5, <code>rels</code>, the 500 ms debounce and result formatting as they are.</li><li>Update the Mirage handler for <code>GET /issue</code> to understand the MATCHES clause (simple substring match on subject/description is enough) and adjust the acceptance test of the global search.</li></ul><p><strong>Contract:</strong> depends on Gaia's new <code>Issue.search MATCHES</code> query operator; no resource change.</p><p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> typing part of a word finds issues by subject or description; typing an issue number finds that issue; the acceptance test passes.</p><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> searching other modules, a full results page, highlighting.</p>", "type": "Task", "priority": "medium", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": ["issue-searchable"], "milestone": "Claude-v1", "newMilestone": false, "screens": []}]}	proposed	\N	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-10-22 09:39:48	2026-10-22 09:39:48	f
c50905076ada-75e5-bcf0-0607a26912f8	4f0ce6006ad9-95f2-5f53-2d0fd54845df	1	{"summary": "One Prometheus issue: move the issue detail 'Issue Hierarchy' box into its own Issue::IssueHierarchy component, built from the existing issue overview row markup (issue-overview > issue-block > priority + data-block) used by the Gantt task list and the project's latest issues. It shows three levels (parent, current issue, children) as collapsed rows, and parent and child rows expand to show their description. The data is already loaded, so Gaia needs no change.", "issues": [{"key": "hierarchy-ui", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "Issue detail: hierarchy box with parent level and collapsible issue overview rows", "description": "<p>The Issue Hierarchy box on the issue detail view (<code>app/components/issue/issue-details.hbs</code>, the <code>issue-hierarchy</code> box near the end) shows every child issue with its full description. With 3-4 children it gets cluttered. It also shows only one level: the parent is missing, and the current issue only appears when it has children. This issue makes the rows compact and expandable, and shows parent, current issue and children as clearly distinct levels. It also covers aprom-3763's point that the current issue's description need not be repeated.</p><p><strong>Reuse the issue overview rows.</strong> Prometheus shows issues as 'issue overview' rows in several places: <code>ul.issue-overview</code> &gt; <code>li</code> &gt; <code>div.issue-block</code> with <code>div.priority</code> (<code>AppUi::PriorityIcon</code>) and <code>div.data-block</code> holding <code>span.subject</code>, optional <code>span.description</code> (<code>translate-comment</code>) and the status <code>span.badge</code> (<code>status-style</code>/<code>status-label</code>). See the Gantt task list (<code>components/app-ui/gantt-chart/task-row.hbs</code>), the project's latest issues (<code>templates/app/project/index.hbs</code> ~l.130) and the issue list (<code>templates/app/project/issue/index.hbs</code> ~l.123); styles in <code>styles/modules/project.scss</code> (<code>.issue-overview</code>). Use exactly this markup and these classes; don't create new row markup.</p><p><strong>Changes</strong></p><ul><li>New Glimmer component <code>app/components/issue/issue-hierarchy.{hbs,js}</code> (<code>Issue::IssueHierarchy</code>, argument <code>@issue</code>). Move the hierarchy box out of <code>issue-details.hbs</code> into it and render <code>&lt;Issue::IssueHierarchy @issue={{this.issue}} /&gt;</code> in its place. Keep the <code>AccessControl @aclContext=\\"issue.read\\"</code> wrapper and the <code>box box-info issue-hierarchy</code> box with its existing title.</li><li>Three levels in one <code>ul.issue-overview</code>, each under a small grey caption <code>li</code> (translations in <code>translations/en-us.json</code>: 'Parent', 'This issue', 'Child issues (n)'):<ul><li><strong>Parent</strong> (<code>issue.parentissue</code>), only when <code>issue.parentId</code> is set. No indent, left bar in <code>$highlightColor</code>.</li><li><strong>This issue</strong>: the existing <code>li.current-issue</code> (crimson left bar). Never expandable, and no description shown, because the page already shows it.</li><li><strong>Children</strong> (<code>issue.childissues</code>): the existing <code>li.child-issue</code> (indent and amber bar), indented one step deeper than the parent row.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Collapsed row</strong> = the Gantt-list form: priority icon, then the <code>data-block</code> with subject (<code>LinkTo app.project.issue.page</code> with <code>(array projectShortcode issueNumber)</code>, as today, then ' - subject') and the status badge, with no description. <strong>Expanded row</strong> = the latest-issues form: the same plus <code>span.description</code> between subject and badge.</li><li>Parent and child rows start collapsed. A <code>btn btn-box-tool</code> chevron on the right of the row (<code>fa-chevron-right</code>, or <code>fa-chevron-down</code> when expanded), where the Gantt row has its eye button, toggles that row. Expanded state is per row, kept in the component (<code>@tracked</code>), and resets on navigation.</li><li>The box shows whenever the issue has a parent or children. The existing <code>Exceptions::NothingFound</code> empty state ('No child issue found for this issue') shows only when it has neither.</li><li>Styles in <code>app/styles/modules/issue.scss</code>, nested under <code>.issue-hierarchy</code>: the parent bar, the child indent relative to the parent, the caption, and the chevron position. Let the box size to its content by overriding, for this box only, the fixed <code>min-height/max-height: 35rem</code> that <code>.issue-overview</code> gets from <code>modules/project.scss</code>. Don't change the shared <code>.issue-overview</code> rules used by the project overview, Gantt and list. Square edges, colours only from <code>variables.scss</code>.</li><li>No data changes: <code>routes/app/project/issue/page.js</code>, <code>controllers/app/project/issue/index.js</code> and <code>controllers/app/project/board.js</code> already load <code>parentissue</code> and <code>childissues</code>. Check that the list and board quick views (which render <code>issue-details</code>) show the new box correctly.</li><li>YUIDoc on the component; keep the license header.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance criteria</strong></p><ul><li>An issue with a parent and 3 children shows the Parent caption with one collapsed row, This issue with the current issue (no chevron, no description), and Child issues (3) with three collapsed rows, each level visually distinct by indent and bar.</li><li>Rows look like the Gantt task list rows when collapsed and like the project's latest issues rows when expanded.</li><li>Clicking a chevron expands only that row. Clicking again collapses it. Issue numbers link to the issue page.</li><li>Only a parent: parent and current issue, no empty message. Neither: the existing empty state.</li><li>The project overview, Gantt and issue list look unchanged.</li><li>Integration test for <code>Issue::IssueHierarchy</code> covering: parent and children; only children; only parent; neither; expand/collapse. Lint and the test suite pass.</li></ul><p><strong>Out of scope</strong></p><ul><li>Extracting the issue overview row into a shared component and migrating Gantt, project overview and issue list to it.</li><li>Grandchildren and parents further up.</li><li>Expand-all.</li><li>Assignee or type in the expanded row.</li><li>Moving the box to the main column (aprom-3763).</li><li>Any Gaia change.</li></ul>", "type": "Task", "priority": "medium", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": [], "milestone": "Claude-v1", "newMilestone": false, "screens": ["issue-hierarchy"]}]}	proposed	\N	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-10-22 13:04:05	2026-10-22 13:04:05	f
489fa7356adc-7056-a2f4-07694c205ee9	e5bbc5d16adc-d3fe-2863-120be2b9005e	3	{"summary": "Four Prometheus issues for the idea pages, all in the Ideas milestone. The message composer gets the toastui rich text editor that issue comments use, and it keeps producing markdown through a new opt-in output option on the editor modifier. The create page then drops 'How it works' and the nested columns so the form fills the content area, and its description (the idea's first message) uses the same full editor. Separately, the shared bootstrap-modal learns to wait for an async confirm: spinner and disabled buttons while it runs, closes on success, stays open on failure. The plan box's approve flow uses it and keeps Approve disabled from the click until the save succeeds or fails. The editor issue and the modal issue can go in parallel; each has one follow-up.", "issues": [{"key": "composer-editor", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "Ideas: rich text editor with toolbar for the message composer", "description": "<p>The idea message composer is a 2-row plain textarea, which is too small for real messages and has no formatting. Give it the same editor issue comments use. Gaia's <code>Ideamessage.body</code> is already an unlimited <code>text</code> column, so this is UI only.</p><ul><li><code>components/idea/composer.{hbs,js}</code>: replace <code>FormFields::FieldTextArea</code> with <code>AppUi::ToastUi</code> (<code>@editType='wysiwyg'</code>, toolbar shown, <code>@hide={{array 'footer'}}</code>), as in <code>components/conversation-ui/message-box.hbs</code>. Keep the Send button (<code>AppUi::Button</code>, <code>@aclContext=\\"ideamessage.create\\"</code>). <strong>Ctrl+Enter or Send</strong> sends, as in issue comments (<code>@onKeyboardSubmit</code>). Plain Enter adds a new line. While sending or when the idea is archived, the composer is disabled. After a successful send the editor is cleared (the modifier resets on <code>clearContents</code> from its pubSub; see how comments clear). On failure the text is kept.</li><li><strong>Messages stay markdown.</strong> The thread renders bodies with <code>modifiers/render-markdown.js</code>, and the planning agent reads and writes markdown. <code>modifiers/initialize-toastui.js</code> only emits <code>editor.getHtml()</code> (change event ~l.189 and keyboard submit ~l.239). Add an optional named argument (e.g. <code>output='markdown'</code>) that emits <code>editor.getMarkdown()</code> instead, passed through by <code>components/app-ui/toast-ui.hbs</code>. The default stays HTML, so the other editors don't change. The create page (separate issue) will use this option too.</li><li>No user mentions or issue search for now: pass no <code>usersList</code>/<code>issueSearch</code>, and make sure the editor and its Tribute setup work without them.</li><li>The editor's height should be comfortable for a few paragraphs, like the issue comment box. Square edges per <code>docs/ui/design-system.md</code>.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> a message with headings, a list, bold text and a code block is sent from the toolbar and renders formatted in the thread. The stored body is markdown. Ctrl+Enter and the Send button both send. The editor clears after a successful send and keeps the text on a failure. An archived idea's composer is disabled. Other ToastUi editors still emit HTML. Integration tests for the composer, and the markdown option covered.</p><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> mentions, issue links, attachments, editing sent messages, the create page.</p>", "type": "Task", "priority": "medium", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": [], "milestone": "Ideas", "newMilestone": false, "screens": []}, {"key": "create-page", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "Ideas: create page fills the content column, no 'How it works', full editor for the first message", "description": "<p>The Start an Idea page wastes space and treats the first message as a small field. The ideas layout (<code>templates/app/project/idea.hbs</code>) already renders each page in a <code>col-md-9</code> content column. <code>templates/app/project/idea/create.hbs</code> nests another <code>.row</code> with a <code>col-md-8</code> form and a <code>col-md-4</code> 'How it works' box inside it, which leaves empty space on both sides of the form. The description becomes the idea's first message (<code>controllers/app/project/idea/create.js</code> <code>afterSave</code>), so it needs the full editor, not a small field.</p><ul><li><code>create.hbs</code>: remove the 'How it works' box and the nested <code>.row</code>/<code>col-md-8</code>/<code>col-md-4</code>. The form box fills the content column, like the 'Brainstorm an idea' box in <code>templates/app/project/idea/index.hbs</code>. Start/Cancel stay below the box. Keep <code>AccessControl @aclContext=\\"idea.create\\"</code>.</li><li>Remove the <code>views.app.idea.create.how.*</code> strings from <code>translations/en-us.json</code>.</li><li>Description (<code>idea.description</code>): replace <code>FormFields::FieldTextArea</code> with <code>AppUi::ToastUi</code> (wysiwyg, toolbar, footer hidden), using the markdown output option added by the composer issue. No mentions or issue search. Wrap it in <code>div.form-field</code> with <code>data-field=\\"idea.description\\"</code>, as in <code>templates/app/project/issue/create.hbs</code>. Give it a height for a real first message, similar to the issue description editor. The value saved to <code>idea.description</code> and the first message is markdown.</li><li>Compare against the issue create page in the running app before calling it done.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> no 'How it works' box. The form box spans the whole content column next to the ideas list, with no empty columns beside it. The description uses the toolbar editor, and its markdown becomes the first message, rendered formatted in the thread. Title validation still works. Existing idea create tests are updated.</p><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> the idea page boxes, mentions.</p>", "type": "Task", "priority": "medium", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": ["composer-editor"], "milestone": "Ideas", "newMilestone": false, "screens": []}, {"key": "modal-async-confirm", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "bootstrap-modal: wait for an async confirm, with loading state, and close on success", "description": "<p>Confirm modals can't show that work is running, and some don't close after confirming. In <code>templates/components/bootstrap-modal.hbs</code> the confirm button calls the passed-in <code>confirm</code> directly (<code>@onClick={{this.confirm}}</code>). The component's own <code>actions.confirm</code> in <code>components/bootstrap-modal.js</code>, which hides the modal when <code>confirm()</code> returns true, never runs. So callers that return <code>true</code> to close (e.g. <code>components/idea/plan-box.js</code> <code>confirmApprove</code>, after aprom-4103) stay open. Check this in the browser first.</p><ul><li>Route the confirm button through the component's own confirm handler.</li><li>If <code>confirm()</code> returns a Promise: mark the modal busy, which disables Confirm and Cancel and shows a <code>fa fa-spinner fa-spin</code> on Confirm. Ignore repeated clicks. Hide the modal as soon as it resolves truthy. When it rejects or resolves falsy, clear the busy state and keep the modal open.</li><li>Synchronous returns behave as documented: <code>true</code> hides, anything else leaves it open. Check the ~31 existing usages for any that relied on the old behaviour (e.g. a caller that closes itself and would now be hidden twice, or returns true but expects to stay open) and adjust them in this PR.</li><li>The close path still goes through <code>hidden.bs.modal</code> -&gt; <code>close</code>, so no backdrop is left behind (aprom-4103). No new jQuery outside this component. Remove the stray backtick after <code>modal-footer</code> in the template.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> a modal whose confirm returns a Promise shows the spinner, ignores double clicks, closes on resolve and stays open (buttons enabled again) on reject. A sync-true modal closes. A sync-false modal stays open. The idea archive modal closes after confirming. Integration test for the component covers all four cases. Full suite passes.</p><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> rewriting bootstrap-modal as a Glimmer component.</p>", "type": "Bug", "priority": "high", "complexity": "complex", "dependsOn": [], "milestone": "Ideas", "newMilestone": false, "screens": []}, {"key": "approve-state", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "Ideas: approve plan shows loading, closes once saved and keeps Approve disabled unless it fails", "description": "<p>Approving a plan gives no feedback: the modal shows no loading or completion and doesn't close, and the Approve button can be pressed again. Use the async confirm of <code>bootstrap-modal</code> and track the approval in the plan box.</p><ul><li><code>components/idea/plan-box.js</code>: a tracked <code>isApproving</code>. Once the user confirms, <code>confirmApprove</code> sets it and returns the Promise of <code>this.args.onApprove(plan)</code>. bootstrap-modal shows the spinner with disabled buttons and closes <strong>as soon as the approval is saved</strong>. It doesn't wait for the issues to be created. On rejection the controller (<code>controllers/app/project/idea/page.js</code> <code>approvePlan</code>) has already reported the error and rolled back. The modal stays open with its buttons back, and <code>isApproving</code> is cleared.</li><li>The footer Approve button (<code>data-idea-plan-approve</code>) is disabled and shows a spinner from the confirmation until the save ends. It is enabled again only if the approval fails. On success the plan becomes <code>approved</code> and <code>canApprove</code> hides the button. A plan that later turns <code>failed</code> (Talos) shows it again, as today.</li><li>Status label: while the selected plan is <code>approved</code> or <code>creating</code>, show <code>fa fa-spinner fa-spin</code> before the status text, so the user sees that issues are being created after the modal closes. The live <code>idea.plan.status.changed</code> reload already moves it to <code>created</code>/<code>failed</code>.</li><li>aprom-4105 moves the status label into <code>box-tools</code>. Whichever lands second keeps both changes.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> after confirming, the modal shows a spinner with disabled buttons and Approve is disabled. When the save succeeds, the modal closes, the button is gone and the label shows Approved with a spinner, then Created. On a failed save the modal stays open, the error is shown and Approve works again. Double clicks create one approval. Integration tests for the plan box cover success and failure.</p><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> live Talos progress inside the modal, cancelling an approval.</p>", "type": "Bug", "priority": "high", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": ["modal-async-confirm"], "milestone": "Ideas", "newMilestone": false, "screens": []}]}	proposed	\N	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-10-24 06:59:20	2026-10-24 06:59:20	f
fb36b5f06ada-2ec4-1fa7-05d2464e55e6	afa54b056ada-2ec4-1fa7-d7b41cc59b57	1	{"summary": "One Prometheus issue: move the assignee dropdown and its save logic out of Issue::IssueDetails into a shared Issue::AssigneeSelect component, keep the issue detail view working as it does now, and use the component on task board cards so clicking the assignee avatar opens the member list and reassigns the issue in place. No Gaia or Hermes changes.", "issues": [{"key": "board-card-assignee", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "Task board: change the assignee from the issue card (shared Issue::AssigneeSelect)", "description": "<p>On the task board the card only shows the assignee's avatar. To reassign an issue, users have to open the quick view and use the dropdown in the issue details. The same dropdown should work directly on the card. The dropdown exists only inside <code>Issue::IssueDetails</code> (aprom-3681 built it there), so this issue moves it into a shared component used in both places.</p><p><strong>Changes</strong></p><ul><li>New Glimmer component <code>app/components/issue/assignee-select.{js,hbs}</code> with one job: show an issue's assignee and let the user pick another project member. Args: <code>@issue</code>, <code>@members</code> (the project members), <code>@compact</code> (avatar-only trigger for cards) and an optional <code>@onUpdated(issue)</code> callback.</li><li>Move the following from <code>components/issue/issue-details.js</code> (~l.1191-1279) into the new component: <code>availableAssignees</code> (active members other than the current assignee), <code>handleAssigneeOptionKeydown</code> and <code>updateAssignee</code> (sets <code>assignedTo</code>/<code>assignee</code>, <code>issue.save()</code>, Messenger toasts with the existing <code>views.app.issue.detail.assignee*</code> strings, <code>rollbackAttributes()</code> on failure). Keep the markup from <code>issue-details.hbs</code> ~l.283-307 (<code>dropdown</code>, <code>dropdown-menu</code>, the <code>data-issue-assignee*</code> attributes), and move its inline <code>style</code> on the option avatar into a scss class.</li><li><code>issue-details.hbs</code> uses the component in place of the inline dropdown and passes <code>projectController.model.members</code> plus the existing <code>postUpdateAssignee</code> as <code>@onUpdated</code>. Its behaviour and tests stay unchanged.</li><li><code>components/app-ui/task-board/milestones.hbs</code> (~l.125, <code>.issue-card-assignee</code>): the avatar becomes the compact trigger, using <code>@membersList</code>, which the board already passes in. Open the menu with <code>dropdown-menu-right</code> so it stays inside the lane, and show the member's name on hover as today.</li><li>Clicking or using the keyboard on the trigger or the menu must not open the quick view and must not start a Sortable drag. Stop the event from propagating, and/or add the element to Sortable's <code>filter</code>/<code>preventOnFilter</code>.</li><li>After a reassignment the card updates in place and stays draggable. Do not re-render the whole card (see aprom-3700). If the reassigned issue is open in the quick view, the quick view shows the new assignee too, since both read the same model.</li><li>Styles go under the board and issue modules in <code>app/styles/modules/</code>. Follow <code>docs/ui/design-system.md</code>: square menu edges, 50% radius only on avatars.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance criteria</strong></p><ul><li>On the task board, clicking a card's assignee avatar opens a list of active project members (avatar + name), without the current assignee. Choosing one saves the issue, shows the existing updating, updated and failed toasts, and updates the avatar on the card.</li><li>Opening the menu or choosing a member does not open the quick view or move the card. The card can still be dragged afterwards.</li><li>If the save fails, the card goes back to the old assignee.</li><li>The issue detail view (page and quick view) behaves exactly as before, through the shared component.</li><li>Tests: an integration test for <code>Issue::AssigneeSelect</code> (lists members, excludes the current assignee, saves, rolls back on error), and an acceptance scenario on the board (Mirage) that reassigns from the card. Existing issue-detail assignee tests still pass.</li></ul><p><strong>Out of scope</strong>: changing status, priority or estimate from the card; unassigning; searching or filtering the member list; live updates from other users (aprom-3978).</p>", "type": "Task", "priority": "medium", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": [], "milestone": "Claude-v1", "newMilestone": false, "screens": []}]}	created	\N	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-10-22 14:47:23	2026-10-22 14:49:15	f
729b95e76adb-bb24-3884-828379070346	dc9f28086adb-14fc-3884-5d3000a5198e	1	{"summary": "One Prometheus issue that redesigns the grouped notifications sidebar from aprom-4063. Groups become an always-open section: a heading with the issue key (linking to the issue), then all of that issue's notifications, newest first, shown with the same item markup and styles as other notifications. Groups and standalone notifications stay ordered by their newest notification. It builds on the 4063 grouping util and service getter, so it depends on aprom-4063.", "issues": [{"key": "group-view", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "Notifications sidebar: show issue groups as an always-open heading with all notifications listed", "description": "<p>aprom-4063 groups notifications by issue, but each group is a collapsed toggle header showing only the newest notification's text and counts. Users have to expand it to see anything, and expanded items lose the normal item styling. The engineer wants each group to be a plain heading with the issue key and every notification of that issue listed under it, always visible.</p><p><strong>Changes</strong></p><ul><li><code>app/components/app-ui/notifications/group.{hbs,js}</code>: remove the collapse. No caret, <code>isExpanded</code>, <code>toggle</code>/<code>onKeyDown</code>, <code>role=\\"button\\"</code>/<code>tabindex</code>/<code>aria-expanded</code>, newest-text preview, update count or unread count. Render <code>li.notification-group</code> with a heading <code>h5.notification-group-heading</code> holding the issue key (<code>SHORTCODE-number</code>, upper case, <code>fa fa-ticket</code> icon). The key is a link to the issue page (same route/link the notification item uses for issue notifications in <code>notification.js</code>). Following the heading link must not mark anything read. Under the heading, list every notification of the group with the existing <code>AppUi::Notifications::Notification</code>, unchanged, newest first.</li><li>Render the heading for every issue group, including groups with a single notification (remove the <code>length 1</code> shortcut that renders a plain item).</li><li>Fix item styling inside groups: today items sit in <code>ul.notification-group-items</code>, which the <code>.notifications-list &gt; li &gt; a</code> rules in <code>app/styles/components/notifications-sidebar.scss</code> don't match, so grouped items lose their padding and flex layout. Grouped items must look exactly like ungrouped ones (e.g. give the inner list the <code>notifications-list</code> class or widen the selector) with no left indent.</li><li>Styles in <code>notifications-sidebar.scss</code>: replace <code>.notification-group-header</code>/<code>.notification-group-caret</code> with <code>.notification-group-heading</code>: a small padded heading with a 1px divider above it (use the existing <code>rgba(210, 214, 222, …)</code> border colour), key in bold link blue (<code>$secondaryColor</code>), square edges, no new colours or shadows (<code>docs/ui/design-system.md</code>). The heading has no unread style; unread stays on the items.</li><li>Order: keep <code>utils/notifications/group-by-issue.js</code> as is. Entries (groups and standalone notifications) sort by their newest notification, newest first, and items inside a group sort newest first. A new notification for an issue whose group sits lower moves that group to the top. Remove anything from the util or service that only the collapsed header used (e.g. unread counts), along with the translation keys <code>views.app.notifications.updateCount</code>/<code>unreadCount</code> if unused.</li><li>Tests: update <code>tests/integration/components/app-ui/notifications/</code> group tests (heading shown, all items rendered without interaction, single-item group has a heading, heading link doesn't mark read) and the acceptance features under <code>tests/acceptance/notification/</code>. Add one unit or acceptance case for the order: issue A's latest notification is 3 minutes old and its oldest is yesterday, issue B's latest is 1 hour old, so A comes before B.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance criteria</strong></p><ul><li>Opening the sidebar shows, per issue, a heading with the issue key followed by all its loaded notifications, with no click needed.</li><li>Groups and standalone notifications are ordered by their most recent notification, newest first, including after a live <code>notification.created</code> push and after loading more pages.</li><li>Grouped items look identical to ungrouped items (avatar, text, timestamp, unread marker).</li><li>Clicking an item still marks it read and navigates as before. Clicking the heading only navigates to the issue.</li><li>Lint and the full Prometheus suite pass. Compare in the running app with the screen attached.</li></ul><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> issue subject in the heading (not in the notification context; needs Gaia), per-group mark-as-read, collapsing, and changes to how the service loads or pages notifications.</p>", "type": "Task", "priority": "medium", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": ["aprom-4063"], "milestone": "Claude-v1", "newMilestone": false, "screens": ["grouped-sidebar"]}]}	proposed	\N	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-10-23 17:59:14	2026-10-23 17:59:14	f
6c4574556af4-c968-1af5-08efbfcd11a7	e66181d96af4-7c69-1af5-a7b779ce5171	2	{"summary": "Paginate the Project Milestones box on the project dashboard in the browser, 5 per page, using the shared Pagination component. The route already loads every milestone and the chart filter needs them all, so there is no Gaia change. One Prometheus issue, in milestone aga-v2.", "issues": [{"key": "milestones-pagination", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "Project dashboard: paginate the Project Milestones box (5 per page)", "description": "<p>The Project Milestones box on the project dashboard (<code>app/templates/app/project/index.hbs</code>, <code>project-milestones-section</code>, in the col-md-4 side column) lists every milestone of the project. On projects with many milestones the box runs far down the page. Paginate it in the browser using the existing shared <code>Pagination</code> component.</p><p><strong>Changes</strong></p><ul><li>The route <code>routes/app/project/index.js</code> keeps loading all milestones (<code>limit: -1</code>, sort <code>Milestone.startDate</code> DESC). The estimated-vs-spent chart filter (<code>milestonesByDateCreatedDesc</code>) and the per-milestone issue counts need the full list. No API change.</li><li>In <code>controllers/app/project/index.js</code>, track the current milestone page and add a getter for the visible slice (page size 5, in the list's existing order). Add an <code>onPageChange({page, pageSize})</code> action that sets the page and returns <code>{ hasMore }</code>. The getter must react to the <code>milestones</code> list, so milestones added through <code>saveMilestone</code> or edited show up without a reload.</li><li>In the milestones <code>box-body</code>, wrap the <code>{{#each}}</code> of <code>Project::Milestones</code> in <code>&lt;Pagination @pageSize={{5}} @onPageChange=... @containerClass=\\"project-milestones-pagination\\"&gt;</code>. Use the <code>&lt;:center&gt;</code> block to show <code>first – last / total</code> (e.g. <code>1 – 5 / 14</code>) from a translation in <code>translations/en-us.json</code>.</li><li>Render the pager only when there are more than 5 milestones. Keep the <code>Exceptions::NothingFound</code> empty state as it is.</li><li>After adding a milestone, the total and the › button update. If the current page becomes empty (should not happen today; there is no delete), fall back to the last page.</li><li>Only add styles if needed, scoped under <code>.project-milestones-section</code> in <code>styles/modules/project.scss</code>, and follow the design system (square edges, existing colours).</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance criteria</strong></p><ul><li>With 14 milestones the box shows the first 5, the footer reads <code>1 – 5 / 14</code>, ‹ is disabled, and › moves to <code>6 – 10 / 14</code>. The last page shows <code>11 – 14 / 14</code> with › disabled.</li><li>With 5 or fewer milestones there is no pager. With none, the existing empty state shows.</li><li>Adding a milestone through the + dialog updates the total right away. The edit pencil and the milestone links still work on every page.</li><li>The estimated-vs-spent chart filter still lists all milestones.</li><li>Tests: an integration or acceptance test (Mirage, 14 milestones) covering page 1, next, last page and no pager at ≤5. Each test fails if the slicing is reverted.</li></ul><p><strong>Out of scope</strong>: server-side paging of milestones, a page-size selector, filtering by status, and reducing the full milestone/issue load on this route.</p>", "type": "Task", "priority": "medium", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": [], "milestone": "aga-v2", "newMilestone": false, "estimatedFiles": 5, "sizeReason": "", "screens": ["milestones-paginated"]}]}	created	\N	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-11-11 10:03:24	2026-11-11 10:05:18	f
703add716adb-4599-3884-192e16f9309a	dc9f28086adb-14fc-3884-5d3000a5198e	2	{"summary": "One Prometheus issue that redesigns the grouped notifications sidebar from aprom-4063. Every issue group, even one with a single notification, becomes an always-open section: a heading with the issue key (built from the same context values as the issue link, and linking to the issue), then all of that issue's notifications, newest first, shown with the same item markup and styles as other notifications. Groups and standalone notifications stay ordered by their newest notification. It builds on the 4063 grouping util and service getter, so it depends on aprom-4063.", "issues": [{"key": "group-view", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "Notifications sidebar: show issue groups as an always-open heading with all notifications listed", "description": "<p>aprom-4063 groups notifications by issue, but each group is a collapsed toggle header showing only the newest notification's text and counts. Users have to expand it to see anything, and expanded items lose the normal item styling. The engineer wants each group to be a plain heading with the issue key and every notification of that issue listed under it, always visible.</p><p><strong>Changes</strong></p><ul><li><code>app/components/app-ui/notifications/group.{hbs,js}</code>: remove the collapse. No caret, <code>isExpanded</code>, <code>toggle</code>/<code>onKeyDown</code>, <code>role=\\"button\\"</code>/<code>tabindex</code>/<code>aria-expanded</code>, newest-text preview, update count or unread count. Render <code>li.notification-group</code> with a heading <code>h5.notification-group-heading</code> holding only the issue key (<code>SHORTCODE-number</code>, upper case, <code>fa fa-ticket</code> icon). Take the key from the same values the issue link is built from: <code>context.projectShortcode</code> + <code>context.issueNumber</code>, already on the group entry as <code>shortcode</code>/<code>issueNumber</code>. Make the key a link to the issue page, using the same route/link the notification item builds for issue notifications in <code>notification.js</code>; reuse that logic rather than duplicating it. Following the heading link must not mark anything read. Under the heading, list every notification of the group with the existing <code>AppUi::Notifications::Notification</code>, unchanged, newest first.</li><li>Render the heading for every issue group, including groups with a single notification (remove the <code>length 1</code> shortcut that renders a plain item).</li><li>Fix item styling inside groups: today items sit in <code>ul.notification-group-items</code>, which the <code>.notifications-list &gt; li &gt; a</code> rules in <code>app/styles/components/notifications-sidebar.scss</code> don't match, so grouped items lose their padding and flex layout. Grouped items must look exactly like ungrouped ones (e.g. give the inner list the <code>notifications-list</code> class or widen the selector) with no left indent.</li><li>Styles in <code>notifications-sidebar.scss</code>: replace <code>.notification-group-header</code>/<code>.notification-group-caret</code> with <code>.notification-group-heading</code>: a small padded heading with a 1px divider above it (use the existing <code>rgba(210, 214, 222, …)</code> border colour), key in bold link blue (<code>$secondaryColor</code>), square edges, no new colours or shadows (<code>docs/ui/design-system.md</code>). The heading has no unread style; unread stays on the items.</li><li>Order: keep <code>utils/notifications/group-by-issue.js</code> as is. Entries (groups and standalone notifications) sort by their newest notification, newest first, and items inside a group sort newest first. A new notification for an issue whose group sits lower moves that group to the top. Remove anything from the component that only the collapsed header used (e.g. unread count), along with the translation keys <code>views.app.notifications.updateCount</code>/<code>unreadCount</code> if unused.</li><li>Tests: update <code>tests/integration/components/app-ui/notifications/</code> group tests (heading with the key shown, all items rendered without interaction, single-item group has a heading, heading links to the issue and doesn't mark read) and the acceptance features under <code>tests/acceptance/notification/</code>. Add one unit or acceptance case for the order: issue A's latest notification is 3 minutes old and its oldest is yesterday, issue B's latest is 1 hour old, so A comes before B.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance criteria</strong></p><ul><li>Opening the sidebar shows, for every issue (even one with a single notification), a heading with the issue key followed by all its loaded notifications, with no click needed.</li><li>Groups and standalone notifications are ordered by their most recent notification, newest first, including after a live <code>notification.created</code> push and after loading more pages.</li><li>Grouped items look identical to ungrouped items (avatar, text, timestamp, unread marker).</li><li>Clicking an item still marks it read and navigates as before. Clicking the heading only navigates to the issue.</li><li>Lint and the full Prometheus suite pass. Compare in the running app with the screen attached.</li></ul><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> issue subject in the heading (not in the notification context; needs Gaia, later), per-group mark-as-read, collapsing, and changes to how the service loads or pages notifications.</p>", "type": "Task", "priority": "medium", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": ["aprom-4063"], "milestone": "Claude-v1", "newMilestone": false, "screens": ["grouped-sidebar"]}]}	created	\N	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-10-23 18:03:28	2026-10-23 18:05:13	f
3f5302576adc-9a36-a2f4-9f08acf2baea	e5bbc5d16adc-d3fe-2863-120be2b9005e	4	{"summary": "Six issues for the idea pages, all in the Ideas milestone. Editor: the message composer gets the toastui rich text editor that issue comments use, producing markdown through a new opt-in output option; the create page then drops 'How it works' and its nested columns and uses the same editor for the first message. Approval: the shared bootstrap-modal learns to wait for an async confirm (spinner, disabled buttons, closes on success, stays open on failure), and the plan box uses it, keeping Approve disabled until the save succeeds or fails. Inline images, after the upload download fix agaia-4106: Prometheus uploads images pasted into the composer as uploads of the idea and renders `![name](upload:<id>)` references, and Talos downloads the referenced images so Nestor can open them. The editor and modal chains can go in parallel; the image issues wait for agaia-4106 (the Prometheus one also for the composer editor).", "issues": [{"key": "composer-editor", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "Ideas: rich text editor with toolbar for the message composer", "description": "<p>The idea message composer is a 2-row plain textarea, which is too small for real messages and has no formatting. Give it the same editor issue comments use. Gaia's <code>Ideamessage.body</code> is already an unlimited <code>text</code> column, so this is UI only.</p><ul><li><code>components/idea/composer.{hbs,js}</code>: replace <code>FormFields::FieldTextArea</code> with <code>AppUi::ToastUi</code> (<code>@editType='wysiwyg'</code>, toolbar shown, <code>@hide={{array 'footer'}}</code>), as in <code>components/conversation-ui/message-box.hbs</code>. Keep the Send button (<code>AppUi::Button</code>, <code>@aclContext=\\"ideamessage.create\\"</code>). <strong>Ctrl+Enter or Send</strong> sends, as in issue comments (<code>@onKeyboardSubmit</code>). Plain Enter adds a new line. While sending or when the idea is archived, the composer is disabled. After a successful send the editor is cleared (the modifier resets on <code>clearContents</code> from its pubSub; see how comments clear). On failure the text is kept.</li><li><strong>Messages stay markdown.</strong> The thread renders bodies with <code>modifiers/render-markdown.js</code>, and the planning agent reads and writes markdown. <code>modifiers/initialize-toastui.js</code> only emits <code>editor.getHtml()</code> (change event ~l.189 and keyboard submit ~l.239). Add an optional named argument (e.g. <code>output='markdown'</code>) that emits <code>editor.getMarkdown()</code> instead, passed through by <code>components/app-ui/toast-ui.hbs</code>. The default stays HTML, so the other editors don't change. The create page and inline images (separate issues) build on this.</li><li>No user mentions or issue search for now: pass no <code>usersList</code>/<code>issueSearch</code>, and make sure the editor and its Tribute setup work without them.</li><li>The editor's height should be comfortable for a few paragraphs, like the issue comment box. Square edges per <code>docs/ui/design-system.md</code>.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> a message with headings, a list, bold text and a code block is sent from the toolbar and renders formatted in the thread. The stored body is markdown. Ctrl+Enter and the Send button both send. The editor clears after a successful send and keeps the text on a failure. An archived idea's composer is disabled. Other ToastUi editors still emit HTML. Integration tests for the composer, and the markdown option covered.</p><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> mentions, issue links, images (separate issue), editing sent messages, the create page.</p>", "type": "Task", "priority": "medium", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": [], "milestone": "Ideas", "newMilestone": false, "screens": []}, {"key": "create-page", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "Ideas: create page fills the content column, no 'How it works', full editor for the first message", "description": "<p>The Start an Idea page wastes space and treats the first message as a small field. The ideas layout (<code>templates/app/project/idea.hbs</code>) already renders each page in a <code>col-md-9</code> content column. <code>templates/app/project/idea/create.hbs</code> nests another <code>.row</code> with a <code>col-md-8</code> form and a <code>col-md-4</code> 'How it works' box inside it, which leaves empty space on both sides of the form. The description becomes the idea's first message (<code>controllers/app/project/idea/create.js</code> <code>afterSave</code>), so it needs the full editor, not a small field.</p><ul><li><code>create.hbs</code>: remove the 'How it works' box and the nested <code>.row</code>/<code>col-md-8</code>/<code>col-md-4</code>. The form box fills the content column, like the 'Brainstorm an idea' box in <code>templates/app/project/idea/index.hbs</code>. Start/Cancel stay below the box. Keep <code>AccessControl @aclContext=\\"idea.create\\"</code>.</li><li>Remove the <code>views.app.idea.create.how.*</code> strings from <code>translations/en-us.json</code>.</li><li>Description (<code>idea.description</code>): replace <code>FormFields::FieldTextArea</code> with <code>AppUi::ToastUi</code> (wysiwyg, toolbar, footer hidden), using the markdown output option added by the composer issue. No mentions or issue search. Wrap it in <code>div.form-field</code> with <code>data-field=\\"idea.description\\"</code>, as in <code>templates/app/project/issue/create.hbs</code>. Give it a height for a real first message, similar to the issue description editor. The value saved to <code>idea.description</code> and the first message is markdown.</li><li>Compare against the issue create page in the running app before calling it done.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> no 'How it works' box. The form box spans the whole content column next to the ideas list, with no empty columns beside it. The description uses the toolbar editor, and its markdown becomes the first message, rendered formatted in the thread. Title validation still works. Existing idea create tests are updated.</p><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> images in the description (the idea doesn't exist yet when it is written), the idea page boxes, mentions.</p>", "type": "Task", "priority": "medium", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": ["composer-editor"], "milestone": "Ideas", "newMilestone": false, "screens": []}, {"key": "modal-async-confirm", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "bootstrap-modal: wait for an async confirm, with loading state, and close on success", "description": "<p>Confirm modals can't show that work is running, and some don't close after confirming. In <code>templates/components/bootstrap-modal.hbs</code> the confirm button calls the passed-in <code>confirm</code> directly (<code>@onClick={{this.confirm}}</code>). The component's own <code>actions.confirm</code> in <code>components/bootstrap-modal.js</code>, which hides the modal when <code>confirm()</code> returns true, never runs. So callers that return <code>true</code> to close (e.g. <code>components/idea/plan-box.js</code> <code>confirmApprove</code>, after aprom-4103) stay open. Check this in the browser first.</p><ul><li>Route the confirm button through the component's own confirm handler.</li><li>If <code>confirm()</code> returns a Promise: mark the modal busy, which disables Confirm and Cancel and shows a <code>fa fa-spinner fa-spin</code> on Confirm. Ignore repeated clicks. Hide the modal as soon as it resolves truthy. When it rejects or resolves falsy, clear the busy state and keep the modal open.</li><li>Synchronous returns behave as documented: <code>true</code> hides, anything else leaves it open. Check the ~31 existing usages for any that relied on the old behaviour (e.g. a caller that closes itself and would now be hidden twice, or returns true but expects to stay open) and adjust them in this PR.</li><li>The close path still goes through <code>hidden.bs.modal</code> -&gt; <code>close</code>, so no backdrop is left behind (aprom-4103). No new jQuery outside this component. Remove the stray backtick after <code>modal-footer</code> in the template.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> a modal whose confirm returns a Promise shows the spinner, ignores double clicks, closes on resolve and stays open (buttons enabled again) on reject. A sync-true modal closes. A sync-false modal stays open. The idea archive modal closes after confirming. Integration test for the component covers all four cases. Full suite passes.</p><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> rewriting bootstrap-modal as a Glimmer component.</p>", "type": "Bug", "priority": "high", "complexity": "complex", "dependsOn": [], "milestone": "Ideas", "newMilestone": false, "screens": []}, {"key": "approve-state", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "Ideas: approve plan shows loading, closes once saved and keeps Approve disabled unless it fails", "description": "<p>Approving a plan gives no feedback: the modal shows no loading or completion and doesn't close, and the Approve button can be pressed again. Use the async confirm of <code>bootstrap-modal</code> and track the approval in the plan box.</p><ul><li><code>components/idea/plan-box.js</code>: a tracked <code>isApproving</code>. Once the user confirms, <code>confirmApprove</code> sets it and returns the Promise of <code>this.args.onApprove(plan)</code>. bootstrap-modal shows the spinner with disabled buttons and closes <strong>as soon as the approval is saved</strong>. It doesn't wait for the issues to be created. On rejection the controller (<code>controllers/app/project/idea/page.js</code> <code>approvePlan</code>) has already reported the error and rolled back. The modal stays open with its buttons back, and <code>isApproving</code> is cleared.</li><li>The footer Approve button (<code>data-idea-plan-approve</code>) is disabled and shows a spinner from the confirmation until the save ends. It is enabled again only if the approval fails. On success the plan becomes <code>approved</code> and <code>canApprove</code> hides the button. A plan that later turns <code>failed</code> (Talos) shows it again, as today.</li><li>Status label: while the selected plan is <code>approved</code> or <code>creating</code>, show <code>fa fa-spinner fa-spin</code> before the status text, so the user sees that issues are being created after the modal closes. The live <code>idea.plan.status.changed</code> reload already moves it to <code>created</code>/<code>failed</code>.</li><li>aprom-4105 moves the status label into <code>box-tools</code>. Whichever lands second keeps both changes.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> after confirming, the modal shows a spinner with disabled buttons and Approve is disabled. When the save succeeds, the modal closes, the button is gone and the label shows Approved with a spinner, then Created. On a failed save the modal stays open, the error is shown and Approve works again. Double clicks create one approval. Integration tests for the plan box cover success and failure.</p><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> live Talos progress inside the modal, cancelling an approval.</p>", "type": "Bug", "priority": "high", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": ["modal-async-confirm"], "milestone": "Ideas", "newMilestone": false, "screens": []}, {"key": "composer-images", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "Ideas: paste or drop images into a message, stored as uploads of the idea and shown inline", "description": "<p>Engineers want to show UI problems in an idea conversation with screenshots (e.g. with the problem circled). This issue waits on <strong>agaia-4106</strong> (<code>GET /upload/{id}</code> returns 404 for idea uploads), which must be fixed first. Today the toastui editor has no image hook, so a pasted image becomes a base64 <code>data:</code> URI inside the body. That bloats messages and is unreadable to the planning agent. Store images as Gaia uploads instead and keep only a reference in the markdown.</p><ul><li><strong>Contract (shared with the Talos issue of this plan):</strong> an image in a message body is <code>![&lt;file name&gt;](upload:&lt;uploadId&gt;)</code>. No tokens or host in the body.</li><li><code>modifiers/initialize-toastui.js</code>: an optional named argument (e.g. <code>onImage</code>) registered as toastui's <code>addImageBlobHook</code>. It gets the blob, awaits <code>onImage(blob)</code> which resolves to <code>{url, alt}</code>, and inserts the image through the hook's callback. Without the argument, behaviour is unchanged. Passed through by <code>components/app-ui/toast-ui.hbs</code>. The modifier makes no API calls itself.</li><li><code>components/idea/composer.js</code> provides <code>onImage</code>. The upload itself goes in a service (not in the component; e.g. extend <code>services/upload-preview.js</code> or add a small upload service). It creates an <code>Upload</code> with <code>relatedTo: 'idea'</code> and <code>relatedId</code> = the idea, the same way <code>components/issue/issue-details.js</code> <code>handleUpload</code> (~l.256) uploads issue attachments through the upload adapter (no raw <code>fetch</code>). Resolve to <code>upload:&lt;id&gt;</code>. Only images (png, jpg, gif, webp). Show the upload in progress, and report a failed upload without losing the text. The composer needs the idea id (pass it from <code>Idea::ConversationBox</code>).</li><li><code>modifiers/render-markdown.js</code>: before handing the markdown to the viewer, replace <code>upload:&lt;id&gt;</code> image sources with the address from <code>services/upload-preview.js</code> <code>previewUrl(id)</code>. Render the text first and the images when their addresses resolve. Check that the result survives <code>useDefaultHTMLSanitizer</code>. A missing or unreadable upload shows its name as text, not a broken page. Clicking an image opens the full size, as message screens do.</li><li>Verify that Gaia refuses an upload with <code>relatedTo idea</code> for an idea in a project the user can't write to. If it doesn't, file an agaia bug; don't work around it here.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> a pasted or dropped screenshot uploads and appears inline in the editor. The sent body contains <code>![name](upload:&lt;id&gt;)</code> and no <code>data:</code> URI. The thread shows the image for every member who can read the idea. Other ToastUi editors are unchanged. Integration or acceptance tests with Mirage cover upload, the stored reference and rendering.</p><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> images in the create page description, non-image attachments, deleting images, images in issue comments.</p>", "type": "Task", "priority": "medium", "complexity": "complex", "dependsOn": ["composer-editor", "agaia-4106"], "milestone": "Ideas", "newMilestone": false, "screens": []}, {"key": "talos-images", "project": "A Dev Tool", "projectCode": "adt", "subject": "Talos: give Nestor the images referenced in idea messages", "description": "<p>Engineers will attach screenshots to idea messages (Prometheus issue of this plan). This issue waits on <strong>agaia-4106</strong> (upload download 404), which must be fixed first. Nestor only sees message bodies as text, so an image reference means nothing to it unless Talos hands over the file. Nestor can Read image files and see them.</p><ul><li><strong>Contract (shared with the Prometheus issue of this plan):</strong> an image in a message body is <code>![&lt;file name&gt;](upload:&lt;uploadId&gt;)</code>.</li><li><code>talos/lib/ideas/conversation.py</code> <code>_prompt</code> (~l.55): for the new messages and the replayed history, find <code>upload:&lt;id&gt;</code> image references. Download each with <code>client.download</code> (<code>lib/ideas/client.py</code> -&gt; <code>lib/tracker.py</code> <code>download</code>) into <code>&lt;LOCAL_DIR&gt;/talos/state/ideas/&lt;ideaId&gt;/images/&lt;uploadId&gt;.&lt;ext&gt;</code>, cached so an image is downloaded once. Build the path from <code>LOCAL_DIR</code>, not the module folder (runtime copy rule). In the prompt, replace the reference with e.g. <code>[image \\"&lt;name&gt;\\": &lt;absolute path&gt;]</code>.</li><li>A failed download doesn't fail the turn: the reference becomes <code>[image \\"&lt;name&gt;\\" could not be downloaded]</code>, and the failure is logged.</li><li><code>talos/agents/nestor/role.md</code>: one line saying that engineer messages can reference images by path and that Nestor should Read them when they matter to the plan. Check that <code>agents/nestor/agent.json</code> allows Read on that folder (its deny list only blocks <code>state/*.db</code> and <code>state/logs</code>).</li><li>Windows and Linux per <code>dev-tools/CLAUDE.md</code>: <code>pathlib</code>, binary writes, no shell.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> unit tests (no network, temp dirs) cover: a reference is downloaded once and rewritten to its path; history replay rewrites too; a failed download yields the placeholder and the turn goes on; a message without images is unchanged. A manual run shows Nestor describing a pasted screenshot.</p><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> images in Nestor's own replies (it has nestor-screen), cleanup of old image files, the standalone Nestor UI and sim.</p>", "type": "Task", "priority": "medium", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": ["agaia-4106"], "milestone": "Ideas", "newMilestone": false, "screens": []}]}	created	\N	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-10-24 07:11:59	2026-10-24 07:17:42	f
ec5482566aef-0d7b-3158-0b4bc900ef43	c89b1b196aee-e15e-6d43-8adc416a487e	13	{"summary": "Fixes for the ezSearch regressions after aprom-4338. Board layout (chained, same files): the milestone tabs wrap to new rows again with the search inline at the top right (30%), using the pre-4338 layout model; the lanes scroll horizontally again; the issue quick preview no longer overlaps the board. Board search fields: the board gets its own search metadata that extends Issue without Milestone (metadata-driven extends/exclude, documented). Everywhere: the suggestion menu gets readable colours.", "issues": [{"key": "board-tabs-wrap", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "Task Board: milestone tabs no longer wrap since the inline search", "description": "<p>Since aprom-4338 the milestone tabs sit in a single row that scrolls sideways, and overflowing tabs are cut off. That came from 4338's own description, which was wrong: the engineer only asked for the search to be inline. The tabs must behave as before and wrap onto the next row when they don't fit, while the search stays inline at the top right.</p><p><strong>Changes</strong></p><ul><li>Restore the pre-4338 layout model in <code>app/styles/modules/board.scss</code> (<code>.board .board-container .nav-tabs-custom</code>, ~l.13-62 before 4338):<ul><li><code>.nav-tabs-custom</code> is <code>position: relative</code>.</li><li><code>.milestone-tabs</code> is an <code>inline-block</code>, now 70% wide, with its tabs wrapping normally (no <code>flex-wrap: nowrap</code>, no <code>overflow-x</code>, no scroll arrows).</li><li><code>.milestone-filters</code> is <code>position: absolute</code> at the top right, now 30% wide, holding <code>&lt;EzSearchCompact @linear={{true}}&gt;</code> at full width.</li></ul>Remove the flex row and tab-strip scroll rules that 4338 added (compare <code>board.scss</code> and <code>components/app-ui/task-board.hbs</code> with <code>dev</code> before 4338).</li><li>The suggestion menu and the Saved menu still open over the content below.</li><li>Below 768px the search goes under the tabs at full width.</li><li>Tabs keep working as drop targets (<code>.drop-zone-area</code>), including on a second row.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance criteria</strong></p><ul><li>With eight milestone tabs, the tabs wrap onto a second row, all are fully visible and clickable, and the search stays at the top right on the first row at 30% width.</li><li>An acceptance or integration check that the search's top equals the first tab's top, and that no tab is clipped.</li><li>Checked in the browser with two and eight tabs, and at 390px.</li></ul><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> lane scrolling and the quick preview (separate issues), search fields.</p>", "type": "Bug", "priority": "high", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": ["aprom-4338"], "milestone": "aga-v1", "newMilestone": false, "estimatedFiles": 3, "sizeReason": "", "screens": ["board-linear"]}, {"key": "board-hscroll", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "Task Board: lanes can no longer be scrolled horizontally", "description": "<p>Since aprom-4338 the Task Board's lanes can't be scrolled sideways any more, so lanes beyond the viewport can't be reached. Before 4338 the lanes scrolled through <code>.board .milestone.box-body { display: flex; overflow-x: auto; }</code> (<code>app/styles/modules/board.scss</code> ~l.69-74). The likely cause is a 4338 change to the board container or its ancestors (a flex parent without <code>min-width: 0</code>, an <code>overflow: hidden</code>, or a width change) that stops the lanes' box from being narrower than its content.</p><p><strong>Changes</strong></p><ul><li>Start with a failing check: an acceptance or integration test showing that the active milestone's <code>.milestone.box-body</code> has <code>scrollWidth &gt; clientWidth</code> and can be scrolled, with enough lanes.</li><li>Compare the board's styles and template with <code>dev</code> before 4338 and remove or fix the change that broke it. Don't add new scroll containers.</li><li>If board-tabs-wrap already fixed it, keep the test and say so in the PR.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance criteria</strong></p><ul><li>The test passes.</li><li>In the browser, lanes scroll horizontally with the scrollbar, trackpad and shift+wheel, and drag-and-drop still auto-scrolls the lanes.</li></ul><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> the tab layout, the quick preview.</p>", "type": "Bug", "priority": "high", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": ["board-tabs-wrap"], "milestone": "aga-v1", "newMilestone": false, "estimatedFiles": 3, "sizeReason": "", "screens": []}, {"key": "board-preview-overlap", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "Task Board: issue quick preview overlaps the board", "description": "<p>Since aprom-4338, opening an issue's quick preview on the Task Board leaves lanes showing through and under the preview panel: in the engineer's screenshot the Done and Feedback lane headers cross the panel's description. Before 4338 the board narrowed and the panel sat beside it (selection flow in <code>controllers/app/project/board.js</code> <code>selectIssue</code>, layout in <code>templates/app/project/board.hbs</code>). 4338 probably changed the board's positioning or stacking (position, z-index or width of the board container or of <code>.milestone-filters</code>). Note that lane headers have <code>z-index: 999</code> (<code>board.scss</code> ~l.84), which becomes a problem once their ancestors' stacking changes.</p><p><strong>Changes</strong></p><ul><li>Compare the board's template and styles with <code>dev</code> before 4338 and restore the board's position, width and stacking.</li><li>The search slot must not change the board container's layout: it is positioned inside <code>.nav-tabs-custom</code> only.</li><li>If an earlier fix already resolved it, add the test and say so in the PR.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance criteria</strong></p><ul><li>An acceptance test opens the quick preview and checks that the preview panel and the board columns don't overlap (bounding boxes).</li><li>In the browser, with the preview open, no lane, lane header or card shows over or under the panel, at desktop width and at 390px.</li></ul><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> the tab layout, lane scrolling.</p>", "type": "Bug", "priority": "high", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": ["board-hscroll"], "milestone": "aga-v1", "newMilestone": false, "estimatedFiles": 3, "sizeReason": "", "screens": []}, {"key": "board-search-fields", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "Task Board: search without the Milestone field (view metadata with extends and exclude)", "description": "<p>On the Task Board the milestone is chosen with the milestone tabs, so offering Milestone in the search makes no sense. The board uses the Issue search fields (<code>views.Issue.search</code>) today. Under the metadata-driven list-view architecture (<code>docs/architecture/metadata-views/architecture.md</code>), a view that needs different search fields declares its own search metadata instead of hard-coding exceptions.</p><p><strong>Changes</strong></p><ul><li><code>app/utils/metadata/metadata.js</code>: add <code>views.TaskBoard.search = { extends: 'Issue', exclude: ['issuemilestone.name'] }</code>.</li><li><code>app/services/query-schema.js</code>: support <code>extends</code> (start from the named module's search metadata: module, text field, fields, values) and <code>exclude</code> (field ids removed from it). Unknown <code>extends</code> or excluded ids throw a clear error in development and tests.</li><li>An excluded field isn't suggested and isn't in the builder. If it is typed, or comes in through <code>@query</code> or a saved search, parsing gives a clear error (\\"Milestone can't be searched here. Use the milestone tabs.\\"), and nothing is applied. Add the error code to the parser and schema helpers (<code>app/utils/query/schema.js</code>, <code>parse.js</code>) and its message to <code>translations/en-us.json</code>; the message can name the view's reason through an optional <code>excludeReason</code> translation key in the metadata.</li><li>Board route: load <code>query-schema</code> <code>load('TaskBoard', {projectId})</code> instead of <code>load('Issue', …)</code>.</li><li>Docs: add a section on <code>extends</code> and <code>exclude</code> to <code>docs/architecture/metadata-views/architecture.md</code>, with this case as the example.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance criteria</strong></p><ul><li>Unit tests: <code>extends</code>/<code>exclude</code> in the service, the error for an excluded field typed, loaded through <code>@query</code> and loaded through a saved search, and unknown names throwing.</li><li>A board acceptance scenario: typing <code>milestone</code> suggests no Milestone field, and <code>milestone:v0.4.8</code> shows the error and sends nothing.</li><li>The Issue list, Gantt and idea list still offer Milestone.</li></ul><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> excluding fields in other views, the board layout fixes.</p>", "type": "Task", "priority": "medium", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": ["aprom-4338"], "milestone": "aga-v1", "newMilestone": false, "estimatedFiles": 8, "sizeReason": "", "screens": []}, {"key": "ez-menu-contrast", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "ezSearch: suggestion menu looks washed out", "description": "<p>Everywhere ezSearch is used, the suggestion menu is hard to read: item labels are light grey, and the highlighted row is light pink with white text. Cause: <code>app/styles/components/ez-search-bar.scss</code> ~l.71 sets <code>li.active &gt; a { background: $primaryColorLight; }</code> without a text colour, so Bootstrap's white text for active items lands on a light background, and the other rows use AdminLTE's grey <code>.dropdown-menu</code> link colour.</p><p><strong>Changes</strong></p><ul><li>In <code>.ez-search-menu</code> (scoped, tokens only):<ul><li>Item labels <code>$darkColor</code>; the field id detail stays <code>$mediumColor</code>.</li><li>The active or hovered row gets a <code>$primaryColor</code> background with white label and detail text, matching the hover of the app's other dropdowns (<code>app/styles/components/dropdown.scss</code> ~l.44-49).</li><li>Section headers and the key hint stay muted but readable.</li><li>No opacity on the menu or its items.</li></ul></li><li>Check that the menu isn't inside an ancestor with reduced opacity (e.g. a disabled or loading state). Fix it at the source if so.</li><li>This applies to <code>&lt;EzSearch&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;EzSearchCompact&gt;</code> (both layouts).</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance criteria</strong></p><ul><li>An integration test asserts that the active row's computed background and text colours are the crimson token and white, and that normal rows use the dark text colour.</li><li>Checked in the browser on the Issue list, the idea list, the Gantt and the Task Board.</li></ul><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> menu content and ranking.</p>", "type": "Bug", "priority": "medium", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": [], "milestone": "aga-v1", "newMilestone": false, "estimatedFiles": 2, "sizeReason": "", "screens": []}]}	created	\N	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-11-07 21:28:12	2026-11-07 21:33:59	f
e86245a96adc-07a6-e36e-17413621f5b6	93059cf66adc-07a6-e36e-fd991ee2f20e	1	{"summary": "One Prometheus issue: add an icon button to each issue group heading in the notifications sidebar that marks all of that group's notifications as read without navigating, reusing the group component's existing _markGroupAsRead. No Gaia change.", "issues": [{"key": "group-mark-read", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "Notifications sidebar: mark an issue group as read from its heading without navigating", "description": "<p>The notifications sidebar groups notifications by issue (aprom-4063, aprom-4104). Today there are only three ways to mark notifications read: click one notification, click the group heading, or use \\"Mark all as read\\". The first two also navigate to the linked entity. Users need to clear one issue's notifications without leaving the page.</p><p><strong>Changes</strong></p><ul><li><code>app/components/app-ui/notifications/group.hbs</code>: put a <code>button</code> on the right side of <code>h5.notification-group-heading</code>, next to the issue-key link but outside it. Icon only, <code>fa fa-check-circle</code> (the same icon as the sidebar's <code>mark-all-read-btn</code>). Give it a <code>title</code> tooltip \\"Mark as read\\" and the attribute <code>data-notification-group-mark-read</code>.</li><li>Render the button only when at least one notification in the group is unread (<code>recipientRecords.firstObject.isRead === \\"0\\"</code>, the same check <code>notification.hbs</code> uses). Add a getter such as <code>hasUnread</code> in <code>group.js</code>.</li><li><code>app/components/app-ui/notifications/group.js</code>: add an action <code>markGroupAsRead(event)</code> that calls <code>event.preventDefault()</code> and <code>event.stopPropagation()</code>, then awaits the existing <code>_markGroupAsRead()</code>. It must not navigate. The unread count, the bell and cross-tab sync are already handled by <code>notifications.markAsRead</code>.</li><li>Ignore further clicks while a save is in progress, e.g. with a tracked flag that disables the button.</li><li>Styles in <code>app/styles/components/notifications-sidebar.scss</code> under <code>.notification-group-heading</code>: lay the heading out so the link sits left and the button right; the button is a plain icon in the existing colours. Square edges. No new colours.</li><li>Add the tooltip text to <code>translations/en-us.json</code> (e.g. <code>views.app.notifications.markGroupAsRead</code>).</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance criteria</strong></p><ul><li>A group with unread notifications shows the button in its heading. A group whose notifications are all read does not.</li><li>Clicking the button marks every notification in the group as read. The items lose their unread style, the unread count and bell update, the button disappears, and the route does not change.</li><li>Clicking the issue key still marks the group as read and navigates, as before.</li><li>Single-item groups behave the same way.</li><li>Integration test in <code>tests/integration/components/app-ui/notifications/</code> for showing and hiding the button and for no navigation. Acceptance scenario in <code>tests/acceptance/notification/</code> (Mirage) for marking a group read from the sidebar.</li></ul><p><strong>Out of scope</strong>: a mark-as-read control on single ungrouped notifications (e.g. membership) and any Gaia change.</p>", "type": "Task", "priority": "medium", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": [], "milestone": "Claude-v1", "newMilestone": false, "screens": []}]}	created	\N	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-10-24 08:40:08	2026-10-24 08:42:53	f
5c5c9e206adb-8ca9-66d5-1c614665818e	c3a4ac636adb-e65f-66d5-35fdd2e28d26	1	{"summary": "Two fixes for the Ideas UI, worked in parallel. A Prometheus issue fixes the plan box on the idea page: the 'View the plan' link opens the version that message proposed, the screens the version lists appear as thumbnails before approval, and the status label sits in box-tools. A Gaia bug fixes GET /upload/{id} returning 404 for idea screen uploads, which stops Talos attaching screens to the issues it creates.", "issues": [{"key": "plan-box-fixes", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "Idea page: 'View the plan' opens its version, plan shows its screens, status label alignment", "description": "<p>The idea page's plan box has three problems: the 'View the plan' link on a message does nothing, the screens a plan lists can't be seen before the plan is approved, and the status label in the box header is off-center.</p><ul><li><strong>View the plan:</strong> <code>components/idea/message-item.hbs</code> links to <code>#ideaplan-&lt;id&gt;</code>, which matches nothing. Replace it with an action passed down from the idea page controller (<code>controllers/app/project/idea/page.js</code>, through <code>Idea::ConversationBox</code>). The action sets the plan to show (for example a tracked <code>shownPlanId</code>) and scrolls the plan box (<code>data-idea-plan</code>) into view. <code>Idea::PlanBox</code> takes the requested plan as an argument and shows that version's tab. Keep the current behaviour otherwise: the latest version by default, the user's tab pick, and a newer version arriving resets the pick.</li><li><strong>Screens in the plan:</strong> under the plan summary, add a row of thumbnails for every screen key listed by any planned issue of the shown version, each key once. Resolve each key to its latest version from the idea's message screens (<code>message.screens</code>: uploadId, key, title, version), because that is what Talos attaches on approval. Reuse <code>Idea::MessageScreens</code> / <code>Idea::MessageScreen</code> (thumbnail plus click to preview) and pass the messages or a resolved list from the page. If a key has no screen, show it as plain text. Keep the existing key list in each issue's meta row.</li><li><strong>Status label:</strong> in <code>components/idea/plan-box.hbs</code>, put the status <code>label</code> inside <code>&lt;div class=\\"box-tools pull-right\\"&gt;</code>, as <code>conversation-box.hbs</code> does, so it is vertically centred in the header. Add no new CSS.</li><li>Translations in <code>translations/en-us.json</code> (<code>views.app.idea.plan.*</code>), Mirage data, and integration tests for plan-box and message-item.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> clicking 'View the plan' on a message for v2 while v3 exists shows the v2 tab and scrolls to the plan box. A plan whose issues list screens shows their latest thumbnails before approval. The status label is centred in the header, like the conversation box tools.</p><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> thumbnails inside each issue's detail, a picker for older screen versions, and the Gaia upload 404 (a separate issue; thumbnails depend on <code>/upload/{id}</code> working).</p>", "type": "Task", "priority": "medium", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": [], "milestone": "Claude-v1", "newMilestone": false, "screens": []}, {"key": "upload-download-404", "project": "A Gaia", "projectCode": "agaia", "subject": "Uploads: GET /upload/{id} returns 404 for idea screen uploads", "description": "<p>When a plan is approved, Talos attaches each planned issue's screens by downloading the PNG through gaia-mcp (<code>GET /api/v1/upload/{id}</code> with the agent's Bearer token) and uploading it to the new issue. For idea dc9f28086adb-14fc-3884-5d3000a5198e this failed: <code>Gaia download of upload 664eb6266adb-bb24-3884-1ae4db44bf6e returned 404</code>, with an empty body. The upload was created by gaia-mcp <code>upload_idea_screen</code> (multipart POST /upload, <code>relatedTo=idea</code>, <code>relatedId=&lt;idea id&gt;</code>).</p><ul><li>Find the root cause. Leads: <code>UploadController::getAction</code> (it throws <code>FileNotfound</code> when <code>filePath</code> is missing on disk and <code>ResourceNotFound</code> when the row can't be read), the <code>Upload</code> read under the agent's ACL (the <code>relatedTo idea</code> scope through Idea in <code>app/metadata/model/Upload.php</code> ~l.171, and <code>Acl::assertRecordIdInScope</code>), and whether a GET with an id reaches <code>getAction</code> at all. Also check which response these exceptions produce (the body was empty).</li><li>Start with an api-tester case that fails: upload a PNG related to an idea as a project member, then <code>GET /upload/{id}</code> returns 200 with the bytes. Add a case that a non-member gets 403/404.</li><li>Fix it in Gaia. If the cause turns out to be on the client side (gaia-mcp <code>download</code>, or Talos <code>lib/ideas/approval.py</code>), record the finding on this issue and open an A Dev Tool issue for it instead.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> approving a plan with screens attaches their PNGs to the created issues, with no 'could not be attached' message. The new api-tester cases pass.</p><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> the download-token preview flow (<code>/preview/get</code>) unless it shares the cause, and re-attaching screens to issues created earlier (e.g. #4104), which is done by hand.</p>", "type": "Bug", "priority": "high", "complexity": "complex", "dependsOn": [], "milestone": "Claude-v1", "newMilestone": false, "screens": []}]}	created	\N	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-10-23 21:45:00	2026-10-23 21:52:26	f
f88538c86adc-f821-179a-c9dac4010cb4	df2235066adc-c3c3-c7b3-5100bb8bba88	1	{"summary": "One A Dev Tool issue. Talos tells Nestor which project an idea belongs to. Nestor's role gets a rule for planning work in other projects, and approval labels every issue created outside the idea's project as a request from that project, to be evaluated before it is implemented. Cross-project creation stays allowed. The Prometheus link handling is filed separately by the engineer.", "issues": [{"key": "nestor-cross-project", "project": "A Dev Tool", "projectCode": "adt", "subject": "Nestor: plan cross-project issues as requests from the idea's project", "description": "<p>Ideas live in one project, but the work often spans several. For example, a Prometheus idea may need a new Gaia API. The engineer decided that Agent Nestor may keep planning issues in any project it can reach. An issue created outside the idea's own project, though, must say clearly that it is a request from that project and must be evaluated before anyone implements it. Today Nestor isn't told which project the idea belongs to, and approval creates every issue the same way.</p><ul><li><strong>Home project in the session prompt:</strong> <code>talos/lib/ideas/conversation.py</code> <code>Conversation.system_prompt</code> adds a line like <em>This idea belongs to A Prometheus (short code aprom)</em>. Resolve it from the idea's <code>projectId</code> (already returned by <code>lib/ideas/client.py</code> <code>idea()</code>) through the tracker's project ids and codes. <code>system_prompt</code> doesn't take the idea today, so pass it in. With the sim source, where an idea may have no project, leave the line out.</li><li><strong>Role rule:</strong> add a short section to <code>talos/agents/nestor/role.md</code> (Splitting the work). Plan the home project's work as usual. When the work needs a change in another project you can plan in, plan it there, but write it as a request: open with what the home project needs and why, then the proposed contract, and ask the receiving team to accept, adapt or decline it. Don't set it to blocker or critical, because that is the receiving project's call. The home-project issue that needs it lists it in <code>dependsOn</code>. In the reply, say which issues are cross-project requests.</li><li><strong>Label on approval, added by code:</strong> in <code>talos/lib/ideas/approval.py</code> <code>Approval.description</code>, when the issue's project differs from the idea's project, put a first paragraph before the description: <code>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Cross-project request&lt;/strong&gt; from &lt;home project name&gt; (idea \\"&lt;title&gt;\\", raised by &lt;owner name&gt;). Evaluate it before implementing it: accept, adapt or decline it on this issue.&lt;/p&gt;</code>. Same-project issues stay unchanged.</li><li><strong>Approval message:</strong> in <code>Approval._tell</code>, tag cross-project issues in the chat line and in the note to Nestor, e.g. <code>agaia-4101 (rule-api, request to A Gaia)</code>.</li><li><strong>Tests</strong> (Talos ideas tests, <code>ai test dev-tools</code>): the label appears for an issue in another project and not for a home-project issue, the system prompt names the home project, and the <code>_tell</code> text tags cross-project issues.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance criteria:</strong> a plan for an idea in A Prometheus with one aprom issue and one agaia issue creates the aprom issue unchanged and the agaia issue with the label as its first paragraph. Nestor's prompt names the idea's project. The approval message shows which issues are requests. Works on Windows and Linux as per the dev-tools rules.</p><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> blocking or limiting cross-project creation (explicitly not wanted), showing refs to issues the user can't read as plain text in Prometheus (filed separately by the engineer), Gaia reference checks (agaia-4075), linking projects, a request badge on the plan card, and a dedicated issue type.</p>", "type": "Task", "priority": "medium", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": [], "milestone": "Claude-v1", "newMilestone": false, "screens": []}]}	proposed	\N	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-10-24 05:58:16	2026-10-24 05:58:16	f
b7cdbcf26adc-f42c-e36e-bdce7eb9c569	5b131d096adc-f42c-e36e-1693e76da846	1	{"summary": "Clicking an issue, comment or time-log notification (or a group's issue heading) opens the issue in the current screen's quick view when that screen shows the issue, and falls back to navigating as today. The first issue adds a small issue-preview service, which screens register an opener with, and wires it into the notification components and the task board. The second issue registers the issue list and the gantt with the same service. Prometheus only; the notification context already has the project shortcode and issue number.", "issues": [{"key": "preview-service-board", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "Notifications: open the issue in the task board's quick view when it is on the board (issue-preview service)", "description": "<p>Clicking a notification always navigates to the issue page, even when you are on the task board and the issue is right there. When the current screen can show the issue, the click should open it there instead. This issue adds the mechanism and wires it to the task board.</p><ul><li><strong>New service <code>app/services/issue-preview.js</code></strong> (one responsibility: hand an 'open this issue' request to the screen that can preview it). <code>register(owner, {projectShortcode, open(issueNumber)})</code> returns a handle or dispose function, following the pattern of <code>hermes.register</code>. <code>open({projectShortcode, issueNumber})</code> returns <code>true</code> when a registered opener for the same project (case-insensitive) took the issue, and <code>false</code> otherwise. One active opener at a time (the current route).</li><li><strong>Task board:</strong> <code>routes/app/project/board.js</code> registers an opener in <code>setupController</code> (shortcode from <code>trackedProject</code>) and disposes it in <code>resetController</code> on exit, next to the Hermes intents. The opener looks for the issue by <code>issueNumber</code> in the lanes currently on the board (<code>controllers/app/project/board.js</code> <code>milestones</code>: open milestones plus backlog, already filtered by the search). If it finds the issue, it calls the existing <code>selectIssue(issue)</code> and returns <code>true</code>. If not, it returns <code>false</code>.</li><li><strong>Notification item</strong> <code>components/app-ui/notifications/notification.js</code> <code>markAsRead</code>: for <code>relatedTo</code> issue, comment and timelog, after marking it read, call <code>issuePreview.open({projectShortcode, issueNumber})</code> from the context and only <code>router.transitionTo(link)</code> when it returns <code>false</code>. Clicks on the user avatar or link and other <code>relatedTo</code> types behave as today.</li><li><strong>Group heading</strong> <code>components/app-ui/notifications/group.js</code> <code>goToIssue</code>: same, using the group's <code>shortcode</code> and <code>issueNumber</code>. Modifier and middle clicks still let the browser open the URL.</li><li>Keep the <code>config.app.notifications.enableNav</code> gate for both paths.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance criteria</strong></p><ul><li>On the task board, clicking an issue, comment or time-log notification (or the group heading) for an issue in a lane on the board opens the board's quick view of that issue. The URL doesn't change, and the notification(s) are marked read.</li><li>For an issue in another project, filtered out by the board search, or not on the board (for example in a completed milestone), the click navigates to the issue page as today.</li><li>After you leave the board, notification clicks navigate as today (the opener is disposed).</li><li>Unit tests for the service. Integration or acceptance tests (Mirage) for board versus fallback, under <code>tests/acceptance/notification/</code>.</li></ul><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> the issue list and gantt (separate issue), scrolling to the comment or time log in the quick view, and milestone and project notifications.</p>", "type": "Task", "priority": "medium", "complexity": "complex", "dependsOn": [], "milestone": "Claude-v1", "newMilestone": false, "screens": []}, {"key": "preview-list-gantt", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "Notifications: open the issue in the issue list and gantt quick views when it is on screen", "description": "<p>The <code>issue-preview</code> service (from the previous issue) lets a screen take a notification click and open the issue in its own quick view. The task board uses it. This issue registers the issue list and the gantt the same way.</p><ul><li><strong>Issue list:</strong> <code>routes/app/project/issue/index.js</code> registers an opener in <code>setupController</code> and disposes it in <code>resetController</code> on exit. The opener finds the issue by <code>issueNumber</code> among the rows currently loaded in <code>controllers/app/project/issue/index.js</code> and calls its <code>selectIssue(issue)</code>. If the issue isn't there, it returns <code>false</code>.</li><li><strong>Gantt:</strong> <code>routes/app/project/gantt.js</code> registers an opener in the same way. The opener finds the issue among the loaded gantt issues and calls <code>openIssuePanel(issue)</code> in <code>controllers/app/project/gantt.js</code>. If the issue isn't there, it returns <code>false</code>.</li><li>Openers only take issues of the screen's own project, as on the board.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance criteria</strong></p><ul><li>On the issue list or the gantt, clicking a notification for an issue shown there opens that screen's quick view or panel.</li><li>If the issue isn't shown (other page, filtered out, other project), the click navigates to the issue page.</li><li>Tests for each screen: the opener is registered and disposed, and both the preview and fallback paths are covered.</li></ul><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> loading an issue that isn't on the current page or filter, and other screens.</p>", "type": "Task", "priority": "medium", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": ["preview-service-board"], "milestone": "Claude-v1", "newMilestone": false, "screens": []}]}	proposed	\N	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-10-24 08:47:51	2026-10-24 08:47:51	f
d68b5c736adc-19b9-179a-3b1008388d0e	df2235066adc-c3c3-c7b3-5100bb8bba88	2	{"summary": "One A Dev Tool issue. Talos tells Nestor which project an idea belongs to. Nestor's role gets a rule for planning work in other projects. Approval marks every issue created outside the idea's project: its subject starts with \\"External Request: \\" and its description opens with a paragraph saying it is a request from the idea's project, to be evaluated before it is implemented. Cross-project creation stays allowed, and the issue type stays Task or Story. The Prometheus link handling is filed separately by the engineer.", "issues": [{"key": "nestor-cross-project", "project": "A Dev Tool", "projectCode": "adt", "subject": "Nestor: plan cross-project issues as External Requests from the idea's project", "description": "<p>Ideas live in one project, but the work often spans several. For example, a Prometheus idea may need a new Gaia API. The engineer decided that Agent Nestor may keep planning issues in any project it can reach. An issue created outside the idea's own project, though, must be clearly marked as a request from that project and must be evaluated before anyone implements it. It stays a Task or Story, and its subject starts with <strong>External Request:</strong>. Today Nestor isn't told which project the idea belongs to, and approval creates every issue the same way.</p><ul><li><strong>Home project in the session prompt:</strong> <code>talos/lib/ideas/conversation.py</code> <code>Conversation.system_prompt</code> adds a line like <em>This idea belongs to A Prometheus (short code aprom)</em>. Resolve it from the idea's <code>projectId</code> (already returned by <code>lib/ideas/client.py</code> <code>idea()</code>) through the tracker's project ids and codes. <code>system_prompt</code> doesn't take the idea today, so pass it in. With the sim source, where an idea may have no project, leave the line out.</li><li><strong>Role rule:</strong> add a short section to <code>talos/agents/nestor/role.md</code> (Splitting the work). Plan the home project's work as usual. When the work needs a change in another project you can plan in, plan it there, but write it as a request: open with what the home project needs and why, then the proposed contract, and ask the receiving team to accept, adapt or decline it. Keep the type Task or Story. Don't add the External Request prefix yourself, because Talos adds it. Don't set it to blocker or critical, because that is the receiving project's call. The home-project issue that needs it lists it in <code>dependsOn</code>. In the reply, say which issues are external requests.</li><li><strong>Marking on approval, done by code:</strong> in <code>talos/lib/ideas/approval.py</code>, when an issue's project differs from the idea's project: (1) the subject passed to <code>tracker.create_issue</code> becomes <code>External Request: &lt;subject&gt;</code>; don't add it twice if the subject already starts with it. (2) <code>Approval.description</code> puts a first paragraph before the description: <code>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;External Request&lt;/strong&gt; from &lt;home project name&gt; (idea \\"&lt;title&gt;\\", raised by &lt;owner name&gt;). Evaluate it before implementing it: accept, adapt or decline it on this issue.&lt;/p&gt;</code>. Same-project issues stay unchanged.</li><li><strong>Approval message:</strong> in <code>Approval._tell</code>, tag external requests in the chat line and in the note to Nestor, e.g. <code>agaia-4101 (rule-api, external request to A Gaia)</code>.</li><li><strong>Tests</strong> (Talos ideas tests, <code>ai test dev-tools</code>): an issue in another project gets the subject prefix (only once) and the paragraph, a home-project issue gets neither, the system prompt names the home project, and the <code>_tell</code> text tags external requests.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance criteria:</strong> a plan for an idea in A Prometheus with one aprom issue and one agaia issue creates the aprom issue unchanged. The agaia issue's subject starts with <code>External Request: </code>, its description opens with the request paragraph, and its type is the one the plan gave (Task or Story). Nestor's prompt names the idea's project. The approval message shows which issues are external requests. Works on Windows and Linux as per the dev-tools rules.</p><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> blocking or limiting cross-project creation (explicitly not wanted), showing refs to issues the user can't read as plain text in Prometheus (filed separately by the engineer), Gaia reference checks (agaia-4075), linking projects, a request badge on the plan card, and a dedicated issue type.</p>", "type": "Task", "priority": "medium", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": [], "milestone": "Claude-v1", "newMilestone": false, "screens": []}]}	created	\N	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-10-24 06:01:58	2026-10-24 06:03:34	f
4e3fd4996adc-e07b-a0a9-e4d5d5de8e32	f057b8796adc-e2c6-a0a9-8a64e255dbf5	1	{"summary": "One Prometheus issue: on the idea page, issues created from a plan are links only when the user can read their project (the idea's project or one of the user's projects). Other created issues show as plain text, so users still see everything their idea produced without links to a permission-denied page. It works whether or not Gaia returns the linked issue. The Gaia scope check and Nestor's 'External Request' wording are filed separately by the engineer.", "issues": [{"key": "readable-issue-links", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "Ideas: created issues in projects the user can't read show as plain text, not links", "description": "<p>A plan approved in one project can create issues in other projects (for example a frontend idea that needs a new API in the backend project). Users should still see every issue their idea created. But a link to an issue in a project they are not a member of only leads to a permission-denied page. So on the idea page, a created issue is a link only when the user can read its project. Otherwise it is plain text. Gaia still decides who can read what. This change only decides whether a link is shown.</p><p><strong>Changes</strong></p><ul><li><code>controllers/app/project/idea/page.js</code>: add a getter for the project ids the user can read: the idea's <code>projectId</code> plus the ids in <code>currentUser.user.projects</code> (<code>services/current-user.js</code> already loads them with <code>include: 'projects'</code>). Pass it to <code>Idea::CreatedIssuesBox</code> and <code>Idea::PlanBox</code> in <code>templates/app/project/idea/page.hbs</code> (for example <code>@readableProjectIds</code>). Don't add new API calls.</li><li><code>components/idea/created-issues-box.{hbs,js}</code>: render the <code>LinkTo</code> (line ~11) only when <code>link.issue.projectId</code> is in the readable ids. Otherwise show the same <code>SHORTCODE-NUMBER</code> and subject as muted text (<code>text-muted</code>), with no anchor.</li><li><code>components/idea/plan-box.{hbs,js}</code>: in the <code>createdIssues</code> getter (~l.141), also record whether each created issue is readable. In the template (~l.42), render the <code>fa-external-link</code> <code>LinkTo</code> only for readable ones. Show the others as muted text with the issue key.</li><li>Handle a link with no issue: Gaia may stop returning issues the user can't read through <code>rels=issue</code>. An <code>ideaplanissue</code> with no loaded issue must not crash or show an empty link. In Created Issues, show the plan item's key and subject from the plan body (item with the same <code>key</code>) as muted text. In the plan box, show no created-issue reference for that item.</li><li>Don't add a tooltip or a new style. Use the existing <code>text-muted</code> class. Put any new string in <code>translations/en-us.json</code>.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance criteria</strong></p><ul><li>A created issue in the idea's project, or in a project the user belongs to, is a link to <code>app.project.issue.page</code>, as it is today.</li><li>A created issue in a project the user doesn't belong to shows its key (and subject in Created Issues) as plain text in both boxes, with no anchor element.</li><li>An <code>ideaplanissue</code> whose <code>issue</code> is missing renders without errors, as plain text from the plan item.</li><li>Mirage data covers all three cases. Integration tests for <code>Idea::CreatedIssuesBox</code> and <code>Idea::PlanBox</code> check link vs. plain text. The existing idea tests still pass.</li></ul><p><strong>Out of scope</strong></p><ul><li>Gaia-side checks on <code>Ideaplanissue.issueId</code> / <code>ideaplanId</code> scope (filed separately in A Gaia).</li><li>Marking plan items that target another project as external requests.</li><li>Special read rules for admins who aren't members of a project (they see plain text).</li></ul>", "type": "Task", "priority": "medium", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": [], "milestone": "Claude-v1", "newMilestone": false, "screens": []}]}	created	\N	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-10-24 06:08:14	2026-10-24 06:10:25	f
ac3329276adc-b462-2863-0e4dad4ef2ef	e5bbc5d16adc-d3fe-2863-120be2b9005e	1	{"summary": "Four Prometheus issues for the idea pages. The composer and the create page's description get the toastui rich text editor used by issue comments, and they keep producing markdown. The create page loses its 'How it works' box and the idea page boxes get tighter. The shared bootstrap-modal learns to wait for an async confirm (spinner, buttons disabled, closes on success, stays open on failure); the approve flow of the plan box then uses it, keeps the Approve button disabled until the save succeeds or fails, and shows progress on the status label. The editor and layout issues are independent; the plan box issue depends on the modal issue.", "issues": [{"key": "composer-editor", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "Ideas: rich text editor with toolbar for the message composer and the idea description", "description": "<p>The idea message composer is a 2-row plain textarea, which is too small for real messages and has no formatting. Give it the same editor that issue comments use. Gaia's <code>Ideamessage.body</code> is already an unlimited <code>text</code> column, so this is UI only.</p><ul><li><code>components/idea/composer.{hbs,js}</code>: replace <code>FormFields::FieldTextArea</code> with <code>AppUi::ToastUi</code> (<code>@editType='wysiwyg'</code>, toolbar shown, <code>@hide={{array 'footer'}}</code>), as in <code>components/conversation-ui/message-box.hbs</code>. Keep the Send button (<code>AppUi::Button</code>, <code>@aclContext=\\"ideamessage.create\\"</code>). Ctrl+Enter sends (<code>@onKeyboardSubmit</code>, as in comments). Plain Enter adds a new line. While sending or when the idea is archived, the composer is disabled. After a successful send the editor is cleared (the modifier listens for <code>clearContents</code> on its pubSub; see how comments clear). On failure the text is kept.</li><li><strong>Messages stay markdown.</strong> The thread renders bodies with <code>modifiers/render-markdown.js</code>, and the planning agent reads and writes markdown. <code>modifiers/initialize-toastui.js</code> only emits <code>editor.getHtml()</code> (change event and keyboard submit). Add an optional named argument (e.g. <code>output='markdown'</code>) that emits <code>editor.getMarkdown()</code> instead, passed through by <code>components/app-ui/toast-ui.hbs</code>. The default stays HTML, so the other editors don't change.</li><li>No user mentions or issue search: pass no <code>usersList</code>/<code>issueSearch</code>, and make sure the editor works without them.</li><li>Create page <code>templates/app/project/idea/create.hbs</code>: the description field (<code>idea.description</code>) uses the same editor in markdown mode, wrapped in a <code>form-field</code> with <code>data-field</code>, like <code>templates/app/project/issue/create.hbs</code>.</li><li>The editor's height should be comfortable for a few paragraphs, like the issue comment box. Square edges per the design system.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> a message with headings, a list, bold text and a code block is sent from the toolbar and renders formatted in the thread. The stored body is markdown. Ctrl+Enter sends. The editor clears after a successful send and keeps the text on a failure. An archived idea's composer is disabled. The create page description uses the editor. Other ToastUi editors still emit HTML. Integration tests for the composer, and the markdown option covered.</p><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> mentions, issue links, attachments, editing sent messages.</p>", "type": "Task", "priority": "medium", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": [], "milestone": "Claude-v1", "newMilestone": false, "screens": []}, {"key": "layout-cleanup", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "Ideas: remove 'How it works' from the create page and tighten the idea page boxes", "description": "<p>The idea pages waste space: the create page has a 'How it works' side box nobody needs, and the idea page boxes have a lot of empty space.</p><ul><li><code>templates/app/project/idea/create.hbs</code>: remove the 'How it works' box (the <code>col-md-4</code> side column) and its <code>views.app.idea.create.how.*</code> strings in <code>translations/en-us.json</code>. With the side column gone, the form box spans the full row (<code>col-md-12</code>); Start/Cancel stay below the box.</li><li><code>components/idea/summary-box.hbs</code>: each stat is an <code>h4</code> with a <code>small</code>, which makes the box tall. Make it a compact list with the label left and the value right, one line per stat, e.g. <code>ul.list-group.list-group-unbordered</code> with <code>pull-right</code> values, as in AdminLTE profile boxes. Reuse an existing pattern from the app if there is one.</li><li><code>components/idea/conversation-box.hbs</code> and <code>styles/modules/idea.scss</code>: remove the extra empty space around the thread and the composer (empty-thread text, padding between the thread footer and the composer footer). Change only <code>.idea-page</code>-scoped styles.</li><li>Compare against the issue detail page in the running app before calling it done.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> no 'How it works' box. The create form uses the full width. The Summary box is about half its current height. The conversation box has no large empty areas. No other pages change. Existing idea tests are updated.</p><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> the plan box layout (aprom-4105), new content.</p>", "type": "Task", "priority": "medium", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": [], "milestone": "Claude-v1", "newMilestone": false, "screens": []}, {"key": "modal-async-confirm", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "bootstrap-modal: wait for an async confirm, with loading state, and close on success", "description": "<p>Confirm modals can't show that work is running, and some don't close after confirming. In <code>templates/components/bootstrap-modal.hbs</code> the confirm button calls the passed-in <code>confirm</code> directly (<code>@onClick={{this.confirm}}</code>). The component's own <code>actions.confirm</code> in <code>components/bootstrap-modal.js</code>, which hides the modal when <code>confirm()</code> returns true, never runs. So callers that return <code>true</code> to close (e.g. <code>components/idea/plan-box.js</code> <code>confirmApprove</code>, after aprom-4103) stay open. Check this in the browser first.</p><ul><li>Route the confirm button through the component's own confirm handler.</li><li>If <code>confirm()</code> returns a Promise: mark the modal busy, which disables Confirm and Cancel and shows a <code>fa fa-spinner fa-spin</code> on Confirm. Ignore repeated clicks. Hide the modal when it resolves truthy. When it rejects or resolves falsy, clear the busy state and keep the modal open.</li><li>Synchronous returns behave as documented: <code>true</code> hides, anything else leaves it open. Check the 31 existing usages for any that relied on the old behaviour (e.g. a caller that closes itself and would now be hidden twice, or returns true but expected to stay open) and adjust them in this PR.</li><li>The close path still goes through <code>hidden.bs.modal</code> -&gt; <code>close</code>, so no backdrop is left behind (aprom-4103). No new jQuery outside this component.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> a modal whose confirm returns a Promise shows the spinner, ignores double clicks, closes on resolve and stays open (buttons enabled again) on reject. A sync-true modal closes. A sync-false modal stays open. The idea archive and approve modals close after confirming. Integration test for the component covers all four cases. Full suite passes.</p><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> rewriting bootstrap-modal as a Glimmer component.</p>", "type": "Bug", "priority": "high", "complexity": "complex", "dependsOn": [], "milestone": "Claude-v1", "newMilestone": false, "screens": []}, {"key": "approve-state", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "Ideas: approve plan shows saving, closes when done and keeps Approve disabled until it fails", "description": "<p>Approving a plan gives no feedback: the modal shows no loading or completion and doesn't close, and the Approve button can be pressed again. Use the async confirm of <code>bootstrap-modal</code> and track the approval in the plan box.</p><ul><li><code>components/idea/plan-box.js</code>: a tracked <code>isApproving</code>. <code>confirmApprove</code> returns the Promise of <code>this.args.onApprove(plan)</code>. bootstrap-modal shows the spinner and closes when it resolves. On rejection the controller (<code>controllers/app/project/idea/page.js</code> <code>approvePlan</code>) has already reported the error and rolled back. The modal stays open and the buttons come back.</li><li>The footer Approve button (<code>data-idea-plan-approve</code>) is disabled from the moment it's pressed, including while the modal is open and saving. It is enabled again only if the approval fails or the modal is cancelled. On success the plan becomes <code>approved</code> and <code>canApprove</code> hides the button. A plan that later turns <code>failed</code> (Talos) shows it again, as today.</li><li>Status label: while the selected plan is <code>approved</code> or <code>creating</code>, show <code>fa fa-spinner fa-spin</code> before the status text, so the user sees that issues are being created. The live <code>idea.plan.status.changed</code> reload already moves it to <code>created</code>/<code>failed</code>.</li><li>aprom-4105 moves the status label into <code>box-tools</code>. Whichever lands second keeps both changes.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> confirming shows a spinner and disabled buttons in the modal. On success the modal closes, the button is gone and the label shows Approved with a spinner, then Created. On a failed save the modal stays open with an error and Approve works again. Double clicks create one approval. Integration tests for the plan box cover success and failure.</p><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> live Talos progress inside the modal, cancelling an approval.</p>", "type": "Bug", "priority": "high", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": ["modal-async-confirm"], "milestone": "Claude-v1", "newMilestone": false, "screens": []}]}	proposed	\N	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-10-24 06:31:08	2026-10-24 06:31:08	f
3e5e67e26adc-986b-1f5a-97badbba1c45	e5bbc5d16adc-d3fe-2863-120be2b9005e	2	{"summary": "Four Prometheus issues for the idea pages. The message composer gets the toastui rich text editor that issue comments use, and it keeps producing markdown through a new opt-in output option on the editor modifier. The create page then drops 'How it works' and the nested columns so the form fills the content area, and its description (the idea's first message) uses the same full editor. Separately, the shared bootstrap-modal learns to wait for an async confirm: spinner and disabled buttons while it runs, closes on success, stays open on failure. The plan box's approve flow uses it and keeps Approve disabled from the click until the save succeeds or fails. The editor issue and the modal issue can go in parallel; each has one follow-up.", "issues": [{"key": "composer-editor", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "Ideas: rich text editor with toolbar for the message composer", "description": "<p>The idea message composer is a 2-row plain textarea, which is too small for real messages and has no formatting. Give it the same editor issue comments use. Gaia's <code>Ideamessage.body</code> is already an unlimited <code>text</code> column, so this is UI only.</p><ul><li><code>components/idea/composer.{hbs,js}</code>: replace <code>FormFields::FieldTextArea</code> with <code>AppUi::ToastUi</code> (<code>@editType='wysiwyg'</code>, toolbar shown, <code>@hide={{array 'footer'}}</code>), as in <code>components/conversation-ui/message-box.hbs</code>. Keep the Send button (<code>AppUi::Button</code>, <code>@aclContext=\\"ideamessage.create\\"</code>). <strong>Ctrl+Enter or Send</strong> sends, as in issue comments (<code>@onKeyboardSubmit</code>). Plain Enter adds a new line. While sending or when the idea is archived, the composer is disabled. After a successful send the editor is cleared (the modifier resets on <code>clearContents</code> from its pubSub; see how comments clear). On failure the text is kept.</li><li><strong>Messages stay markdown.</strong> The thread renders bodies with <code>modifiers/render-markdown.js</code>, and the planning agent reads and writes markdown. <code>modifiers/initialize-toastui.js</code> only emits <code>editor.getHtml()</code> (change event ~l.189 and keyboard submit ~l.239). Add an optional named argument (e.g. <code>output='markdown'</code>) that emits <code>editor.getMarkdown()</code> instead, passed through by <code>components/app-ui/toast-ui.hbs</code>. The default stays HTML, so the other editors don't change. The create page (separate issue) will use this option too.</li><li>No user mentions or issue search for now: pass no <code>usersList</code>/<code>issueSearch</code>, and make sure the editor and its Tribute setup work without them.</li><li>The editor's height should be comfortable for a few paragraphs, like the issue comment box. Square edges per <code>docs/ui/design-system.md</code>.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> a message with headings, a list, bold text and a code block is sent from the toolbar and renders formatted in the thread. The stored body is markdown. Ctrl+Enter and the Send button both send. The editor clears after a successful send and keeps the text on a failure. An archived idea's composer is disabled. Other ToastUi editors still emit HTML. Integration tests for the composer, and the markdown option covered.</p><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> mentions, issue links, attachments, editing sent messages, the create page.</p>", "type": "Task", "priority": "medium", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": [], "milestone": "Claude-v1", "newMilestone": false, "screens": []}, {"key": "create-page", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "Ideas: create page fills the content column, no 'How it works', full editor for the first message", "description": "<p>The Start an Idea page wastes space and treats the first message as a small field. The ideas layout (<code>templates/app/project/idea.hbs</code>) already renders each page in a <code>col-md-9</code> content column. <code>templates/app/project/idea/create.hbs</code> nests another <code>.row</code> with a <code>col-md-8</code> form and a <code>col-md-4</code> 'How it works' box inside it, which leaves empty space on both sides of the form. The description becomes the idea's first message (<code>controllers/app/project/idea/create.js</code> <code>afterSave</code>), so it needs the full editor, not a small field.</p><ul><li><code>create.hbs</code>: remove the 'How it works' box and the nested <code>.row</code>/<code>col-md-8</code>/<code>col-md-4</code>. The form box fills the content column, like the 'Brainstorm an idea' box in <code>templates/app/project/idea/index.hbs</code>. Start/Cancel stay below the box. Keep <code>AccessControl @aclContext=\\"idea.create\\"</code>.</li><li>Remove the <code>views.app.idea.create.how.*</code> strings from <code>translations/en-us.json</code>.</li><li>Description (<code>idea.description</code>): replace <code>FormFields::FieldTextArea</code> with <code>AppUi::ToastUi</code> (wysiwyg, toolbar, footer hidden), using the markdown output option added by the composer issue. No mentions or issue search. Wrap it in <code>div.form-field</code> with <code>data-field=\\"idea.description\\"</code>, as in <code>templates/app/project/issue/create.hbs</code>. Give it a height for a real first message, similar to the issue description editor. The value saved to <code>idea.description</code> and the first message is markdown.</li><li>Compare against the issue create page in the running app before calling it done.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> no 'How it works' box. The form box spans the whole content column next to the ideas list, with no empty columns beside it. The description uses the toolbar editor, and its markdown becomes the first message, rendered formatted in the thread. Title validation still works. Existing idea create tests are updated.</p><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> the idea page boxes, mentions.</p>", "type": "Task", "priority": "medium", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": ["composer-editor"], "milestone": "Claude-v1", "newMilestone": false, "screens": []}, {"key": "modal-async-confirm", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "bootstrap-modal: wait for an async confirm, with loading state, and close on success", "description": "<p>Confirm modals can't show that work is running, and some don't close after confirming. In <code>templates/components/bootstrap-modal.hbs</code> the confirm button calls the passed-in <code>confirm</code> directly (<code>@onClick={{this.confirm}}</code>). The component's own <code>actions.confirm</code> in <code>components/bootstrap-modal.js</code>, which hides the modal when <code>confirm()</code> returns true, never runs. So callers that return <code>true</code> to close (e.g. <code>components/idea/plan-box.js</code> <code>confirmApprove</code>, after aprom-4103) stay open. Check this in the browser first.</p><ul><li>Route the confirm button through the component's own confirm handler.</li><li>If <code>confirm()</code> returns a Promise: mark the modal busy, which disables Confirm and Cancel and shows a <code>fa fa-spinner fa-spin</code> on Confirm. Ignore repeated clicks. Hide the modal as soon as it resolves truthy. When it rejects or resolves falsy, clear the busy state and keep the modal open.</li><li>Synchronous returns behave as documented: <code>true</code> hides, anything else leaves it open. Check the ~31 existing usages for any that relied on the old behaviour (e.g. a caller that closes itself and would now be hidden twice, or returns true but expects to stay open) and adjust them in this PR.</li><li>The close path still goes through <code>hidden.bs.modal</code> -&gt; <code>close</code>, so no backdrop is left behind (aprom-4103). No new jQuery outside this component. Remove the stray backtick after <code>modal-footer</code> in the template.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> a modal whose confirm returns a Promise shows the spinner, ignores double clicks, closes on resolve and stays open (buttons enabled again) on reject. A sync-true modal closes. A sync-false modal stays open. The idea archive modal closes after confirming. Integration test for the component covers all four cases. Full suite passes.</p><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> rewriting bootstrap-modal as a Glimmer component.</p>", "type": "Bug", "priority": "high", "complexity": "complex", "dependsOn": [], "milestone": "Claude-v1", "newMilestone": false, "screens": []}, {"key": "approve-state", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "Ideas: approve plan shows loading, closes once saved and keeps Approve disabled unless it fails", "description": "<p>Approving a plan gives no feedback: the modal shows no loading or completion and doesn't close, and the Approve button can be pressed again. Use the async confirm of <code>bootstrap-modal</code> and track the approval in the plan box.</p><ul><li><code>components/idea/plan-box.js</code>: a tracked <code>isApproving</code>. Once the user confirms, <code>confirmApprove</code> sets it and returns the Promise of <code>this.args.onApprove(plan)</code>. bootstrap-modal shows the spinner with disabled buttons and closes <strong>as soon as the approval is saved</strong>. It doesn't wait for the issues to be created. On rejection the controller (<code>controllers/app/project/idea/page.js</code> <code>approvePlan</code>) has already reported the error and rolled back. The modal stays open with its buttons back, and <code>isApproving</code> is cleared.</li><li>The footer Approve button (<code>data-idea-plan-approve</code>) is disabled and shows a spinner from the confirmation until the save ends. It is enabled again only if the approval fails. On success the plan becomes <code>approved</code> and <code>canApprove</code> hides the button. A plan that later turns <code>failed</code> (Talos) shows it again, as today.</li><li>Status label: while the selected plan is <code>approved</code> or <code>creating</code>, show <code>fa fa-spinner fa-spin</code> before the status text, so the user sees that issues are being created after the modal closes. The live <code>idea.plan.status.changed</code> reload already moves it to <code>created</code>/<code>failed</code>.</li><li>aprom-4105 moves the status label into <code>box-tools</code>. Whichever lands second keeps both changes.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> after confirming, the modal shows a spinner with disabled buttons and Approve is disabled. When the save succeeds, the modal closes, the button is gone and the label shows Approved with a spinner, then Created. On a failed save the modal stays open, the error is shown and Approve works again. Double clicks create one approval. Integration tests for the plan box cover success and failure.</p><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> live Talos progress inside the modal, cancelling an approval.</p>", "type": "Bug", "priority": "high", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": ["modal-async-confirm"], "milestone": "Claude-v1", "newMilestone": false, "screens": []}]}	proposed	\N	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-10-24 06:54:54	2026-10-24 06:54:54	f
1fadfea26adc-b1c2-91a7-e34517a3dd09	5b131d096adc-f42c-e36e-1693e76da846	2	{"summary": "A notification click works out its target from the notification's relatedTo/relatedId columns (comment and time-log notifications point at their issue). If a screen with an issue quick view (task board, issue list or gantt) is open and holds that issue id, the issue opens there; otherwise Prometheus navigates to the issue detail page. Gaia first backfills relatedTo/relatedId on notifications created before agaia-4064, so every row can be resolved. Prometheus then adds an issue-preview service and the task board as the first preview view, and finally registers the issue list and the gantt.", "issues": [{"key": "notif-backfill", "project": "A Gaia", "projectCode": "agaia", "subject": "Notifications: backfill relatedTo and relatedId on notifications created before the columns existed", "description": "<p>agaia-4064 added <code>relatedTo</code>/<code>relatedId</code> to <code>system_notifications</code> and sets them on every new notification. Older rows still have NULL. Prometheus is about to use these columns to decide what a notification click opens, so every existing row needs them.</p><ul><li>Add a one-off, idempotent backfill that only touches rows where <code>relatedId</code> is NULL. Gaia has no data-migration mechanism yet (only <code>core/tasks/MigrationTask.php</code> for schema sync and <code>core/tasks/AdminTask.php</code>), so choose the smallest fitting form, for example a CLI task action next to these. Document how to run it in the PR and in the repo docs.</li><li>Use the same mapping as the modules in <code>app/api/v1/controllers/components/notifications/modules/</code>:<ul><li>Issue, comment and time-log notifications: <code>relatedTo = issue</code>, <code>relatedId</code> = the id in the description's <code>{{Issue@&lt;id&gt;}}</code> tag (every one has it).</li><li>Milestone notifications: <code>relatedTo = milestone</code>, with the milestone id from <code>context</code>.</li><li>Membership notifications: <code>relatedTo = project</code>, with the project id from <code>context</code> (or the membership it names).</li></ul>Tell them apart by <code>context.relatedTo</code>. If the context lacks an id, look it up from the related record.</li><li>Leave rows that can't be resolved as NULL and count them in the task output (updated, skipped, unresolved).</li><li>Don't change <code>context</code>, <code>description</code> or the recipient rows.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance criteria</strong></p><ul><li>After it runs, every resolvable old notification has the same <code>relatedTo</code>/<code>relatedId</code> a newly created one of its kind would have.</li><li>Running it again changes nothing.</li><li>Tests cover each kind (issue, comment, time log, milestone, membership) plus the unresolved case.</li></ul><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> changing how new notifications are created, and any API change.</p>", "type": "Task", "priority": "medium", "complexity": "complex", "dependsOn": [], "milestone": "Claude-v1", "newMilestone": false, "screens": []}, {"key": "preview-service-board", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "Notifications: open the issue in the current screen's quick view by relatedTo/relatedId (issue-preview service, task board first)", "description": "<p>Clicking a notification always goes to a URL built from its context. Notifications now carry <code>relatedTo</code>/<code>relatedId</code>, which name the entity they belong to (comment and time-log notifications point at their issue). When the screen you are on has an issue quick view and shows that issue, the click should open it there; otherwise it should open the issue detail page. This issue adds the mechanism and the task board as the first preview view.</p><ul><li><strong>New service <code>app/services/issue-preview.js</code></strong> (one responsibility: send an issue to the open screen that can preview it).<ul><li><code>register(owner, {hasIssue(issueId), open(issueId)})</code> returns a dispose function, following the pattern of <code>hermes.register</code>. One active registration: the screen that is open.</li><li><code>open(issueId)</code> returns <code>true</code> when the registered view has the issue and opened it, and <code>false</code> otherwise.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Task board:</strong> <code>routes/app/project/board.js</code> registers in <code>setupController</code> and disposes in <code>resetController</code> on exit, next to the Hermes intents. <code>hasIssue</code> looks for the id in the lanes on screen (<code>controllers/app/project/board.js</code> <code>milestones</code>: open milestones plus backlog, already filtered by the search, as <code>findIssueById</code> does). <code>open</code> calls the existing <code>selectIssue(issue)</code>.</li><li><strong>Notification click</strong> (<code>components/app-ui/notifications/notification.js</code> <code>markAsRead</code>):<ul><li>When <code>notification.relatedTo === 'issue'</code>: mark the notification read, then call <code>issuePreview.open(notification.relatedId)</code>. If no view took it, transition to <code>app.project.issue.page</code> with the project shortcode and issue number from the notification's context. These are only route parameters; the target is always <code>relatedId</code>.</li><li>A notification without <code>relatedId</code> is only marked read.</li><li>Clicks on a user avatar or user link, and notifications whose <code>relatedTo</code> is milestone or project, behave as today.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Group heading</strong> (<code>components/app-ui/notifications/group.js</code> <code>goToIssue</code>): same flow, using the <code>relatedId</code> of the group's notifications. Modifier and middle clicks still let the browser open the <code>href</code> in a new tab.</li><li><strong>Live notifications:</strong> check that the <code>notification.created</code> envelope's <code>changes</code> includes <code>relatedTo</code>/<code>relatedId</code> (<code>services/notifications.js</code> <code>onNotificationCreated</code>). If it doesn't, file a Gaia issue rather than reading the context.</li><li>Keep the <code>config.app.notifications.enableNav</code> gate.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance criteria</strong></p><ul><li>On the task board, clicking an issue, comment or time-log notification (or the group heading) whose issue is in a lane on the board opens the board's quick view. The URL doesn't change, and the notifications are marked read.</li><li>If the issue isn't on the board (other project, filtered out, completed milestone), or you aren't on the board, the click opens the issue detail page.</li><li>After you leave the board, its registration is disposed.</li><li>Unit tests for the service. Mirage acceptance tests under <code>tests/acceptance/notification/</code> for the quick view and the fallback.</li></ul><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> the issue list and gantt (next issue), scrolling to the comment or time log in the quick view, and milestone and project notifications.</p>", "type": "Task", "priority": "medium", "complexity": "complex", "dependsOn": ["notif-backfill"], "milestone": "Claude-v1", "newMilestone": false, "screens": []}, {"key": "preview-list-gantt", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "Notifications: issue list and gantt open a notification's issue in their quick views", "description": "<p>The <code>issue-preview</code> service (previous issue) lets the open screen take a notification click by issue id and show the issue in its quick view; the task board already uses it. This issue registers the issue list and the gantt as preview views.</p><ul><li><strong>Issue list:</strong> <code>routes/app/project/issue/index.js</code> registers in <code>setupController</code> and disposes in <code>resetController</code> on exit. <code>hasIssue(issueId)</code> checks the rows currently loaded in <code>controllers/app/project/issue/index.js</code>. <code>open</code> calls its <code>selectIssue(issue)</code>.</li><li><strong>Gantt:</strong> <code>routes/app/project/gantt.js</code> registers and disposes in the same way. <code>hasIssue</code> checks the loaded gantt issues. <code>open</code> calls <code>openIssuePanel(issue)</code> in <code>controllers/app/project/gantt.js</code>.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance criteria</strong></p><ul><li>On the issue list or the gantt, clicking a notification whose issue is shown there opens that screen's quick view or panel.</li><li>Otherwise the click opens the issue detail page.</li><li>Tests for each screen: registration is disposed, and both the quick view and fallback paths are covered.</li></ul><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> loading issues that aren't on the current page or filter, and other screens.</p>", "type": "Task", "priority": "medium", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": ["preview-service-board"], "milestone": "Claude-v1", "newMilestone": false, "screens": []}]}	created	\N	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-10-24 08:53:51	2026-10-24 14:07:14	f
35904f456aec-8a66-ed75-4fffff3c30a3	ff0998196aec-c6f1-ed75-2f407967e79a	1	{"summary": "One Gaia issue: bake docker/php/conf.d/custom.ini (memory_limit = 512M) into the deployed image (docker/dev/Dockerfile) and the local image (Dockerfile). Today it only reaches containers through docker-compose bind mounts, so the deployed Gaia runs with PHP's default memory limit.", "issues": [{"key": "php-ini-in-images", "project": "A Gaia", "projectCode": "agaia", "subject": "Docker: copy custom.ini (memory_limit 512M) into the Gaia images", "description": "<p><code>docker/php/conf.d/custom.ini</code> sets <code>memory_limit = 512M</code>, but only the bind mounts in <code>docker-compose.yaml</code> apply it. Neither Dockerfile copies it, so the deployed image built from <code>docker/dev/Dockerfile</code> runs with the base image's default memory limit.</p><ul><li><code>docker/dev/Dockerfile</code>: add <code>COPY ./docker/php/conf.d/custom.ini /usr/local/etc/php/conf.d/zz-custom.ini</code> next to the existing <code>zz-opcache.ini</code> copy.</li><li><code>Dockerfile</code> (local build): add the same <code>COPY</code> so the image is correct without compose too. The existing compose mounts stay as they are.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance criteria</strong></p><ul><li>An image built from <code>docker/dev/Dockerfile</code> reports <code>memory_limit =&gt; 512M</code> from <code>docker run --rm &lt;image&gt; php -i | grep memory_limit</code>, with no volumes mounted.</li><li>The same holds for an image built from <code>Dockerfile</code>.</li><li>The local compose stack still starts and reports 512M.</li></ul><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> changing the base image in <code>docker-images/gaia</code>, other ini settings and the compose mounts.</p>", "type": "Task", "priority": "medium", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": [], "milestone": "Claude-v1", "newMilestone": false, "estimatedFiles": 2, "sizeReason": "", "screens": []}]}	proposed	\N	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-11-05 07:24:37	2026-11-05 07:24:37	f
c9a12aa16af5-fdcf-d19d-7ea8069d0690	8870b9e26af5-dbba-d19d-df4cdc28a203	2	{"summary": "One Gaia fix, written as a request from A Prometheus. Upload reads (GET /upload, GET /upload/:id) return fileThumbnail as a base64 data URI, the same as issue reads with included uploads. Today they return the raw 0/1 flag. The preview modal's token request then overwrites the thumbnail in the Ember store with \\"1\\", so the thumbnail breaks after the modal closes. Prometheus needs no change.", "issues": [{"key": "upload-read-thumbnail", "project": "A Gaia", "projectCode": "agaia", "subject": "Uploads: return fileThumbnail as a data URI on upload reads, not the raw 0/1 flag", "description": "<p><strong>Request from A Prometheus.</strong> On the issue detail page, clicking an attachment opens the preview modal (<code>Issue::FilePreview</code>). The modal fetches a one-time token with <code>GET /api/v1/upload/:id?download=true</code>. Ember Data merges that response into the same <code>upload</code> record the attachment list uses. The response has <code>fileThumbnail: \\"1\\"</code> (the raw DB flag), not the base64 data URI the issue read returned. So after the modal closes, the thumbnail renders as <code>&lt;img src=\\"1\\"&gt;</code> and shows as a broken image. Prometheus needs <code>fileThumbnail</code> to mean the same thing on every read.</p><p><strong>Cause:</strong> <code>FilethumbComponent::afterRead</code> (<code>app/api/v1/controllers/components/FilethumbComponent.php</code>) only converts upload entries in <code>finalData['included']</code>. <code>IssueController</code> and <code>WikiController</code> use it, but <code>UploadController</code> (<code>$uses = ['Fileattachment']</code>, custom <code>getAction</code>/<code>listAction</code>) does not. Its reads return the smallint flag.</p><p><strong>Proposed change</strong> (please accept, adapt or decline):</p><ul><li>Extend <code>FilethumbComponent::afterRead</code> so it also converts <code>type == 'upload'</code> entries in <code>finalData['data']</code>, handling both a list and a single resource. Move the conversion into one private method shared by <code>data</code> and <code>included</code>.</li><li>Add <code>'Filethumb'</code> to <code>UploadController::$uses</code>. Check that <code>getAction</code> and <code>listAction</code> fire <code>rest:afterRead</code> after <code>finalData</code> is built (<code>getAction</code> does; verify <code>listAction</code>). The <code>downloadLink</code> attribute must still be returned.</li><li>If the thumbnail file under <code>filesystem/uploads/thumbnail/thumb_&lt;id&gt;.jpg</code> is missing or unreadable, return <code>\\"0\\"</code> (the value the UI already reads as \\"no thumbnail\\") instead of a data URI of an empty string. Apply the same rule to issue and wiki reads.</li><li>Keep the <code>\\"0\\"</code>/null behaviour for uploads without a thumbnail.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance criteria</strong></p><ul><li><code>GET /upload/:id</code> and <code>GET /upload/:id?download=true</code> for an image with a thumbnail return <code>fileThumbnail</code> starting with <code>data:</code>, identical to the value in the issue read's <code>included</code>.</li><li><code>GET /upload</code> (list) does the same for every upload with a thumbnail.</li><li>Uploads without a thumbnail, or whose thumbnail file is missing, return <code>\\"0\\"</code>.</li><li>Issue and wiki reads are unchanged except for the missing-file case.</li><li>A test (api-tester case and/or unit test) fails before the fix and passes after. gaia-dev graph and generated docs are refreshed (the <code>uses</code> list of <code>upload</code> changes).</li></ul><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> replacing the flag/data-URI field with a thumbnail URL endpoint, and any Prometheus changes.</p>", "type": "Task", "priority": "medium", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": [], "milestone": "aga-v2", "newMilestone": false, "estimatedFiles": 4, "sizeReason": "", "screens": []}]}	created	\N	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-11-12 14:44:25	2026-11-12 14:45:53	f
40c5e2516ae0-a6b8-29af-874af1ca346c	afa54b056ada-2ec4-1fa7-d7b41cc59b57	2	{"summary": "One Prometheus bug fix for aprom-4102: the assignee dropdown on task board cards never opens because the compact Issue::AssigneeSelect stops the click before Bootstrap's document-level dropdown handler sees it. Remove that stopPropagation, add a caret so users can see the avatar is clickable, make sure the menu isn't clipped by the lane, and add a test that opens the menu through the avatar.", "issues": [{"key": "card-assignee-menu-fix", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "Task board: card assignee dropdown never opens (click stopped before Bootstrap sees it)", "description": "<p>aprom-4102 added the shared <code>Issue::AssigneeSelect</code> component and uses it in compact form on task board cards (<code>components/app-ui/task-board/milestones.hbs</code> ~l.126). On the board, clicking a card's assignee avatar does nothing: no menu opens and nothing shows that the avatar is clickable. The issue detail view, which uses the non-compact form, works.</p><p><strong>Cause</strong>: the wrapper <code>.dropdown</code> in <code>components/issue/assignee-select.hbs</code> has <code>{{on \\"click\\" this.stopPropagation}}</code>. When <code>@compact</code> is set, <code>stopPropagation</code> (<code>assignee-select.js</code> ~l.40) stops the event. Bootstrap 3 opens dropdowns from one delegated <code>click.bs.dropdown.data-api</code> listener on <code>document</code>, so the click from <code>[data-toggle=\\"dropdown\\"]</code> never gets there. Stopping the click isn't needed: the card has no click handler (the quick view opens only from the title <code>LinkTo</code>), and Sortable already ignores the dropdown (<code>modifiers/initialize-sortable.js</code> ~l.582, <code>filter: '[data-issue-assignee-dropdown]'</code>, <code>preventOnFilter: false</code>).</p><p><strong>Changes</strong></p><ul><li>Start with a test that fails: an acceptance scenario on the board (Mirage) that clicks the avatar trigger on a card (<code>[data-issue-assignee-toggle]</code>), checks that the dropdown gets the <code>open</code> class and that its menu is visible, then picks a member and checks the new avatar.</li><li>In <code>assignee-select.hbs</code>, remove the <code>click</code> handler from the wrapper. Keep the <code>mousedown</code> one only if a test shows it's needed to stop a drag; otherwise remove it and the <code>stopPropagation</code> action.</li><li>Compact trigger: add a small <code>fa fa-caret-down</code> after the avatar so it reads as a dropdown, the same way the detail view does. Size and space it in the <code>&amp;.assignee-select-compact</code> block in <code>styles/modules/issue.scss</code> (~l.639) so the card footer layout stays unchanged.</li><li>Check in the running app that the menu is fully visible on the last card of a lane. The lane body (<code>.milestone.box-body</code>, <code>styles/modules/board.scss</code> ~l.69) has <code>overflow-x: auto</code>, which also clips vertically. If the menu is cut off, open it upward on cards (Bootstrap's <code>dropup</code> on the compact wrapper). Don't change the lane's overflow or z-index.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance criteria</strong></p><ul><li>On the task board, clicking a card's assignee avatar (or caret) opens the member menu. Choosing a member saves the issue and updates the avatar, with the existing toasts.</li><li>Clicking the avatar doesn't open the quick view. Dragging the card by its body still works, before and after a reassignment.</li><li>The menu is fully visible for the first and last card of a lane.</li><li>The issue detail view's assignee dropdown is unchanged. Existing tests and lint pass.</li></ul><p><strong>Out of scope</strong>: other quick edits on the card, and live updates from other users (aprom-3978).</p>", "type": "Bug", "priority": "high", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": [], "milestone": "Claude-v1", "newMilestone": false, "screens": []}]}	proposed	\N	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	Agent Nestor	2026-10-27 16:07:28	2026-10-27 16:07:28	f
175f88a66ae5-27dd-83ef-ba9503f03470	6471d1b46ae5-10d2-613b-e3f2313332d0	4	{"summary": "Three independent Prometheus issues in aga-v1. screen-preview makes the screens Nestor designs visible in the idea thread and the plan box before any issue is created, by showing the full image (via the download token, as inline images already do) instead of the broken thumbnail. plan-review adds a review mode to the idea page that gives the plan the wide column and the conversation the narrow one. browser-collapse lets the ideas list collapse so the idea page uses the full width.", "issues": [{"key": "screen-preview", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "Idea screens: show the image in the thread and plan box, not a broken thumbnail", "description": "<p>Agent Nestor designs UI screens during an idea. Talos renders them to PNG, uploads them as Uploads of the idea and attaches them to the message (<code>Ideamessage.screens</code>: uploadId, key, title, version, width, height). Engineers can't see these screens on the idea page, which makes them useless at the ideation stage: they only see them once issues are created with the PNGs attached.</p><p>Likely cause (reproduce first with a failing test): <code>Idea::MessageScreen</code> (<code>components/idea/message-screen.{hbs,js}</code>) uses <code>services/upload-preview.js</code> <code>thumbnail()</code>, which returns <code>upload.fileThumbnail</code> as the image <code>src</code>. In Gaia <code>Upload.fileThumbnail</code> is a <strong>bool</strong> (<code>gaia/app/metadata/model/Upload.php</code> ~l.126). Only the POST response carries a base64 thumbnail, and that one is a 198x132 crop. On page load the <code>img</code> is therefore broken or missing, and the only way to see the screen is clicking through to a new tab.</p><ul><li>Show each screen as its full image, scaled to the available width, using <code>uploadPreview.previewUrl(uploadId)</code> (download token + <code>/preview/get/&lt;token&gt;</code>). That is how <code>modifiers/render-markdown.js</code> shows pasted images, and it works. Show the title and version (<code>views.app.idea.conversation.screenVersion</code>) under it, and the <code>fa-picture-o</code> placeholder while it loads or if it fails.</li><li>A click still opens the full-size image in a new tab (existing <code>open</code>).</li><li>Remove the <code>thumbnail()</code> use, or the method itself if nothing else uses it. Don't treat the bool as an address anywhere.</li><li>Message thread: a screen spans the message width (<code>.idea-message-screen</code> in <code>styles/modules/idea.scss</code> is a fixed 160px today). Plan box screen row (<code>components/idea/plan-box.hbs</code> ~l.23, same component): keep a compact size that fits the side column; the review-mode issue widens it.</li><li>Guard the async load after destroy (check <code>isDestroying</code>/<code>isDestroyed</code> after the await).</li><li>Tests: integration test for <code>Idea::MessageScreen</code>. With a Mirage upload whose <code>fileThumbnail</code> is <code>true</code>, it renders an <code>img</code> whose <code>src</code> is the preview address, not <code>true</code>. On a failed token it shows the placeholder.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> on the idea page, every screen in Nestor's messages and in the plan box shows its image as soon as the page loads, before any plan is approved. Click opens it full size.</p><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> an in-app lightbox, Gaia thumbnail generation, Talos changes.</p>", "type": "Bug", "priority": "high", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": [], "milestone": "aga-v1", "newMilestone": false, "estimatedFiles": 6, "sizeReason": "", "screens": []}, {"key": "plan-review", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "Idea page: plan review mode with the plan in the wide column", "description": "<p>The idea page (<code>templates/app/project/idea/page.hbs</code>) puts the conversation in <code>col-md-8</code> and the Plan, Created Issues and Summary boxes in <code>col-md-4</code>. The page itself sits in the <code>col-md-9</code> beside the ideas browser. That suits chatting, but reviewing a plan with several issues in about a quarter of the screen is hard. Engineers need to switch to a layout that favours the plan.</p><ul><li>Add a box-tool button to the Plan box header (<code>components/idea/plan-box.hbs</code>, <code>btn btn-box-tool</code>, <code>fa-expand</code> / <code>fa-compress</code>, title from <code>translations/en-us.json</code>) that toggles review mode through an action passed from the page.</li><li>Review mode: the side column (Plan, Created Issues, Summary) becomes <code>col-md-8</code> and the conversation <code>col-md-4</code>, with the plan column first, so the plan is on the left. The composer stays usable. Normal mode is today's layout.</li><li>State: query param <code>view=plan</code> on the page route (<code>controllers/app/project/idea/page.js</code>), so the mode survives reload and can be linked. Opening another idea keeps the mode. Leaving the ideas route resets it.</li><li>The plan's screen row and issue rows use the extra width (screens larger than in the side column). No new colours or components: reuse the plan box markup and classes in <code>styles/modules/idea.scss</code>.</li><li>Tests: acceptance cases in <code>tests/acceptance/ideas/idea-plan.feature</code>: the button switches the columns, a reload with <code>?view=plan</code> opens in review mode, toggling back restores the layout.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> from any idea with a plan, one click gives the plan the wide column and the conversation the narrow one. Another click goes back. Approve and version tabs work in both modes.</p><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> collapsing the ideas browser (separate issue), resizable columns.</p>", "type": "Task", "priority": "medium", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": [], "milestone": "aga-v1", "newMilestone": false, "estimatedFiles": 8, "sizeReason": "", "screens": []}, {"key": "browser-collapse", "project": "A Prometheus", "projectCode": "aprom", "subject": "Ideas: collapse the ideas browser so the idea page uses the full width", "description": "<p>The ideas browser takes <code>col-md-3</code> of the ideas layout (<code>templates/app/project/idea.hbs</code>), so an open idea (conversation and plan) only gets <code>col-md-9</code>. Engineers working in one idea want the full width for the conversation and the plan.</p><ul><li>Add a collapse button to the browser box header (<code>box-tools</code>, <code>btn btn-box-tool</code>, <code>fa-angle-double-left</code>, title in <code>translations/en-us.json</code>) next to the \\"New\\" button.</li><li>Collapsed: the browser column is hidden and the outlet column becomes <code>col-md-12</code>. A small box-tool button (<code>fa-angle-double-right</code>) stays in the idea page's content header or at the left of the outlet to expand it again.</li><li>State: a tracked flag on <code>controllers/app/project/idea.js</code>, kept in <code>localStorage</code> so the choice survives reloads. Default expanded.</li><li>The New Idea page (create route) and the idea page both follow the flag.</li><li>Styles, if any, scoped under <code>.idea-wrapper</code> in <code>styles/modules/idea.scss</code>. Square edges, existing colours.</li><li>Tests: acceptance cases in <code>tests/acceptance/ideas/idea-browser.feature</code>: collapse hides the list and widens the content, expand restores it, the state survives a reload.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> one click hides the ideas list and the idea page spans the content area. One click brings it back. The choice is remembered.</p><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> plan review mode (separate issue), mobile layout changes.</p>", "type": "Task", "priority": "medium", "complexity": "simple", "dependsOn": [], "milestone": "aga-v1", "newMilestone": false, "estimatedFiles": 5, "sizeReason": "", "screens": []}]}	