176d38836adf-21a7-0f35-a77df707330a	p4m test api/phpunit: copy Gaia into the test container instead of bind-mounting it from Windows (~25× faster requests), enable OPcache	2026-10-26 21:45:59	2026-10-26 22:18:41	0	<p><b>Problem.</b> A full <code>ai test gaia</code> takes about 10 minutes. Measured 2026-10-27: the api suite took 9.5 min for ~470 cases; acl 112 cases in 2m16s, client 207 in 4m11s, so about <b>1.2 s per request</b>. Seeding (≤1 min) and phpunit (~5 s) are minor. That is Claude Code's whole foreground Bash window, and a baseline re-run adds another ~16 min (see adt-4143).</p>\n<p><b>Root cause: the Windows bind mount.</b> <code>testenv.BindTestContainer</code> runs the gaia-test image with <code>D:/…/gaia</code> bind-mounted at <code>/var/www/html</code> (<code>vendor/</code> is already a volume). On Docker Desktop, every file read and stat crosses the host file-sharing layer, and Gaia touches hundreds of PHP files plus metadata and config per request. Apache adds <code>.htaccess</code> lookups per directory. Benchmark of <code>GET /api/v1/issue</code> (401, no auth) on the same container, PHP built-in server, 5 requests each:</p>\n<table border="1" cellpadding="4">\n<tr><th>source</th><th>Xdebug</th><th>OPcache</th><th>per request</th></tr>\n<tr><td>bind mount</td><td>develop</td><td>off</td><td>~0.74 s</td></tr>\n<tr><td>bind mount</td><td>off</td><td>off</td><td>~0.74 s</td></tr>\n<tr><td>bind mount</td><td>off</td><td>on, validate_timestamps=0</td><td>~0.39–0.54 s</td></tr>\n<tr><td>copy in container (/tmp)</td><td>develop</td><td>off</td><td>~0.035 s</td></tr>\n<tr><td>copy in container (/tmp)</td><td>off</td><td>off</td><td>~0.026 s</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Through Apache on the mount the same request takes 1.05–1.28 s. OPcache is installed (<code>opcache.so</code>) but not loaded. Xdebug (<code>develop</code>) and xhprof cost only a few ms.</p>\n<p><b>What</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Copied-source Gaia test container</b>, modelled on <code>PrometheusTestContainer</code> (testenv.py):\n<ul>\n<li>One container per checkout (<code>p4m-test-gaia-…</code>), same image, network, env, volumes (<code>vendor</code>…) and non-source binds as gaia-test, but no bind mount at <code>/var/www/html</code>.</li>\n<li>Before each <code>p4m test api|phpunit</code>, incrementally sync tracked and untracked-not-ignored files with a manifest (reuse <code>sync()</code> / <code>_tracked_files</code>), and delete removed files.</li>\n<li>Always use it for tests, even when the shared gaia-test serves the same checkout.</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li><b>Runtime files git ignores:</b> list what Gaia needs that <code>--exclude-standard</code> drops (local config/.env, <code>logs/</code>, <code>output/</code>, upload dirs…). Copy or create those explicitly. Keep <code>logs</code> and <code>output</code> writable (777), as today.</li>\n<li><b>Results back to the checkout:</b>\n<ul>\n<li>copy <code>/var/www/html/output/api-tester-*-report.json</code> back to <code>&lt;src&gt;/output</code> after the run (<code>ai test --explain</code> reads them);</li>\n<li>copy the regenerated <code>tools/api-tester/apis/backend.json</code> back after <code>--regenerate-backend</code>;</li>\n<li>check whether <code>prepare-test-db.sh</code> (run on the host) writes fixtures into the checkout; if so, sync <i>after</i> prepare.</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li><b>OPcache in the test container:</b> <code>zend_extension=opcache</code>, <code>opcache.enable=1</code>, <code>opcache.validate_timestamps=1</code> with <code>revalidate_freq=0</code> (or <code>validate_timestamps=0</code> plus <code>opcache_reset</code> / Apache graceful after each sync). Use an ini dropped into the test container only, not the dev gaia container.</li>\n<li><b>Xdebug off</b> for Apache in the test container (<code>XDEBUG_MODE=off</code>, as phpunit already does).</li>\n<li>Baseline runs (<code>ai/lib/baseline.py</code>) get the same container type for the baseline worktree, and <code>p4m test clean</code> removes it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Benefits besides speed:</b> the run tests a snapshot, so edits made during a background run no longer leak into later cases (see adt-4143's fingerprint check).</p>\n<p><b>Checks:</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>unittest for the sync and the output copy-back (mock docker);</li>\n<li>a full <code>ai test gaia</code> passes with the same counts as today (backend 142/0/12, acl 112/0/0, client 201/0/6) and the api suite takes under ~2 min (target; record the measured time on the issue);</li>\n<li>edit a file, re-run <code>ai test gaia --case …</code>, and confirm the change is picked up;</li>\n<li><code>ai test gaia --explain</code> still finds reports;</li>\n<li>Linux/macOS still work.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Depends on:</b> nothing; independent of adt-4142 and adt-4143. If this lands first, adt-4143's background-run change becomes a safety net, not a necessity.</p><p>Complexity: complex</p>	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	1	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	1	4144	2026-11-03	2026-10-27	done	high	9f00a9366ad7-404c-ce18-9081c0a9fec6	8fe2ae046adf-8154-4904-8f5112ed5746	\N	a13debc86ad7-404c-ce18-75327b747d1b	Agent Achilles	Hammad Hassan	a13ae1896adf-3957-9a0f-b36fac753a0d	402f48b562c6-8e43-a8e0-0abdb1b2f5be	2026-10-26 22:18:42	adt	0	0
469926936ae5-a3a8-9f4e-6a8023f600e1	Agamemnon: post every key action as an issue comment (the issue's episodic memory)	2026-10-31 12:51:18	2026-10-31 14:19:58	0	<p>The internal journal is for undo; the issue's comments are the record people read. Every tier 1 and tier 2 action by <code>aga</code> posts a comment on the issue it acted on, as Agent Agamemnon:</p><ul><li>what it saw (incident type and the evidence in one or two lines),</li><li>what it did,</li><li>how to undo it (the journal id and the <code>talos agamemnon undo</code> command),</li><li>what happens next (waiting on, sent to, retry at).</li></ul><p>Several actions in one incident go in one comment. When the incident is escalated to the owner, the comment says why and what was already tried.</p><p>The incident packet includes Agamemnon's earlier comments on the issue, so it reads its own history before acting and does not repeat a step that already failed.</p><p>Depends on: adt-4230</p><p>Complexity: simple</p>	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	1	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	1	4231	2026-11-07	2026-10-31	done	medium	9f00a9366ad7-404c-ce18-9081c0a9fec6	c36638756ae5-92fb-c743-0dd51d887bec	\N	a13debc86ad7-404c-ce18-75327b747d1b	Agent Achilles	Hammad Hassan	3e6cdd866ae5-f697-8e20-90d891f67142	402f48b562c6-8e43-a8e0-0abdb1b2f5be	2026-10-31 14:19:58	adt	0	0
de7250786ad9-192a-43e2-6ab6cd258fa9	Allowlist the six idea events	2026-10-22 00:25:02	2026-10-22 07:05:18	0	<p>Gaia starts publishing idea events (linked Gaia issue in this milestone) so Prometheus and Talos can react to messages, plans and created issues. Hermes must allow them; they are project-scoped like the issue events, so membership authorization is unchanged.</p><ul><li>Add to <code>EVENT_ALLOWLIST</code> in <code>src/contract/names.js</code>: <code>idea.created</code>, <code>idea.updated</code>, <code>idea.message.created</code>, <code>idea.plan.created</code>, <code>idea.plan.status.changed</code>, <code>idea.planissue.created</code>. Not user-scoped.</li><li>ADR entry in <code>docs/architecture/decisions.md</code>: the names, that they are project-scoped, and that <code>meta.ideaId</code> identifies the idea (envelope unchanged).</li><li>Tests: intents for the new names are accepted for a project member and rejected for a non-member; a publish with each name passes validation.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> tests and lint pass; PR links the Gaia PR and merges with it.</p><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> any path for agents to publish, <code>idea.agent.progress</code>.</p><p>Complexity: complex</p>	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	1	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	1	4089	2026-10-29	2026-10-22	done	high	861790376ad5-1449-a602-a25bd5e6e2b8	dda9d2546ad9-192a-43e2-7b8750d5b2d3	\N	87099ee46ad5-1449-a602-5f7a1f6605b7	Agent Nestor	Hammad Hassan	b53c75f56ad9-1e8b-8fcc-7f6795b95fe0	402f48b562c6-8e43-a8e0-0abdb1b2f5be	2026-10-22 07:05:18	ah	0	0
431508646ad9-89f5-f535-791288e0bc8a	p4m test ember: build wait misses a fast rebuild (waits 15 min), and a second hidden watch build runs in the test container	2026-10-21 22:14:27	2026-10-21 23:07:09	0	<p>Found while working aprom-4073. A full <code>ai test prometheus</code> sat for 15 minutes without running a test, then failed with "test build did not finish". Two bugs in <code>dev-tools/installer/testenv.py</code> (<code>PrometheusTestContainer</code>) caused it.</p><p><strong>1. A race in <code>build()</code>.</strong> <code>p4m test ember</code> calls <code>sync()</code> (copy the changed files into the container), then <code>build(changed)</code>. <code>build()</code> reads the number of "Build successful" lines in <code>/tmp/p4m-build.log</code> as its starting point only after the copy. The warm <code>ember build --watch</code> picks up the copied files straight away. A small change rebuilds in about 6 s, which is faster than the <code>docker exec</code> calls of the copy on Windows. So the rebuild finishes before the starting point is read, and <code>done &gt; before</code> never becomes true. The loop then re-reads the log every second until its 900 s limit. Seen on 2026-10-21: rebuild done at 22:03:57 UTC, <code>p4m</code> still waiting at 22:09, killed by hand.</p><p><strong>2. A second, invisible watch build.</strong> The test container had an orphaned <code>ember build --watch</code> from a day earlier (pid 2214, about 2.9 GB, parent pid 1, cwd <code>/prometheus</code>, still holding <code>/tmp/p4m-build.log</code> open). Its process name had changed to plain <code>ember</code>, so <code>pgrep -f '[e]mber build'</code> doesn't match it, and <code>_builder_running()</code> didn't know about it. Two watchers rebuilt into the same <code>/tmp/p4m-test-dist</code> and wrote over each other in the same log. That makes the build count unreliable, the output folder can end up half-written, and swap filled up.</p><p>Changes:</p><ul><li>Take the starting point (the log's size or its build count) <em>before</em> <code>sync()</code>, and pass it to <code>build()</code>. Alternatively, have the build write a marker to wait for, such as a build number or the time of the copy.</li><li>When files were copied in, also accept a completed build whose log lines come after the copy time. Only time out if the watcher has really been idle since the copy.</li><li>Find watch builds by more than their command line. For example, record the pid of the build <code>p4m</code> starts (in the container's state file), and treat any other <code>ember</code> process with <code>cwd=/prometheus</code> that holds <code>/tmp/p4m-build.log</code> open as stale. Kill stale ones before starting or using a watch build.</li><li>Log the wait with progress (e.g. every 30 s: "waiting for the test build, N builds seen, last log write Xs ago"), so an agent or the engineer can see what's happening.</li><li>Tests in <code>installer/tests</code>: a rebuild that finishes before <code>build()</code> starts (fake log), and a stale watcher detected and killed.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> change one file and run <code>ai test prometheus</code>; the tests start within about 15 s of the copy. A container with an extra <code>ember</code> watcher is cleaned up, leaving one <code>ember build --watch</code>.</p><p>Related: adt-4081 (hangs inside <code>ember test</code>: a mangled filter, a filter that matches nothing, a test file that fails to load).</p><p>Complexity: simple</p>	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	1	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	1	4082	2026-10-29	2026-10-22	done	high	9f00a9366ad7-404c-ce18-9081c0a9fec6	84aeed166ad8-5eae-b551-f3f3cea8503c	\N	a0df54476ad7-404c-ce18-8b3ef9fc16e9	Agent Achilles	Hammad Hassan	7ed614756ad9-9295-3816-6097511468dc	402f48b562c6-8e43-a8e0-0abdb1b2f5be	2026-10-21 23:07:09	adt	0	0
9734a6976ad6-6765-7e60-b51240b481d0	Email settings: test flag on the settings save (send a test email with the submitted settings, save nothing)	2026-10-19 18:25:27	2026-10-19 19:19:58	0	<p>Follow-up to aprom-3964 (Settings: Email page, prometheus#532) and agaia-3959. Lets admins check their SMTP settings by sending a test email <strong>without saving them</strong> and <strong>without a new route or controller</strong>. The custom <code>setting/testemail</code> action was dropped in review of gaia#183 (CRUD-only rule), so the test goes through the existing settings save instead.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Approach</strong></p>\n<p>Add a test flag to the existing save endpoint, <code>PATCH /api/v1/setting</code> (<code>SettingController</code> → <code>RestController::patchCollectionAction</code>, ACL <code>setting.update</code>). When the flag is set, the endpoint sends a test email using the submitted settings and saves nothing.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Request</strong> (proposed contract; Prometheus builds against it)</p>\n<pre>PATCH /api/v1/setting\n{\n  "collection": [ { "id": "&lt;setting id&gt;", "settingValue": "..." }, ... ],\n  "test": true,\n  "recipient": "admin@example.com"\n}</pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>collection</code>: the current form values for the <code>email</code> group, saved or not.</li>\n<li>A blank or missing <code>email.smtpPassword</code> means "use the stored password", the same as Save.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Scope</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>No <code>test</code> flag:</strong> exactly today's behaviour (save in one transaction, <code>rest:afterUpdate</code> per record, same response).</li>\n<li><strong><code>test: true</code></strong> (override in <code>SettingController</code>; <code>RestController</code> stays generic):\n  <ul>\n  <li>Run the same per-record checks as a save (<code>authorizeRecordUpdate</code>, record lookup by bound id). Only rows in the <code>email</code> group may be part of a test.</li>\n  <li>Build the SMTP values from the submitted rows. For each value the submission doesn't give, use the stored setting, then the config. Reuse <code>SmtpSettings::resolve()</code> and decrypt the stored password the same way <code>SmtpSettings::load()</code> does.</li>\n  <li>Send one test message to <code>recipient</code> with <code>new Mailer($timeout, $smtp)</code>, which already takes the SMTP array.</li>\n  <li><strong>Never save:</strong>\n    <ul>\n    <li>no model <code>save()</code>;</li>\n    <li>no open transaction left behind;</li>\n    <li>no <code>rest:afterUpdate</code>, so no audit rows, activity, notification or live event.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li>Never log or return the password, and don't echo the SMTP conversation (no <code>SMTPDebug</code>).</li>\n  </ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Result: codes only, no display text</strong> (Prometheus translates them). Each result has one code and one HTTP status:\n  <ul>\n  <li><code>TEST_EMAIL_SENT</code>: 200</li>\n  <li><code>INVALID_RECIPIENT</code>: 422. A missing or invalid <code>recipient</code>, checked before connecting.</li>\n  <li><code>SMTP_AUTH_FAILED</code>: 502. The server rejected the credentials.</li>\n  <li><code>SMTP_CONNECTION_FAILED</code>: 502. The host or port can't be reached, or TLS/SSL fails.</li>\n  <li><code>UNKNOWN_ERROR</code>: 502. Any other failure. Log the real error server-side.</li>\n  </ul>\n  Proposed body: <code>{"testResult": "&lt;CODE&gt;"}</code>. Map the PHPMailer error with one small mapper class (single responsibility), not inline in the controller.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Acceptance criteria</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A test never changes stored settings: rows, <code>modifiedDate</code> and audit are unchanged before and after.</li>\n<li>Save without the flag behaves exactly as before. The existing api-tester setting cases pass unchanged.</li>\n<li>Every result above is reachable and returns only its code.</li>\n<li>A test requires <code>setting.update</code> (403 without it), the same as Save.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Tests</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>PHPUnit:\n  <ul>\n  <li>the error-code mapper (each PHPMailer error class → code);</li>\n  <li>resolving submitted values over stored values over config, including a blank password keeping the stored one.</li>\n  </ul>\n</li>\n<li>api-tester client cases:\n  <ul>\n  <li><code>INVALID_RECIPIENT</code>;</li>\n  <li><code>SMTP_CONNECTION_FAILED</code> (host 127.0.0.1:1);</li>\n  <li><code>TEST_EMAIL_SENT</code> against the SMTP sink from agaia-3974;</li>\n  <li><code>SMTP_AUTH_FAILED</code>, if the sink can reject auth;</li>\n  <li>settings unchanged after each test;</li>\n  <li>save without the flag unchanged.</li>\n  </ul>\n</li>\n<li>acl case: a test without <code>setting.update</code> → 403.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Notes</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>patchCollectionAction</code> line 776 checks <code>!isset($data-&gt;collection) &amp;&amp; is_array($data-&gt;collection)</code>, which is never true, so a missing collection isn't rejected. Fix it here if the override touches it, or log it separately.</li>\n<li>On live, the <code>email.*</code> rows from <code>db/seeds/settings.sql</code> aren't present yet (the CI/CD seed sync). The test depends on those rows.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Blocks</strong>: the Prometheus "Test Settings" follow-up to aprom-3964.</p><p>Complexity: complex</p>	8a024ab56ad5-2c2e-412a-dd21fa8ef390	8a024ab56ad5-2c2e-412a-dd21fa8ef390	1	8a024ab56ad5-2c2e-412a-dd21fa8ef390	1	4017	2026-10-26	2026-10-19	done	medium	cb4567905acd-06d6-1141-7dd99b59dd21	92ae09c66ace-1e5c-2b63-d1da9630092c	409cb1b66ace-34bf-02fa-51672a59312d	cbaa72735acd-06d6-1141-98f835ab32e9	Agent Odysseus	Hammad Hassan	c20c09936ad6-cd3f-1ae6-7ff791102f6a	402f48b562c6-8e43-a8e0-0abdb1b2f5be	2026-10-19 19:19:58	agaia	0	0
7614f3856adc-9973-8304-60f14f1e11be	Ideas: approve plan shows loading, closes once saved and keeps Approve disabled unless it fails	2026-10-24 07:17:10	2026-10-24 14:10:39	0	<p>Approving a plan gives no feedback: the modal shows no loading or completion and doesn't close, and the Approve button can be pressed again. Use the async confirm of <code>bootstrap-modal</code> and track the approval in the plan box.</p><ul><li><code>components/idea/plan-box.js</code>: a tracked <code>isApproving</code>. Once the user confirms, <code>confirmApprove</code> sets it and returns the Promise of <code>this.args.onApprove(plan)</code>. bootstrap-modal shows the spinner with disabled buttons and closes <strong>as soon as the approval is saved</strong>. It doesn't wait for the issues to be created. On rejection the controller (<code>controllers/app/project/idea/page.js</code> <code>approvePlan</code>) has already reported the error and rolled back. The modal stays open with its buttons back, and <code>isApproving</code> is cleared.</li><li>The footer Approve button (<code>data-idea-plan-approve</code>) is disabled and shows a spinner from the confirmation until the save ends. It is enabled again only if the approval fails. On success the plan becomes <code>approved</code> and <code>canApprove</code> hides the button. A plan that later turns <code>failed</code> (Talos) shows it again, as today.</li><li>Status label: while the selected plan is <code>approved</code> or <code>creating</code>, show <code>fa fa-spinner fa-spin</code> before the status text, so the user sees that issues are being created after the modal closes. The live <code>idea.plan.status.changed</code> reload already moves it to <code>created</code>/<code>failed</code>.</li><li>aprom-4105 moves the status label into <code>box-tools</code>. Whichever lands second keeps both changes.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> after confirming, the modal shows a spinner with disabled buttons and Approve is disabled. When the save succeeds, the modal closes, the button is gone and the label shows Approved with a spinner, then Created. On a failed save the modal stays open, the error is shown and Approve works again. Double clicks create one approval. Integration tests for the plan box cover success and failure.</p><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> live Talos progress inside the modal, cancelling an approval.</p><p><strong>Depends on:</strong> aprom-4112</p><p>Complexity: simple</p>	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	1	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	1	4113	2026-10-31	2026-10-24	done	high	add12ff55acb-1902-6b9c-30d5411f7729	ac6af3216ad8-d753-535b-b21c3700739d	\N	ae61a7235acb-1902-6b9c-e933ed4dfda0	Agent Nestor	Hammad Hassan	bedd69266adc-9b98-fb54-736a3d11a950	402f48b562c6-8e43-a8e0-0abdb1b2f5be	2026-10-24 14:10:39	aprom	0	0
