1e29f7536a5f-85b6-d50a-b10f0f926a6d	Implement Data-Level Access Control for Relationships	2026-07-21 09:25:50	2026-07-21 17:10:15	0	<p>Update Gaia's backend authorization behavior to support data-level access control for related resources.</p>\n<p>A model can be accessed directly through its own API endpoint or returned as a relationship of another resource. For example, an <code data-backticks="1">Issue</code> can be accessed directly through <code data-backticks="1">GET /api/v1/issues</code> or returned as a relationship of a <code data-backticks="1">Project</code>.</p>\n<p>When a user does not have permission to perform a specific action on a related resource, that resource must not be included in the relationship response. The authorization decision should therefore apply consistently regardless of whether the resource is accessed directly or returned as part of another resource.</p>\n<p>For example, if the user has <code data-backticks="1">0</code> access to the <code data-backticks="1">read</code> action on the <code data-backticks="1">Issue</code> resource:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code data-backticks="1">GET /api/v1/issues</code> → Access should be denied.</li>\n<li><code data-backticks="1">GET /api/v1/projects?rels=issues</code> → Project should be returned, but the <code data-backticks="1">issues</code> relationship should not be included.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This ensures that resource-level authorization and relationship data exposure follow the same security model.<br>\n<br data-tomark-pass=""></p>\n<h3>Functional Requirements</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Implement data-level authorization for resources returned through relationships.</li>\n<li>Evaluate the user's permission for each related resource based on the relevant action.</li>\n<li>If the user does not have access to a related resource, exclude that relationship data from the API response.</li>\n<li>Do not deny access to the parent resource solely because the user lacks access to one of its relationships.</li>\n<li>Apply the same authorization rules consistently for direct resource access and relationship access.</li>\n<li>Ensure unauthorized relationship data is not exposed through nested or chained relationships.</li>\n<li>Support existing <code data-backticks="1">rels</code> behavior without breaking authorized relationship loading.</li>\n<li>Ensure authorization is applied at the backend/API level and cannot be bypassed by manipulating request parameters.</li>\n<li>Avoid unnecessary database queries for relationships that the user is not authorized to access, where practical.</li>\n<li>Maintain consistent API response structures when unauthorized relationships are excluded.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Expected Behavior</h3>\n<p>Given a <code data-backticks="1">Project</code> resource with an <code data-backticks="1">issues</code> relationship:</p>\n<p><strong>Scenario 1 — User has Issue read access</strong><br>\n<br data-tomark-pass=""></p>\n<pre><code>GET /api/v1/projects?rels=issues\n</code></pre>\n<p>Response includes:<br>\n<br data-tomark-pass=""></p>\n<pre><code>Project\n└── issues\n    ├── Issue 1\n    ├── Issue 2\n    └── Issue 3\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Scenario 2 — User does not have Issue read access</strong><br>\n<br data-tomark-pass=""></p>\n<pre><code>GET /api/v1/projects?rels=issues\n</code></pre>\n<p>Response includes:<br>\n<br data-tomark-pass=""></p>\n<pre><code>Project\n</code></pre>\n<p>The <code data-backticks="1">issues</code> relationship must not be returned.</p>\n<p><strong>Scenario 3 — Direct Issue access without permission</strong><br>\n<br data-tomark-pass=""></p>\n<pre><code>GET /api/v1/issues\n</code></pre>\n<p>The request must be denied according to the existing authorization behavior.</p>\n	7ea8b586623c-5e99-bfcb-d51d01a7a16b	7ea8b586623c-5e99-bfcb-d51d01a7a16b	7ea8b586623c-5e99-bfcb-d51d01a7a16b	7ea8b586623c-5e99-bfcb-d51d01a7a16b	1	3908	2026-07-22	2026-07-21	done	medium	cb4567905acd-06d6-1141-7dd99b59dd21	f1d8e6336a5f-5863-d50a-b12ddae1fd5c	\N	cbaa72735ac