625dd7916adb-b6fb-3a5e-83b2e77013ce	Uploads: GET /upload/{id} returns 404 for idea screen uploads	2026-10-23 21:49:22	2026-10-24 14:21:35	0	<p>When a plan is approved, Talos attaches each planned issue's screens by downloading the PNG through gaia-mcp (<code>GET /api/v1/upload/{id}</code> with the agent's Bearer token) and uploading it to the new issue. For idea dc9f28086adb-14fc-3884-5d3000a5198e this failed: <code>Gaia download of upload 664eb6266adb-bb24-3884-1ae4db44bf6e returned 404</code>, with an empty body. The upload was created by gaia-mcp <code>upload_idea_screen</code> (multipart POST /upload, <code>relatedTo=idea</code>, <code>relatedId=&lt;idea id&gt;</code>).</p><ul><li>Find the root cause. Leads: <code>UploadController::getAction</code> (it throws <code>FileNotfound</code> when <code>filePath</code> is missing on disk and <code>ResourceNotFound</code> when the row can't be read), the <code>Upload</code> read under the agent's ACL (the <code>relatedTo idea</code> scope through Idea in <code>app/metadata/model/Upload.php</code> ~l.171, and <code>Acl::assertRecordIdInScope</code>), and whether a GET with an id reaches <code>getAction</code> at all. Also check which response these exceptions produce (the body was empty).</li><li>Start with an api-tester case that fails: upload a PNG related to an idea as a project member, then <code>GET /upload/{id}</code> returns 200 with the bytes. Add a case that a non-member gets 403/404.</li><li>Fix it in Gaia. If the cause turns out to be on the client side (gaia-mcp <code>download</code>, or Talos <code>lib/ideas/approval.py</code>), record the finding on this issue and open an A Dev Tool issue for it instead.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> approving a plan with screens attaches their PNGs to the created issues, with no 'could not be attached' message. The new api-tester cases pass.</p><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> the download-token preview flow (<code>/preview/get</code>) unless it shares the cause