914e56956af1-e797-98d9-ff94b0067065	Talos: a Gaia agent's run uses a delegated token with its current scopes	2026-11-09 13:20:17	2026-11-09 14:52:37	0	<p><strong>Problem.</strong> <code>talos/lib/delegated_tokens.py</code> <code>TokenCache</code> keeps each Gaia agent's delegated token for its full hour and renews it only about 5 minutes before expiry. Gaia fixes a token's scopes when it issues it, so a change to the agent's <code>delegationScopes</code> in Prometheus takes up to an hour to reach the agent.</p>\n<p><strong>Seen on 2026-11-09.</strong> <code>issuestatus.*</code> and <code>issuetype.*</code> were added to Hera after Talos started at 16:41. Hera's 17:25 run on milestone aga-v1 (A Prometheus) still used the 16:41 token, and every <code>gaia_create_issue</code> failed with <em>Gaia GET issuestatus returned 403</em>. The run ended blocked after reading all 79 issues. A freshly granted token for Hera at the same time could read issuestatus and issuetype.</p>\n<p><strong>Change.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Each workflow step run starts on a newly granted delegated token, not a cached one. Renewal during a long run stays as it is.</li>\n<li>Also drop the cached token when discovery (the 300 s poll) sees that an agent's <code>delegationScopes</code> changed.</li>\n<li>Log at run start which scopes the token carries (names only, never the token), so a 403 can be traced to the scopes in use.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Acceptance.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Unit test: a run started after a scope change gets a token from a new grant, not the cached one.</li>\n<li>Unit test: discovery seeing changed scopes forgets the agent's cached token.</li>\n<li>Local agents (Nestor, Odysseus/Achilles, Agamemnon) are not touched; they don't use delegated tokens.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Depends on adt-4411 (same <code>_gaia_agents</code> code).</p><p>Complexity: simple</p>	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	1	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	1	4420	2026-11-16	2026-11-09	done	high	9f00a9366ad7-404c-ce18-9081c0a9fec6	712014526af0-7d1c-cccd-ee6dc2488b11	\N	a13debc86ad7-404c-ce18-75327b747d1b	Agent Achilles	Hammad Hassan	01b326406af1-2d79-98d9-a2a271367220	402f48b562c6-8e43-a8e0-0abdb1b2f5be	2026-11-09 14:52:37	adt	0	0
c90f67706af7-581a-5a96-643be878960f	Add a Server-Timing header to static files served by Apache	2026-11-13 19:36:41	2026-11-13 20:31:01	0	<h3>Why</h3><p>On prom.projects4.me, DevTools shows 1.6–3.75 s of "Waiting for server" for small static files (1.3 KB CSS, the logo PNG, the board HTML), while the server's CPU stays under 10%. Measurements from outside suggest the time is network and HTTP/2 queueing, not Apache:</p><ul><li>On a kept-alive connection each static request takes ~0.25 s, which is one round trip to OVH (192.99.45.92).</li><li>A single connection carries ~250–350 KB/s, and the ~1.5 MB of gzipped assets share one HTTP/2 connection, so small files queue behind the large JS bundles.</li></ul><p>A Server-Timing header lets anyone confirm this from the browser. If DevTools shows Apache spending about 1 ms beside 2.5 s of waiting, the wait is not on the server.</p><h3>What</h3><ul><li>Enable <code>mod_headers</code>: add <code>headers</code> to the <code>a2enmod</code> line in <code>docker/dev/Dockerfile</code> and <code>docker/qa/Dockerfile</code>. It is available in the image but not enabled.</li><li>In <code>docker/apache/prometheus.conf</code>, inside the VirtualHost:<pre>&lt;IfModule mod_headers.c&gt;\n    Header always set Server-Timing "apache;desc=\\"%D\\""\n&lt;/IfModule&gt;</pre></li></ul><p>Apache's <code>%D</code> is the request duration in microseconds and renders as <code>Server-Timing: apache;desc="D=812"</code>. This exact config was checked in a throwaway container from the deployed image (Apache 2.4.38).</p><h3>Acceptance criteria</h3><ul><li>After deploying to dev, every response from prom.projects4.me (HTML, /assets/*, /img/*) carries <code>Server-Timing: apache;desc="D=…"</code>.</li><li>DevTools shows the entry in each request's Timing tab under "Server Timing".</li><li>Nothing else about the responses changes: same status codes, compression and rewrite behaviour.</li></ul><h3>Out of scope</h3><ul><li>Long-lived <code>Cache-Control</code> for the fingerprinted /assets/* files: same file, separate issue.</li><li>CDN, TCP tuning on the node, and bundle size: follow-ups from the same investigation.</li></ul><p>Complexity: simple</p>	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	1	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	1	4543	2026-11-21	2026-11-14	done	medium	add12ff55acb-1902-6b9c-30d5411f7729	6ba71de26ace-1e5c-2b63-25d38169aba6	\N	ae5c14575acb-1902-6b9c-78da78712ee9	Agent Achilles	Hammad Hassan	850cedf46af7-5b5b-7458-b4c076c1b337	402f48b562c6-8e43-a8e0-0abdb1b2f5be	2026-11-13 20:31:01	aprom	0	0
