a3e6a3296af1-35f0-87ea-4a1815d2c3d8	Talos: a Gaia agent runs on the template projects it can see, instead of failing on one it can't	2026-11-09 11:46:43	2026-11-09 13:26:56	0	<p>The gaia-runtime template lists A Gaia, A Prometheus, A Hermes, A Ares and A Dev Tool. Hera is a member of only the first three, so Talos refused to start it: <code>No project named or short-coded 'A Ares' is visible to agent.hera@projects4.me</code>. It now runs only because the local <code>gaia-runtime/agent.local.json</code> narrows <code>projects</code> by hand.</p><p><strong>Change:</strong> when starting a Gaia agent, keep the template projects visible on its delegated token, log the skipped ones once, and fail only when none is visible. Re-check on each discovery round so a new membership is picked up.</p><p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> tests: invisible projects are skipped and logged; no visible project means not started; a new membership is used after the next round.</p><p><strong>Depends on:</strong> adt-4411</p><p><strong>Estimated files:</strong> 3</p><p>Complexity: simple</p>	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	1	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	1	4417	2026-11-16	2026-11-09	done	medium	9f00a9366ad7-404c-ce18-9081c0a9fec6	712014526af0-7d1c-cccd-ee6dc2488b11	\N	a0df54476ad7-404c-ce18-8b3ef9fc16e9	Agent Achilles	Hammad Hassan	a13d66b56af1-db9a-6ff5-4ecb4fa62cd7	402f48b562c6-8e43-a8e0-0abdb1b2f5be	2026-11-09 13:26:56	adt	0	0
cfb2bed06af7-581a-5a96-62ac678ed536	Add a Server-Timing breakdown to API responses (behind SERVER_TIMING)	2026-11-13 19:36:47	2026-11-15 23:12:03	0	<h3>Why</h3><p>Gaia exposes no timing information, so a slow request in DevTools can't be split into network time and server time. Measurements from the recent latency investigation:</p><ul><li>Inside the OVH pod, a simple authenticated list takes ~62 ms, and an unknown route (bootstrap only) ~17 ms.</li><li>From <code>logs/db.log</code>, most database time on a simple request is Phalcon's model-metadata introspection: about 24 ms of ~45 ms, re-run on every request because the metadata store is in-memory per request.</li><li>Real queries take ~1 ms each, plus a new DB connection per request (6.6 ms on OVH).</li></ul><p>A Server-Timing header puts this breakdown in each request's DevTools Timing tab, and the apibench tool already parses it.</p><h3>What</h3><ul><li>A small collector class in <code>core/libs/utils</code> with one responsibility: record named durations and counts, and format them as a Server-Timing value.</li><li>In <code>public/index.php</code>, register it with <code>header_register_callback</code> so it covers the normal response and every exception handler. This was prototyped and works on both 200 and 500 responses in the deployed image.</li><li>Feed it from the existing DB events listener in <code>index.php</code>, which already times every query:<ul><li><code>db</code>: query time, with the query count in desc;</li><li><code>schema</code>: queries hitting <code>information_schema</code> / <code>pg_*</code>, i.e. Phalcon metadata introspection.</li></ul></li><li>Also mark <code>boot</code> (until the application handles the request) and <code>total</code>.</li><li>Example: <code>Server-Timing: boot;dur=7.1, schema;dur=24.0;desc="4 tables", db;dur=9.8;desc="11 queries", total;dur=62.2</code></li><li>Send <code>Timing-Allow-Origin</code> for the Prometheus origin, so the app's own scripts can read the timings cross-origin. DevTools shows them regardless.</li></ul><h3>Configuration and security</h3><ul><li>Off unless the env variable <code>SERVER_TIMING=1</code> is set, read via <code>getenv()</code> in a <code>config/*.php</code> file. Enable it in the dev and QA environments only.</li><li>Detailed timings on <code>/api/v1/token</code> could help an attacker tell existing accounts from missing ones, so it must stay off in production.</li></ul><h3>Acceptance criteria</h3><ul><li>With <code>SERVER_TIMING=1</code>, every API response (success, 4xx, 5xx, OPTIONS excluded) carries a Server-Timing header with boot, db, schema and total entries.</li><li>Without the flag, the header is absent.</li><li>PHPUnit covers the collector's formatting. An api-tester case checks that the header is present with the flag and absent without it.</li><li>No measurable overhead when disabled.</li></ul><h3>Out of scope (follow-ups from the same investigation)</h3><ul><li>Caching the Phalcon model metadata (Stream or APCu adapter): measured 36–66% faster requests locally.</li><li>Gzip for <code>application/json</code>: Apache's deflate.conf in the image skips JSON, so API responses go out uncompressed (3.7 MB raw on a board load).</li><li>Emulated prepares and persistent connections, and throttling the per-request <code>lastActivityAt</code> write.</li></ul><p>Complexity: simple</p>	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	1	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	1	4544	2026-11-21	2026-11-14	done	medium	cb4567905acd-06d6-1141-7dd99b59dd21	92ae09c66ace-1e5c-2b63-d1da9630092c	\N	cbaa72735acd-06d6-1141-98f835ab32e9	Agent Achilles	Hammad Hassan	29aba5876af7-3aef-5948-3c08468f9ea9	402f48b562c6-8e43-a8e0-0abdb1b2f5be	2026-11-15 23:12:03	agaia	0	0
798c0d086af1-35f0-87ea-c90bb0f29375	p4m configure gaia-agents: a step that only writes the Gaia-agent runtime settings	2026-11-09 11:46:01	2026-11-09 14:52:39	0	<p>Talos needs <code>talos/agents/gaia-runtime/agent.local.json</code> (reviewer: <code>acceptAssignmentsFrom</code>, <code>handoffTo</code>, <code>reviewerName</code>) to run Gaia-declared agents. Today only a full <code>p4m configure</code> writes it, and that run also rewrites every credential file.</p><p><strong>Change:</strong> add a sub-command <code>p4m configure gaia-agents</code> that writes only that file (merging into an existing one) and touches nothing else. The full configure keeps calling the same function. README and the Talos "no reviewer configured" message point to the sub-command.</p><p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> test: the sub-command writes only that file; other env/config files are byte-identical afterwards.</p><p><strong>Depends on:</strong> adt-4413</p><p><strong>Estimated files:</strong> 4</p><p>Complexity: simple</p>	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	1	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	1	4414	2026-11-16	2026-11-09	done	medium	9f00a9366ad7-404c-ce18-9081c0a9fec6	712014526af0-7d1c-cccd-ee6dc2488b11	\N	a13debc86ad7-404c-ce18-75327b747d1b	Agent Achilles	Hammad Hassan	fbd59db16af1-cf26-94c2-9735d7719366	402f48b562c6-8e43-a8e0-0abdb1b2f5be	2026-11-09 14:52:39	adt	0	0
c81702936ad8-d753-535b-a0de7e8c1636	Ideas: Plan with versions, Created Issues and Summary side boxes	2026-10-21 14:57:12	2026-10-22 09:33:32	0	<p>The <code>col-md-4</code> side boxes on <code>app.project.idea.page</code> (the right column of the attached <code>idea-page</code> screen).</p><ul><li><strong>Plan</strong> (<code>box-success</code>): the idea's latest <code>ideaplan</code> by default, with <code>nav nav-tabs</code> per version (v1, v2, ...) to read earlier ones; status label pulled right, reusing existing classes: proposed = <code>new</code>, approved = <code>in_review</code>, creating = <code>in_progress</code>, created = <code>done</code>, failed = <code>closed</code> (with <code>error</code> shown), superseded = <code>deferred</code>. Shows <code>body.summary</code> and a condensed table of planned issues: project short code, subject, priority via <code>AppUi::PriorityIcon</code>; a row expands to type, complexity, <code>dependsOn</code> and the description (stored HTML: render sanitized, never raw). An Approve vN button in the box footer on the latest version when its status is proposed or failed and ACL allows <code>ideaplan.update</code>; after confirmation it sets <code>status</code> approved. Empty state when there is no plan yet.</li><li><strong>Created Issues</strong> (<code>box-info</code>): the idea's <code>ideaplanissue</code> records with their issue (shortcode-number and subject), linked to <code>app.project.issue.page</code>, grouped by plan version; empty state as in the screen.</li><li><strong>Summary</strong> (<code>box-info</code>): <code>h4</code> facts: created by and date, updated, message count, plan versions, total <code>costUsd</code> of the messages.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> switching versions shows the right plan; Approve appears only where described and updates the status; created issues link to their pages; integration tests per box and a Mirage acceptance test for approve; checked against the attached screen