63dae6286aa0-6333-ab77-04925463dc59	Scope Pace.js progress bar to route navigation only; exclude Socket.IO and Hermes heartbeat activity	2026-09-09 05:57:55	2026-09-16 13:10:16	0	<h3>Description</h3><p>The application uses Pace.js (javascript-behaviors) to provide visual loading feedback during route transitions defined in router.js. Hermes real-time messaging is initialized via Socket.IO, which maintains persistent connections, heartbeats, and reconnection cycles. Pace currently restarts on route reopen, which is desired, but it also reacts to Socket.IO connection and request activity, causing misleading progress indicators unrelated to page navigation. This story defines a clear separation: Pace serves route load/reload UX only; Socket.IO/Hermes traffic must not trigger or restart the progress bar. Delivering this improves perceived performance accuracy, reduces user confusion during background reconnects, and establishes a maintainable boundary between navigation loading state and real-time transport activity.</p>\n<h3>Functional Requirements</h3><ul><li>Pace.js MUST continue to start, progress, and complete during route navigation, initial load, and reload events initiated through router.js.</li><li>Pace.js MUST NOT start, restart, or remain active due to Socket.IO connection establishment, disconnection, reconnection, heartbeat/ping, or any Hermes-related socket request/response.</li><li>Socket.IO and Hermes initialization, heartbeat, and reconnection logic MUST operate independently without side effects on Pace state or UI.</li><li>Implementation MUST centralize trigger rules so future transport layers (e.g., additional WebSocket clients) can be excluded from Pace without duplicating logic.</li><li>Regression coverage MUST confirm route-based loading behavior is unchanged from the prior acceptable experience.</li></ul>\n<h3>Expected Behavior</h3><p>Users see the Pace.js progress bar only when navigating between routes or when the application performs a defined route load/reload via router.js. Background Socket.IO activity—including initial connect, heartbeat, reconnection attempts, and Hermes socket requests—runs silently with no Pace progress bar activation, restart, or flicker.</p>\n<h3>UI/UX Considerations</h3><p>Avoid progress bar flicker or false loading states during intermittent network conditions or Hermes reconnects; users should not interpret background socket activity as a page transition. Ensure route navigation still shows consistent, predictable loading feedback on desktop and mobile. Loading, empty, and error states for routed views must remain unaffected; only the trigger scope for Pace changes.</p>\n<h3>Acceptance Criteria</h3><ul><li>Given a user navigates via router.js (including reopen/reload of a route), Pace.js displays and completes as it did before this change.</li><li>Given Socket.IO connects successfully on app load, Pace.js does not start or restart.</li><li>Given Socket.IO disconnects and reconnects (including automatic retry), Pace.js does not start, restart, or remain visible.</li><li>Given Hermes heartbeat or socket request/response cycles occur while the user remains on the same route, Pace.js does not activate.</li><li>Given simultaneous route navigation and Socket.IO reconnection, only the route navigation triggers Pace; socket activity does not extend or restart the bar.</li><li>Manual or automated verification documents the exclusion mechanism and confirms no regression in router.js-driven loading behavior.</li></ul>\n<h3>Expected Outcome</h3><p>Route navigation retains reliable loading feedback via Pace.js while all Socket.IO/Hermes connection and heartbeat activity is invisible to the progress bar, eliminating false loading signals and clarifying ownership between navigation UX and real-time transport.</p>\n<h3>Recommendations</h3><ul><li>Audit Pace.js configuration and any global AJAX/fetch interceptors to explicitly ignore Socket.IO endpoints and transport URLs.</li><li>Introduce a single guard or allowlist (router-initiated navigation events only) rather than ad hoc checks scattered across behaviors.</li><li>Coordinate with Hermes owners to document which socket events are lifecycle vs. data so exclusions remain stable as Hermes evolves.</li><li>Add lightweight integration tests or a manual test matrix covering connect, disconnect, reconnect, and concurrent route change scenarios.</li></ul>	7ea8b586623c-5e99-bfcb-d51d01a7a16b	7ea8b586623c-5e99-bfcb-d51d01a7a16b	7ea8b586623c-5e99-bfcb-d51d01a7a16b	7ea8b586623c-5e99-bfcb-d51d01a7a16b	1	3940	2026-09-16	2026-09-09	done	medium	add12ff55acb-1902-6b9c-30d5411f7729	a5c362b86a91-2952-7d17-301ac1af4e15	\N	ae459c625acb-1902-6b9c-4dab4f2a0678	Rana Nouman	Hammad Hassan	\N	402f48b562c6-8e43-a8e0-0abdb1b2f5be	2026-09-16 13:10:16	aprom	0	0
