082f8b236aa1-e114-519a-3aa9ccf33429	Taskboard and Gantt: suppress stale-data reload prompt on originating page and fix false reload failure	2026-09-09 07:04:40	2026-09-16 13:27:50	0	<h3>Description</h3><p>Hermes-driven views (Taskboard, Gantt) notify users when external data changes via a non-destructive prompt: &quot;New data is available. Reload this view?&quot; This pattern preserves user control but must not surface when the user already performed the change on the active view, and reload actions must not report failure when navigation succeeds. Two defects undermine trust in live updates: redundant prompts on the originating page and a false error state during successful reload. Resolving both restores predictable UX, reduces support noise, and aligns live-update behavior across project views.</p>\n<h3>Functional Requirements</h3><ul><li>When a Hermes data-change event originates from user action on the currently active Taskboard or Gantt view, do not display the &quot;New data is available. Reload this view?&quot; prompt for that change.</li><li>Continue to display the reload prompt on Taskboard and Gantt when data changes originate from other sessions, users, routes, or background processes.</li><li>When the user confirms reload from the prompt after issue creation (from any route), complete reload without surfacing a user-facing failure message if the view successfully refreshes.</li><li>Handle route/navigation transitions during reload so aborted transitions (e.g., TransitionAborted) are not treated as reload failures when the page state is updated correctly.</li><li>Ensure consistent reload-prompt and reload-success behavior between Taskboard and Gantt.</li><li>Log suppressed prompts and transition-abort cases at appropriate levels for observability without exposing technical errors to end users.</li></ul>\n<h3>Current Behavior</h3><p>On Taskboard, when a user performs an action on the same page that triggers a Hermes update (e.g., creating or updating an issue), the &quot;New data is available. Reload this view?&quot; prompt still appears even though the change originated locally. On Taskboard and Gantt, after issue creation from any route, accepting the reload prompt often shows &quot;reload fails, please try again&quot; while the page actually reloads; developer tools report Live reload failed TransitionAborted: TransitionAborted.</p>\n<h3>Expected Behavior</h3><p>Users who trigger a data change on the active Taskboard or Gantt view see updated data without a redundant reload prompt. When a reload prompt is shown for externally sourced changes and the user chooses to reload, the view refreshes cleanly with no false failure message, and success or genuine failure is communicated accurately.</p>\n<h3>UI/UX Considerations</h3><p>Do not interrupt in-context workflows with unnecessary reload prompts. Reload confirmation should show a clear loading state and resolve to success without flicker or contradictory error toasts. Avoid exposing internal transition errors; use concise, actionable copy only when reload genuinely fails. Maintain responsive behavior on Taskboard and Gantt during reload; preserve scroll or selection where feasible after refresh.</p>\n<h3>Acceptance Criteria</h3><ul><li>Creating or updating an issue directly on Taskboard does not show &quot;New data is available. Reload this view?&quot; for that same change.</li><li>Creating an issue from another route while Taskboard or Gantt is open still shows the reload prompt when Hermes reports new data.</li><li>Clicking reload after issue creation on Taskboard completes without displaying &quot;reload fails, please try again&quot; when the view successfully updates.</li><li>Clicking reload after issue creation on Gantt completes without displaying &quot;reload fails, please try again&quot; when the view successfully updates.</li><li>No TransitionAborted or live-reload failure is surfaced to the user when navigation completes and data is current.</li><li>Genuine reload failures (network or server errors) still show an appropriate error message and allow retry.</li><li>Regression: externally driven updates (other users, API, background sync) continue to trigger the reload prompt on Taskboard and Gantt.</li></ul>\n<h3>Expected Outcome</h3><p>Taskboard and Gantt provide accurate, low-friction live-update behavior: no redundant prompts for self-initiated changes, and reliable reload confirmation without false errors—improving user confidence in real-time project data.</p>\n<h3>Recommendations</h3><ul><li>Introduce event metadata (sourceView, sourceSessionId, or correlationId) in Hermes payloads so views can ignore events they already applied optimistically or via local mutation handlers.</li><li>Centralize reload orchestration in a shared view-refresh utility that distinguishes TransitionAborted from true failures and debounces duplicate reload requests.</li><li>Add integration tests covering same-page create/update, cross-route create, and reload acceptance on Taskboard and Gantt.</li><li>Document the reload-prompt contract for all Hermes-connected views to prevent inconsistent behavior as new views adopt the pattern.</li></ul>	7ea8b586623c-5e99-bfcb-d51d01a7a16b	7ea8b586623c-5e99-bfcb-d51d01a7a16b	7ea8b586623c-5e99-bfcb-d51d01a7a16b	7ea8b586623c-5e99-bfcb-d51d01a7a16b	1	3941	2026-09-16	2026-09-09	done	medium	add12ff55acb-1902-6b9c-30d5411f7729	a5c362b86a91-2952-7d17-301ac1af4e15	\N	ae61a7235acb-1902-6b9c-e933ed4dfda0	Rana Nouman	Hammad Hassan	\N	402f48b562c6-8e43-a8e0-0abdb1b2f5be	2026-09-16 13:27:50	aprom	0	0
32754ae66aee-19e9-5a2a-3d57daa8519d	Talos dispatcher: issues waiting on dependencies survive a reconnect or restart	2026-11-07 08:11:30	2026-11-07 08:46:16	0	<p>agaia-4303 was held waiting on agaia-4302. At the next Hermes reconnect, reconcile re-checked it with <code>require_allowed_modifier</code>. The last modifier was the worker itself, because of its own "waiting on #4302" comment, so <code>policy.decide</code> (<code>talos/lib/policy.py</code> ~l.80) skipped it and it left the held set. When 4302 was done, nothing released 4303. Talos log 2026-11-07 11:55: <code>#4303 skipped: last changed by a user who can't hand this agent work (left the held set)</code>. The held set is also in memory only, so a Talos restart loses it the same way.</p><ul><li>Save the held set (issue, dependencies, last commented waiting set) in talos.db, so <code>DependencyGate</code> (<code>talos/lib/dependency_gate.py</code>) loads it at start. The dispatcher's <code>release_held("reconnect", None)</code> then re-checks all of them on reconcile.</li><li>Reconcile no longer drops an issue the worker itself held. For an issue in the saved held set, the last-modifier check is skipped: the hold started from an accepted assignment. The check still applies to issues Talos has never seen.</li><li>An issue leaves the saved set when it is released, worked, unassigned from the agent, or no longer actionable.</li><li>README: dependency gating survives restarts.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> unit tests: an issue held, then a reconcile where the last modifier is the agent itself, stays held and is released when its dependency turns done; the held set survives a new Dispatcher on the same db; an unknown issue last changed by a stranger is still skipped.</p><p><strong>Out of scope:</strong> the orchestrator's stranded-issue sweep (separate issue).</p><p><strong>Estimated files:</strong> 6</p><p>Complexity: simple</p>	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	1	b