045d74826ae1-affe-9a2f-69bce6d4ebe1	Talos dispatcher: thread-safe usage-limit pause in its own class, priority read when a job is taken	2026-10-28 07:28:04	2026-10-28 08:06:16	0	<p>Part of adt-4157. Follow-ups from adt-4160 (dev-tools#42) and adt-4162 #3 and #6 (dev-tools#44). The worker pool now runs several threads per agent. The usage-limit state (<code>_limit_streak</code>, <code>_paused_until</code> in <code>talos/lib/dispatcher.py</code>) is shared between those threads without a lock.</p><p><strong>Changes</strong></p><ul><li>Extract a <code>UsagePause</code> class (own module under <code>talos/lib/</code>) that owns the streak, the pause deadline and the backoff, behind a lock.</li><li>Make the pause wait interruptible (<code>threading.Event.wait</code>), so stop and shutdown don't hang until the reset time.</li><li>Put a limited issue back at the front of the queue, not the back.</li><li>Return a named constant instead of the string <code>'limited'</code> from <code>handle</code>, and have <code>policy.read_outcome</code> return it, so the dispatcher no longer checks limits separately.</li><li>Move the tracker call out of <code>enqueue</code>: read the issue's priority when a worker takes the job, so priority changes made while the issue waits are respected.</li></ul><p><strong>Acceptance</strong>: unit tests with several workers hitting a limit at once give one pause and one comment; stop interrupts a pause; a limited issue runs first after the reset; the queue order uses the priority at pop time. Behaviour with <code>maxParallel: 1</code> is unchanged.</p><p><strong>Out of scope</strong>: spend-based concurrency throttling.</p><p>Complexity: simple</p>	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	1	b7844c206ad7-8b87-6fc0-22987a8fb708	1	4169	2026-11-04	2026-10-28	done	medium	9f00a9366ad7-404c-ce18-9081c0a9fec6	b1a296116ae0-36a5-29af-371308f931b7	\N	a13debc86ad7-404c-ce18-75327b747d1b	Agent Nestor	Hammad Hassan	6687ec616ae1-7db8-a