6f587fc26ae1-c1d3-53f6-930f99a9cdca	Migration: repair dashboards of existing users	2026-10-28 12:49:51	2026-11-03 13:04:53	0	<p>Users created through the API have a default dashboard with <code>widgets = 'issuesToday, weeklyMilestones'</code>, and neither widget exists. Some users may have an empty widget list or no dashboard row. Their data needs to be repaired.</p>\n<h4>Scope</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Add a migration that sets <code>widgets</code> to the default list (<code>recentIssues, activeMilestones, weeklyActivities, weeklyIssueTimelogs, weeklyConversations</code>) on every non-deleted <code>dashboards</code> row whose value is <code>issuesToday, weeklyMilestones</code>, null or empty.</li>\n<li>In the same migration, insert a <code>Default Dashboard</code> row with the default widget list for every non-deleted user who has no non-deleted dashboard.</li>\n<li>Dashboards whose widgets were customised keep their widgets.</li>\n<li>Running the migration more than once does not create duplicate dashboards.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Tests</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Run it against a database that has all of these: users with the old widget list, users with an empty list, users with no dashboard, and users with a customised list. Confirm the result for each case.</li>\n</ul><p>Complexity: simple</p>	8a024ab56ad5-2c2e-412a-dd21fa8ef390	8a024ab56ad5-2c2e-412a-dd21fa8ef390	7ea8b586623c-5e99-bfcb-d51d01a7a16b	8a024ab56ad5-2c2e-412a-dd21fa8ef390	7ea8b586623c-5e99-bfcb-d51d01a7a16b	4177	2026-11-04	2026-10-28	deferred	high	cb4567905acd-06d6-1141-7dd99b59dd21	92ae09c66ace-1e5c-2b63-d1da9630092c	\N	cb9597e45acd-06d6-1141-34137d85f7a9	Agent Odysseus	Rana Nouman	57aaf5e26ae1-88d1-b56e-a0d991a1f682	d293f53862c6-8e43-047e-1fffdc448fcf	2026-10-28 13:45:58	agaia	0	0
f37335d16af3-1487-2734-ddb12b85d11c	Upgrade to Ember 4.12 LTS and ember-data 4.12, and clear the 5.0 deprecations	2026-11-10 21:34:43	2026-11-12 23:24:53	0	<p>A safe intermediate step: ember-source, ember-cli and ember-data 4.1 → 4.12 LTS.</p><ul><li>Add ember-cli-deprecation-workflow. Fix every deprecation that ends in 5.0, or record it as a known item.</li><li>ember-data 4.12 adds deprecation warnings for record-array and has-many array methods (<code>toArray</code>, <code>objectAt</code>, <code>filterBy</code>, <code>firstObject</code>, …). Use them to find and fix the record-array call sites that the prototype-extension issue leaves out.</li><li>The full suite must stay green.</li></ul><p>Complexity: complex</p>	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	1	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	1	4485	2026-11-18	2026-11-11	done	medium	add12ff55acb-1902-6b9c-30d5411f7729	70a8ecd06af3-1487-2734-22a7ca0b5d62	\N	ae5c14575acb-1902-6b9c-78da78712ee9	Agent Achilles	Hammad Hassan	6db3d5c96af5-ed29-7681-07e0e49fd84c	402f48b562c6-8e43-a8e0-0abdb1b2f5be	2026-11-12 23:24:54	aprom	0	0
005c04846af3-885a-2734-33b9b32f7250	Upgrade to Ember 6.12 LTS	2026-11-10 21:34:56	2026-11-13 11:26:12	0	<p>The final step, now that the other EmberUpgrade issues are done: ember-source and ember-cli ~6.12 on the classic build (not Vite, so ember-cli-yadda keeps working), with ember-data 5.x.</p><blockquote><p><strong>Note: use the trial findings below as a guideline, not as the implementation.</strong> They come from a throwaway trial whose goal was only to find out what breaks; the fixes there were the quickest way past each failure, not reviewed or production-ready changes. Use them to know where to look and what the cause was, then design each fix properly under the repo's rules (layering, SOLID, Octane conventions, the design system), and review it on its own merits. Don't copy or apply the trial patch.</p></blockquote><h4>Trial findings (13 Nov 2026, throwaway container, <code>dev</code> @ 9c68b5fe on Node 22)</h4><p>Result: with the changes below, the full suite was green (<strong>1461 pass, 0 fail</strong>, 3 todo: 407 acceptance, 485 integration, 568 unit). Progress across the runs: 118 → 98 → 47 → 5 → 0 failures.</p><ol><li><strong>Dependencies.</strong> The trial bumped ember-source ~6.12.1, ember-cli ~6.12.0, ember-data ~5.3, ember-power-select ^9, ember-concurrency ^5, ember-resolver ^13, ember-load-initializers ^3, @ember/optional-features ^3, @glimmer/component ^2, @ember/string ^4, @ember/jquery ^2, @ember/render-modifiers ^3, ember-inflector ^5 and ember-cli-sass ^11. <code>npm install</code> resolved without <code>--legacy-peer-deps</code>. Choose the exact versions yourself; the newest ember-data 5.x may be a better fit than 5.3.</li><li><strong>Undeclared dependencies.</strong> <code>ember-basic-dropdown</code> must be a direct dependency, or its components (e.g. <code>&lt;BasicDropdownWormhole&gt;</code>) aren't available to the app. <code>components/app-ui/milestone-block.js</code> imports <code>@embroider/util</code>, which was never declared (see 4).</li><li><strong>Classic component templates (silent failure).</strong> Ember 6 no longer resolves component templates from <code>app/templates/components/</code>; those components render as an empty element, with no error. This caused every modal failure (<code>bootstrap-modal</code> and everything built on it). Affected (they have a component class in <code>app/components/</code>): application-controlbar, attachment-icon, priority-icon, relate-user, interval-selector, relate-issuepriority, bootstrap-modal, quill-editor, relate-simple, tree-list, relate-issue. The trial moved each template next to its class. The remaining templates there are dead or looked up by name; aprom-4512 covers them.</li><li><strong><code>ensureSafeComponent</code> with a string.</strong> <code>app-ui/milestone-block</code> passes a component name string, and @embroider/util can't resolve names on Ember 6 (“couldn't locate the runtime resolver”). The trial imported the <code>milestone-blocks/*</code> components and chose one from a map by status. Any approach that hands the template a component class instead of a string works.</li><li><strong>ember-power-select 9 / ember-basic-dropdown 9:</strong><ul><li><code>&lt;PowerSelectMultiple&gt;</code> is gone; use <code>&lt;PowerSelect @multiple={{true}}&gt;</code> (<code>form-fields/field-multi-select.hbs</code>, <code>field-relate-multi.hbs</code>, and the classic <code>templates/components/form-fields/relatemulti.hbs</code>).</li><li>The test helpers <code>selectChoose</code>, <code>selectSearch</code>, <code>removeMultipleOption</code> (and <code>clearSelected</code>, <code>getDropdownItems</code>) moved to <code>ember-power-select/test-support</code>; <code>clickTrigger</code> and <code>typeInSearch</code> stay in <code>…/test-support/helpers</code>. About 19 step and test files import them.</li><li><strong>Dropdowns need a destination element.</strong> Without <code>&lt;BasicDropdownWormhole /&gt;</code> in the application template, no dropdown content is rendered, which would break every power-select in the real app, not just in tests. In integration tests (no application template) dropdowns fall back to <code>rootElement</code> from ember-basic-dropdown's config; the trial called <code>setConfig({ rootElement: '#ember-testing' })</code> from <code>ember-basic-dropdown/config</code> in <code>tests/test-helper.js</code>. Decide where the config belongs (for example <code>app.js</code> with an environment-based value).</li><li>In v9, <code>selectChoose</code> treats its value as a CSS selector first; an option text like <code>issue.*</code> is not a valid selector. That error went away once the destination was fixed, but keep it in mind for option texts with special characters.</li></ul></li><li><strong>A test that uses a private Glimmer API.</strong> <code>tests/unit/components/issue/issue-details-upload-test.js</code> (from aprom-4480) imports <code>ARGS_SET</code> from <code>@glimmer/component/-private/component</code>, which @glimmer/component 2 no longer exports. The trial set the file aside. <strong>It must be rewritten, not deleted:</strong> either render the component and capture the instance, or move the upload-queue logic into a plain class it can test directly. Then check that it still asserts at most 3 concurrent uploads, that every file finishes, and that an oversized file is rejected.</li><li><strong>What still works:</strong> ember-cli-yadda 0.7 with yadda pinned at 2.2.0 (all Gherkin features ran), ember-cli-mirage 3 with the current <code>mirage/config.js</code>, ember-file-upload 10, ember-concurrency 5 with the async-arrow tasks, and the test-helper shims from the 4.12 work.</li></ol><h4>Watch out</h4><ul><li>The root <code>Dockerfile</code> installs <code>ember-cli@4.12.3</code> globally (<code>npm install -g ember-cli@4.12.3</code>). Update it to match the project's ember-cli 6.12, or drop the global install and use the project's own <code>npx ember</code>. Check the other images for similar pins.</li><li><code>package-lock.json</code> is in <code>.gitignore</code> and never committed. CI (<code>ci.yml</code>, <code>cache-dependencies.yml</code>) and the Dockerfiles run a fresh <code>npm install</code>, and the CI <code>node_modules</code> cache is keyed on <code>package.json</code>, so the version bumps trigger a clean install. Only <code>package.json</code> needs to change; write the version bumps by hand, because the trial's <code>package.json</code> was rewritten with different line endings.</li><li>Check the dropdowns, modals and milestone blocks in the running app, not only in tests. The template and dropdown-destination problems both fail silently in the browser.</li></ul><h4>Out of scope (aprom-4512)</h4><p>Leftover templates in <code>app/templates/components/</code>, the <code>field/*</code> template lookups, and the Ember 7 deprecations (<code>using-amd-bundles</code> / <code>use-ember-modules</code>, <code>importing-inject-from-ember-service</code>, the ember-data Store extending <code>EmberObject</code>).</p><h4>Done when</h4><ul><li>The full suite is green, including the rewritten upload-queue test.</li><li>The app runs locally on Ember 6.12, with dropdowns, modals and milestone blocks checked in the browser.</li></ul><p>Timing: 6.12 is the last 6.x release and the route to Ember 7. Its bugfix support ends around 8 Dec 2026; security fixes run until about May 2027.</p><p>Complexity: complex</p>	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	1	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	1	4486	2026-11-18	2026-11-11	done	high	add12ff55acb-1902-6b9c-30d5411f7729	70a8ecd06af3-1487-2734-22a7ca0b5d62	\N	ae5c14575acb-1902-6b9c-78da78712ee9	Agent Achilles	Hammad Hassan	e1f01a526af6-fb03-ff1f-f1228bfeace5	402f48b562c6-8e43-a8e0-0abdb1b2f5be	2026-11-13 11:26:12	aprom	0	0
0d05ef766af3-4c62-9e71-90efd6320697	Address-review runs read Gaia feedback and CI, and Talos does the hand-back	2026-11-10 21:47:57	2026-11-10 22:05:37	0	<p>On aprom-4470, the reviewer approved PR prometheus#740 and wrote "CI is failing" as a Gaia comment. Two address-review runs (Talos log odysseus-4470-20261111-023525.json and odysseus-4470-20261111-023630.json) found "no open threads" and handed the issue back without fixing CI. In the second run, the hand-back command failed: <code>ai comment</code> could not read Git Bash's <code>/tmp/c.html</code>. The agent still reported <code>done</code>, which Talos flagged as <code>not_handed_back</code>.</p>\n<h4>Causes</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><code>ai review &lt;repo&gt; &lt;pr&gt; --open</code> and the <code>in_review</code> prompt in <code>talos/agents/odysseus/agent.json</code> only look at GitHub review threads. They miss feedback given as Gaia comments and a red CI.</li>\n<li>On address-review runs, the hand-back to <code>in_review</code> depends on the agent typing <code>ai status ... in_review --assignee reviewer</code> itself. That is why reruns sometimes stay <code>in_progress</code>. The first run's hand-off goes through code (<code>ai ship</code> / the Talos <code>ai handoff</code>).</li>\n<li><code>_check_handed_back</code> (talos/lib/dispatcher.py) checks only the assignee. A run that reassigns the issue but leaves it <code>in_progress</code> passes the check.</li>\n<li>dispatcher.py <code>_run</code>: <code>if issue.get("status") == "in_review"</code> never matches, because <code>_mark_picked_up</code> has already rewritten <code>issue["status"]</code> to <code>in_progress</code>. As a result, <code>_back_to_merge_loop</code> never runs.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Changes</h4>\n<ol>\n<li><code>ai review &lt;repo&gt; &lt;pr&gt; --open --issue N</code> prints the open threads and two more things:\n  <ul>\n  <li>the CI state of the PR head, with the names of failing checks (reuse the CI check in <code>ai/lib/mergegate.py</code>);</li>\n  <li>human comments on the Gaia issue posted after the agent's last comment or hand-off.</li>\n  </ul>\n  It ends with an explicit line saying whether anything needs addressing. "Nothing to address" only when threads, comments and CI are all clear.</li>\n<li>The <code>in_review</code> prompt in agent.json:\n  <ul>\n  <li>passes <code>--issue {issue}</code>;</li>\n  <li>says that Gaia comments and failing CI count as review feedback;</li>\n  <li>tells the agent not to set the status or assignee, and to end with <code>done</code>.</li>\n  </ul></li>\n<li>Talos hands back <code>in_review</code>-action runs itself. After <code>done</code>, if the issue is still with the agent, it runs <code>ai handoff</code>, the same way shipping defers it. When the issue is in an auto-merge milestone, it goes through <code>_back_to_merge_loop</code>.</li>\n<li><code>_check_handed_back</code> also checks that the status is not the pickup status (<code>in_progress</code>).</li>\n<li>Keep the issue's status from before pickup, so the post-review routing (item 3) sees <code>in_review</code>.</li>\n<li><code>ai comment</code> also accepts the comment text inline (<code>--text</code>), so agents don't write temp files. Git Bash's <code>/tmp</code> path doesn't exist for the Windows Python behind <code>ai</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Tests in <code>talos/tests</code> and the ai tests for each change. Update the Talos README / role.md where they describe address-review runs.</p>\n<p>Companion change: the address-review skill lives in the Pr4M workspace repo (<code>.claude/skills/address-review/SKILL.md</code>). That PR is opened separately and should merge after this one.</p><p>Complexity: simple</p>	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	1	549d42946ad7-581a-8cbb-bbef380694da	1	4487	2026-11-18	2026-11-11	done	medium	9f00a9366ad7-404c-ce18-9081c0a9fec6	84aeed166ad8-5eae-b551-f3f3cea8503c	\N	a0df54476ad7-404c-ce18-8b3ef9fc16e9	Agent Achilles	Hammad Hassan	