f3d095766af0-5896-16ca-65dd4159fd63	f3c480836af0-5896-16ca-46db513fac3d	d78532e16aeb-924f-f101-2c4a67101962	d883f1756aeb-924f-f101-622ecbc66dce	run_agent	72e3d4a86ae8-11b0-bd37-a3bd55a4171b	73094b556ae8-11b0-bd37-4da81643c41f	add12ff55acb-1902-6b9c-30d5411f7729	milestone	3b34c2b96af0-b3ed-60c0-369a444a326a	Project ID: add12ff55acb-1902-6b9c-30d5411f7729 \nMilestone ID: 3b34c2b96af0-b3ed-60c0-369a444a326a Milestone Name: aga-Quick List	new	\N	80	finished	blocked	<p><strong>Blocked: the retro issue was not created.</strong></p><p>Creating an issue fails with <code>Gaia GET issuestatus returned 403: Access Denied</code> for Hera (agent.hera). I tried it twice, with and without an explicit status. <code>gaia_project_options</code> fails the same way, and <code>gaia_whoami</code> cannot read memberships (403). Reading milestones and issues works.</p><p><strong>To unblock:</strong> give Hera read access to issue statuses in project A Prometheus, or confirm the agent has the right project membership and role. Then re-run Hera on milestone aga-Quick List.</p><p><strong>Gathered:</strong> all 20 issues (#4350-#4368, #4373), each with its comments and time logs. They are all done, none were carried over, and 6h12m was logged. No issue has a time estimate (all 0m); only "Estimated files" exists. No duplicate retro issue exists.</p><p><strong>Top findings:</strong></p><ol><li>Four PRs hit merge conflicts, three in translations/en-us.json (#4350, #4352, #4353; the fourth was #4366). CodeRabbit's daily limit was reached, so 19 of 20 issues were reviewed by hand.</li><li>Design decisions surfaced at hand-off rather than at spec time. #4356 flagged the missing paging total, and #4359 shipped without pagination showing until #4360 fixed it. Nine of the 20 issues (#4360-#4368) were follow-ups.</li><li>Shipping (full suite and design review) used most of the logged time on small changes, for example 35 of 39 minutes on #4367.</li></ol><p><strong>Proposed actions:</strong> serialise or split the shared translations file; print the first build-error lines in <code>ai test</code>; require flagged follow-ups to be created or waived at ship time; clear Messenger toasts between acceptance scenarios; record time estimates on issues.</p><p><strong>Data gaps:</strong> no done-dates or status history, so lateness and time in feedback can't be measured. I did not read PR review history with <code>gh</code>, so review rounds come from issue comments only.</p>	2026-11-08 17:42:15	2026-11-08 21:27:15	2026-11-09 11:32:03	2026-11-09 11:35:00	2026-11-08 17:27:15	2026-11-09 11:35:00	f
22bb1b486af1-a449-3228-2561d11276b2	22ae5ae16af1-a449-3228-0ebe34227084	d78532e16aeb-924f-f101-2c4a67101962	d883f1756aeb-924f-f101-622ecbc66dce	run_agent	72e3d4a86ae8-11b0-bd37-a3bd55a4171b	73094b556ae8-11b0-bd37-4da81643c41f	add12ff55acb-1902-6b9c-30d5411f7729	milestone	9cacd6286ae5-b7f5-613b-853cf931f5e8	Project ID: add12ff55acb-1902-6b9c-30d5411f7729 \nMilestone ID: 9cacd6286ae5-b7f5-613b-853cf931f5e8 Milestone Name: aga-v1	new	\N	80	finished	blocked	<p><strong>Blocked: cannot create the retro issue.</strong></p><p>Every <code>gaia_create_issue</code> call in A Prometheus fails with <em>Gaia GET issuestatus returned 403: Access Denied</em>. This happened with an explicit status and type, and again with only the required fields. <code>gaia_project_options</code> fails with the same 403, and <code>gaia_whoami</code> could not read Hera's memberships (also 403). So Hera's Gaia account seems to lack read access to issuestatus (and membership) in A Prometheus. No search found an existing "Retro - aga-v1" issue, and nothing was written to the tracker.</p><p><strong>What would unblock it:</strong> grant agent.hera read access to issuestatus (and membership) in A Prometheus, or create an empty "Retro - aga-v1" issue assigned to Hammad Hassan with no milestone, and I will fill it in. Then re-dispatch Hera.</p><p><strong>Gathered (all 79 issues read):</strong></p><ul><li>Delivered 78 of 79 (99%); #4341 is wont_fix because #4339 had already fixed the overlap.</li><li>1,527 min (25h 27m) logged, all agent active minutes. No issue has a time estimate (0m everywhere), so estimate accuracy and overruns cannot be computed.</li><li>Nothing was late: every issue was done before 2026-11-30.</li></ul><p><strong>Three most important findings:</strong></p><ol><li>Test infrastructure caused the most lost cycles: Docker down (#4207, #4208, #4222, #4242, #4244, #4287, #4340, #4343), flaky tests (edit project: #4205-#4208; file-preview: #4273, #4282, #4283, #4287), and a shared test log giving contradictory results (#4207).</li><li>Missing "Depends on" links on the first batch (#4036, #4045, #4056, #4057, #4058, #4059) meant each was assigned, blocked and reset before shipping on 2026-11-02. The ezSearch chain, by contrast, was serialised cleanly by file-overlap holds.</li><li>From #4323 on, PRs were not sent to CodeRabbit (credit used up), and browser checks on 21 UI issues plus many design-review follow-ups were left to the human reviewer. #4338's Task Board search design also led to five follow-up issues (#4339, #4340, #4341, #4344, #4347).</li></ol><p>The full retro text (summary, numbers, went well and didn't, patterns, surprises, five proposed actions, data gaps and the 79-row ledger) is drafted. I did not create a partial issue.</p>	2026-11-09 12:20:54	2026-11-09 16:05:54	2026-11-09 12:25:17	2026-11-09 12:32:04	2026-11-09 12:05:54	2026-11-09 12:32:04	f
